    <!--BlockML Constraint — value constraint branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.constraint.ValueConstraint-->
    <block>
      <name>
        ValueConstraint
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:constraint.ValueConstraint
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Constraint
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A rule for a concrete value
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract branch for value-level constraints. Generic parameter T binds automatically
            to the effective ValueType of the enclosing Property at validate time — authors never specify T
            in BML. Valid only under a Property member's &lt;constraints> container
            (BML_CONSTRAINT_TARGET_MISMATCH otherwise). Examples: Min, Max, Pattern, ValuePresent.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <genericTypes>
        <T baseType="blockml:core.ValueType" />
      </genericTypes>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — value present constraint

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.constraint.ValuePresent-->
    <block>
      <name>
        ValuePresent
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:constraint.ValuePresent
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:constraint.ValueConstraint
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A rule that a value must be set
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Scalar constraint: T binds to the property type. The property value must be present
            after all model resolution is complete — inheritance, contracts, default values, and instance
            value binding. Only authored values count; expression evaluation does not satisfy ValuePresent
            (Stage 2 does not evaluate expressions). Enforced by validateConstraints() at Stage 2 only when
            validation references an understood Validation block (typically validation:Error). Declares no
            own properties beyond the inherited validation reference.
      </documentation>
      <genericTypes>
        <T baseType="blockml:core.ValueType" />
      </genericTypes>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — alias branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Alias-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Alias
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Alias
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Entity
      </baseType>
      <is>
        An alternative Block type name
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract branch marker for alias Entity instances in an aliases aggregation on
            org.blockml.bml.definition.BlockDefinition only (root blocks and Sub-Blocks). Domain libraries
            define concrete alias blocks extending this branch for catalogue-controlled alternative names.
            The core library provides org.blockml.bml.meta.FreeTextAlias for ad-hoc text names and
            org.blockml.bml.meta.FixedAlias as the protected base for catalogue aliases whose members are
            set on derived alias block definitions; comma-separated shorthand on &lt;aliases> expands to
            FreeTextAlias instances only. Not XML
            namespace prefix aliases. Not search tags — use org.blockml.bml.core.Keyword branch for those.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:core.Entity" relation="SeeAlso">
          Alias instances are Entity children inside aliases aggregations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Keyword" relation="ContrastsWith">
          Parallel branch for search and discovery tags — not alternative type names.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — Annotation branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Annotation

  Formalizes annotation namespaces (xmlns targets that are not packages).
  Orthogonal to the Definition meta-model — Annotations do not define block structure.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Annotation
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Annotation
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A declared annotation namespace with typed directives
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract branch for annotation namespaces. Every BML-semantic xmlns prefix must resolve to
        either a Package (FQN prefix under which types live) or a concrete Annotation. Annotations are
        orthogonal to the Definition meta-model (org.blockml.bml.definition.*): they do not describe
        block structure, members, or constraints. They declare which directive QNames are legal under
        a preferred authoring alias (e.g. bml, scad).
        
        preferredAlias is authoring convention only — the BOM stores the Annotation FQN, never the
        XML prefix. directives lists the concrete Directive kinds this annotation exposes.
        
        Directive taxonomy (SubBlocks — not Definition member kinds):
        • Directive — base; property name is the directive local name
        • Member (name="*") — wildcard member directives under this namespace
          → Property · Aggregation · Association · Capability
        • Element — element-form directives
          → Document (name="document") · Summary (name="summary") · Examples (name="examples")
            · Example (name="example") — sibling under StructuralDefinition examples container
            · Value (name="value") — PropertyDefinition value-field facets
        • Attribute — attribute-form directives (id, with, each, …)
        
        Naming overlap with Definition (Property, Aggregation, …) is classification of directive form
        only — Annotation.Property is not def:PropertyDefinition. Annotation.Value facets nested under
        a property declaration (e.g. &lt;myProp>&lt;scad:value>…&lt;/scad:value>&lt;/myProp>)
        are not the same as a member QName &lt;scad:value/> under &lt;properties> — parent
        context disambiguates.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:core.Definition" relation="ContrastsWith">
          Definition meta-model describes block structure — Annotations do not.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.AnnotationModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Normative Annotation authoring — facets, match rules, Package vs Annotation.
        </see>
      </references>
      <properties>
        <preferredAlias abstract="true" type="blockml:type.Text">
          <documentation>
            Preferred xmlns prefix for this annotation (e.g. bml, scad). Authoring
                    convention only — never stored in the BOM. Concrete annotations must supply a value.
          </documentation>
        </preferredAlias>
      </properties>
      <aggregations>
        <directives type="blockml:core.Annotation.Directive[]">
          <documentation>
            Concrete directives exposed by this annotation. Attribute and Element
                    directives match by fixed name; Member directives match any local name (name="*").
          </documentation>
        </directives>
      </aggregations>
      <subBlocks>
        <block>
          <name>
            Directive
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Directive
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Block
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A typed annotation directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Abstract base for all annotation directives. The name property is the
                    directive local name under the annotation namespace (e.g. id, with, document, examples,
                    example, value). Member directives fix name to "*"; Document, Summary, Examples, Example,
                    and Value fix document / summary / examples / example / value; Attribute and Element
                    concretes set an explicit local name.
          </documentation>
          <abstract>
            true
          </abstract>
          <properties>
            <name abstract="true" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Directive local name in the annotation namespace. "*" on Member means any
                            local name. Fixed on Document ("document"), Summary ("summary"), Examples ("examples"),
                            Example ("example"), and Value ("value").
              </documentation>
            </name>
          </properties>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Member
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Member
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Directive
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A wildcard member annotation directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Member-shaped directive under an annotation namespace. name is fixed to "*"
                    — the concrete local name comes from the authoring QName (e.g. scad:value). Specialized by
                    Property, Aggregation, Association, and Capability. Not a Definition member type.
          </documentation>
          <abstract>
            true
          </abstract>
          <properties>
            <name final="true" override="true" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Wildcard — any local name under this annotation is a candidate member
                            directive of this kind.
              </documentation>
              <value>*</value>
            </name>
          </properties>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Property
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Property
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Member
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A property-shaped member annotation directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Classifies annotation QNames as property-shaped member directives (name="*").
                    Orthogonal to org.blockml.bml.definition.PropertyDefinition.
          </documentation>
          <abstract>
            true
          </abstract>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Aggregation
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Aggregation
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Member
          </baseType>
          <is>
            An aggregation-shaped member annotation directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Classifies annotation QNames as aggregation-shaped member directives
                    (name="*"). Orthogonal to org.blockml.bml.definition.AggregationDefinition.
          </documentation>
          <abstract>
            true
          </abstract>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Association
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Association
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Member
          </baseType>
          <is>
            An association-shaped member annotation directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Classifies annotation QNames as association-shaped member directives
                    (name="*"). Orthogonal to org.blockml.bml.definition.AssociationDefinition.
          </documentation>
          <abstract>
            true
          </abstract>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Capability
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Capability
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Member
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A capability-shaped member annotation directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Classifies annotation QNames as capability-shaped member directives
                    (name="*"). Orthogonal to org.blockml.bml.core.Capability and
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.CapabilityDefinition.
          </documentation>
          <abstract>
            true
          </abstract>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Element
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Element
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Directive
          </baseType>
          <is>
            An element-form annotation directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Directive that appears as an XML element under the annotation namespace
                    (e.g. bml:container, scad:document, scad:examples, sibling scad:example, nested scad:value).
                    Concrete directives set name; Document, Summary, Examples, Example, and Value are standard
                    element kinds with fixed names.
          </documentation>
          <abstract>
            true
          </abstract>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Attribute
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Attribute
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Directive
          </baseType>
          <is>
            An attribute-form annotation directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Directive that appears as an XML attribute under the annotation namespace
                    (e.g. bml:id, bml:each, bml:with). Concrete directives set name to the attribute local
                    name.
          </documentation>
          <abstract>
            true
          </abstract>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Document
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Document
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Element
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A document prose facet directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Standard element directive for namespaced document prose facets. name is
                    fixed to "document" (e.g. scad:document). Distinct from the default-namespace
                    Definition property documentation — Annotation facets never satisfy required canonical
                    documentation slots.
          </documentation>
          <properties>
            <name final="true" override="true" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Fixed local name for document prose facets under an annotation
                            namespace.
              </documentation>
              <value>document</value>
            </name>
          </properties>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Summary
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Summary
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Element
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A summary prose facet directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Standard element directive for namespaced summary prose facets. name is
                    fixed to "summary" (e.g. scad:summary). Distinct from the default-namespace Definition
                    property summary.
          </documentation>
          <properties>
            <name final="true" override="true" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Fixed local name for summary prose facets under an annotation
                            namespace.
              </documentation>
              <value>summary</value>
            </name>
          </properties>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Examples
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Examples
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Element
          </baseType>
          <is>
            An examples container facet directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Standard element directive for namespaced examples containers. name is fixed
                    to "examples" (e.g. scad:examples). Parallel to Document and Summary facets: the default-
                    namespace &lt;examples> aggregation on StructuralDefinition remains the canonical slot;
                    Annotation-prefixed containers (scad:examples, …) hold independent facet-specific sample
                    sets and never replace the canonical examples aggregation. Prefer Annotation.Example
                    (scad:example sibling under the same &lt;examples>) when a target-language sample pairs
                    1:1 with a canonical &lt;example>.
          </documentation>
          <properties>
            <name final="true" override="true" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Fixed local name for examples container facets under an annotation
                            namespace.
              </documentation>
              <value>examples</value>
            </name>
          </properties>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Example
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Example
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Element
          </baseType>
          <is>
            An example entry facet directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Standard element directive for a target-language sample as a sibling of
                    StructuralDefinition.Example under the same &lt;examples> container. name is fixed to
                    "example" (e.g. scad:example next to &lt;example>). Parent context: direct child of
                    &lt;examples> with an Annotation prefix → this Example facet entry; unprefixed
                    &lt;example> → StructuralDefinition.Example. Same shape as a canonical example (required
                    title, optional language / snippet, CDATA body). Prefer this over a parallel scad:examples
                    container when BML and OpenSCAD bodies are one logical pair (match by title). Does not
                    replace canonical &lt;example> entries.
          </documentation>
          <properties>
            <name final="true" override="true" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Fixed local name for example entry facets under an annotation
                            namespace (sibling of canonical &lt;example> inside &lt;examples>).
              </documentation>
              <value>example</value>
            </name>
          </properties>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Value
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Value
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Annotation.Element
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A property value-field facet directive
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Standard element directive for namespaced PropertyDefinition value facets.
                    name is fixed to "value" (e.g. &lt;myProp>&lt;scad:value>1.6&lt;/scad:value>&lt;/myProp>).
                    Multiplies the value field of an existing property along the Annotation namespace axis —
                    same property identity (myProp), not a member QName scad:myProp and not a top-level
                    &lt;properties>&lt;scad:value/>&lt;/properties> member (that is Annotation.Property /
                    Member). Parent context disambiguates: nested under a property element → Value facet;
                    direct child of properties → Member when the Annotation declares Property (name="*").
                    Cross-namespace faceting is valid (for example &lt;something:myProp>&lt;scad:value>…);
                    same-namespace self-faceting is forbidden (for example
                    &lt;scad:value>&lt;scad:value>…). Canonical &lt;value> remains the default-namespace
                    slot; facet values never replace it for BML semantics — renderers may prefer a matching
                    Annotation facet.
          </documentation>
          <properties>
            <name final="true" override="true" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Fixed local name for PropertyDefinition value-field facets under an
                            annotation namespace.
              </documentation>
              <value>value</value>
            </name>
          </properties>
        </block>
      </subBlocks>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — root Block type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Block-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Block
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Block
      </type>
      <is>
        A semantic building block
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Root semantic unit of BlockML — the sole inheritance root for all Block definitions.
        Every concept in the grammar is a Block: composable domain types, ValueTypes, Languages,
        Status values, Constraints, Capabilities, Services, Libraries, and Definition meta-model
        types. Unqualified name Block resolves to this type.
        
        Role and constraints:
        • Sole inheritance root — omits &lt;baseType>; all other definitions must declare a non-empty parent FQN
        • Abstract — not used directly as a composition instance type
        • Further members defined by sub-types — Properties, Aggregations,
          Associations, and Capabilities via the baseType chain
        • Composition instance identity uses bml:id and bml:version (not member attributes)
        • Documentation prose language is Definition meta (def:BlockDefinition.language), not a
          member of this type — this document authors &lt;language>&lt;English/>&lt;/language> as the
          framework default for inheritance
        
        Type reference rules:
        • Aggregation member types may be any Block FQN
        • Association member types must be InstanceReference(T) or InstanceReference(T)[]
        • Property and Capability payload types must resolve to a ValueType
        • Capability implementation language attributes reference org.blockml.bml.language.* — unqualified names permitted
        
        Documentation model (on every root block definition — see def:BlockDefinition /
        doc:DocumentationModel):
        • Definition identity first — &lt;name>, &lt;type>, &lt;baseType> (baseType omitted only here)
        • &lt;is> — always required after identity; clear Urbild designation (what the Block semantically represents)
        • &lt;summary> — required when documentation is too long for a quick scan
        • &lt;language> — optional Definition meta; natural language for documentation prose
          (inherits from enclosing Library document, then this Block document's English default)
        • &lt;documentation> — always required; technical description; optional language
          attribute when prose deviates from effective block language
        • &lt;isNot> — required when confusion risk exists
        • Optional extensions: &lt;since>, &lt;changelog>, &lt;examples>, &lt;references>
        Omitted is or documentation → BML_MISSING_IS / BML_MISSING_DOCUMENTATION; missing isNot or
        summary when required → BML_MISSING_IS_NOT / BML_MISSING_SUMMARY (warning V1).
        
        Serialization:
        • bml:xml ${ bml:Self } — composable instances render under their declared type tag
          (for example &lt;core:Block>, &lt;myns:Wall>)
        • Definition branch types describe block structure and carry their own bml:xml rules
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:core.ValueType" relation="Extends">
          Property and capability payload types.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Language" relation="Extends">
          Implementation language bindings and NaturalLanguage branch.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Status" relation="Extends">
          Lifecycle status references.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Relation" relation="Extends">
          Cross-reference relationship categories on see entries.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Constraint" relation="Extends">
          Property validation constraints.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Capability" relation="Extends">
          Capability implementation contracts.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Definition" relation="Extends">
          Meta-model for block structure (WIP).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" aggregation="language" relation="Defines">
          Definition meta — documentation prose language on every Block document.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Shared documentation model for structural definition elements.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.DocumentationModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Block documentation model — is, documentation, extensions.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.DefinitionModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Root type exception and Definition branch.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.EverythingIsABlock" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Everything is a Block — type system overview.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BmlDirectives" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Definition branch bml:xml serialization rules.
        </see>
      </references>
      <language>
        <blockml:core.Block.English />
      </language>
      <bml:xml>${ bml:Self }</bml:xml>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — abstract library descriptor base type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.BlockLibrary

  Framework base type — lives in core, not exported via any Library blocks catalog.
  Concrete Library blocks (Library.bml) inherit from BlockLibrary.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        BlockLibrary
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.BlockLibrary
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A publishable Block catalog type
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract publishable library unit — groups exported Block and ValueType definitions,
        optional XSD schemas, dependencies on other libraries, and nested sub-libraries.
        Replaces the legacy library.xml descriptor.
        
        Framework base type in org.blockml.bml.core — not exported via Library blocks.
        Concrete Library.bml files inherit from BlockLibrary and declare aggregations explicitly.
        
        Catalog aggregations are public and readOnly: foreign Blocks and tools may inspect a library
        descriptor (e.g. list exports or dependencies) in expressions; only the Library definition
        (and child overrides) may author the catalog. Instances must not rewrite these members.
        
        Documentation prose language is Definition meta (def:BlockDefinition.language) on the Library
        document — not a catalog aggregation. Exported blocks inherit that document language unless
        they declare their own &lt;language>.
        
        Aggregations (each aggregation member is opened by name; none is default):
        • readMe — required human/LLM reading entry (ReadMe block, 1..1)
        • bootstrap — optional automation SPOE (Bootstrap block, 0..1)
        • dependencies — other Library blocks this library depends on (typical: inner library
          depends on outer)
        • subLibraries — nested child Library blocks grouped under this library
        • schemas — optional legacy XSD paths as FilePathEntity instances
        • blocks — exported Block and ValueType definitions (membership SSOT)
        
        readMe and bootstrap hold TypeReferenceEntity children — empty QName sugar sets value to
        that FQN. dependencies, subLibraries, and blocks use the same pattern. Optional bml:version on
        sugar (or explicit TypeReferenceEntity) children is an exact-version assert against the
        referenced type (value) — same semantics as external composition references; omitted → resolve
        from TypeRegistry / library context; no match → BML_UNKNOWN_VERSION. schemas holds
        FilePathEntity composition children; value is the relative .xsd path from the library package
        root (directory containing Library.bml).
        
        Concrete Library.bml &lt;references> rule — at most two &lt;see> entries:
        1. Required: relation=ReadFirst targeting the same ReadMe named in readMe.
        2. Optional: relation=ThenContinueWith targeting the Bootstrap named in bootstrap, only when
           bootstrap is present. Signals: after reading, continue by executing the automation SPOE.
        No AuthoringGuide / DeepDive / Extends / SeeAlso (or other) entries on concrete Libraries —
        those belong on documentation blocks, not on the catalog descriptor.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.LibrarySystem" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Library format — structure and blocks membership.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.LibrarySystem" relation="SeeAlso">
          Libraries, packages, namespaces, versioning, and imports.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.FilePathEntity" relation="SeeAlso">
          Entity wrapper used for schemas entries.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.ReadMe" relation="Requires">
          Required readMe target — human/LLM reading entry.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Bootstrap" relation="SeeAlso">
          Optional bootstrap target — automation SPOE (0..1).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.ThenContinueWith" relation="SeeAlso">
          Optional Library &lt;see> after ReadFirst when bootstrap is present.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" aggregation="language" relation="SeeAlso">
          Definition meta — Library document language; default for exported blocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity" relation="Requires">
          Entity payload for readMe, bootstrap, dependencies, subLibraries, and blocks.
        </see>
      </references>
      <properties>
        <description readOnly="true" type="blockml:type.Text">
          <documentation>
            Human-readable summary of what this library provides. Public readOnly —
                    inspectable from foreign Blocks; authored on the Library definition only.
          </documentation>
        </description>
      </properties>
      <aggregations>
        <readMe readOnly="true" type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity(blockml:core.ReadMe)[1]">
          <documentation>
            Required human/LLM reading entry for this library. Exactly one ReadMe
                    TypeReferenceEntity — empty QName sugar or explicit value. Typically
                    &lt;blockml:README /> on the core library. Missing or duplicate →
                    BML_TYPE_REFERENCE_MULTIPLICITY. Optional bml:version is an exact-version assert against
                    the referenced ReadMe type. Public readOnly — inspectable from foreign Blocks; authored on
                    the Library definition only.
          </documentation>
        </readMe>
        <bootstrap readOnly="true" type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity(blockml:core.Bootstrap)">
          <documentation>
            Optional single point of entry (SPOE) for automated startup — tasks, agents,
                    CI pipelines. Bare TypeReferenceEntity = 0..1. At most one Bootstrap; omit when the library
                    has no automation entry. Not the human reading entry (use readMe). Optional bml:version
                    is an exact-version assert against the referenced Bootstrap type. Public readOnly —
                    inspectable from foreign Blocks; authored on the Library definition only.
          </documentation>
        </bootstrap>
        <dependencies readOnly="true" type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity(blockml:core.BlockLibrary)[]">
          <documentation>
            Other Library blocks this library depends on. Typical pattern: an inner
                  library lists the outer library here. TypeReferenceEntity instances (QName sugar or explicit
                  value). Optional bml:version pins or asserts an exact Library version (e.g.
                  &lt;blockml:Library bml:version="3.0.0" />). Public readOnly — foreign tools may read
                  the dependency list; only the Library definition may author it.
          </documentation>
        </dependencies>
        <subLibraries readOnly="true" type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity(blockml:core.BlockLibrary)[]">
          <documentation>
            Nested child Library blocks grouped under this library. A library may declare
                  sub-libraries alongside blocks. TypeReferenceEntity instances. Optional bml:version is an
                  exact-version assert against the referenced Library. The outer library must not re-export
                  inner library blocks in blocks — consumers depend on the inner library directly. Public
                  readOnly — inspectable from foreign Blocks; authored on the Library definition only.
          </documentation>
        </subLibraries>
        <schemas readOnly="true" type="blockml:type.FilePathEntity[]">
          <documentation>
            Optional legacy XSD schema paths during migration. Each entry is a
                    FilePathEntity composition child; value is the relative .xsd path from the library package
                    root. Omit when all types are exported via blocks. Public readOnly — inspectable from
                    foreign Blocks; authored on the Library definition only.
          </documentation>
        </schemas>
        <blocks readOnly="true" type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity(blockml:core.Block)[]">
          <documentation>
            Exported Block and ValueType definitions. Membership is defined exclusively by
                  this list — not by directory layout. TypeReferenceEntity instances naming each export.
                  Optional bml:version is an exact-version assert against the exported type when that type
                  declares its own version. Public readOnly — the publishable catalog is inspectable from
                  foreign Blocks (e.g. library-management tools); only the Library definition may author
                  membership.
          </documentation>
        </blocks>
      </aggregations>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — library/process automation SPOE role

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Bootstrap-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Bootstrap
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Bootstrap
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        An automated library start entry
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract empty branch for Bootstrap blocks — the optional single point of entry
            (SPOE) for automated startup: tasks, agents, CI pipelines, and similar process logic. Intentionally
            empty — no properties or aggregations. Concrete subtypes add scenario-specific members when
            needed.
        
            Distinct from org.blockml.bml.core.ReadMe, which is the required human/LLM reading entry on a
            Library (readMe aggregation). A Library may omit bootstrap (0..1) when it has no automated
            startup SPOE; it must never declare more than one Bootstrap.
        
            Human/LLM orientation (curated ReadFirst reading order, TaskIndex catalog) lives on ReadMe
            blocks — not on Bootstrap.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:core.ReadMe" relation="ContrastsWith">
          Human/LLM reading entry — Library.readMe, not bootstrap.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.BlockLibrary" relation="SeeAlso">
          Optional bootstrap aggregation (0..1 SPOE).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BootstrapModel" relation="ContrastsWith">
          TypeRegistry bootstrap vs library ReadMe / Bootstrap roles.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — capbility type branch (capability implementation contract)

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Capability-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Capability
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Capability
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A behaviour endpoint
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Capability branch of BlockML. Declares a capability type. Every capability may
            carry an implementation whose language attribute references a Language FQN.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <properties>
        <_hasInput readOnly="true" type="blockml:type.Boolean">
          <documentation>
            Whether the capabality has an input and thus requires a specified inputType.
          </documentation>
          <value>false</value>
        </_hasInput>
        <_hasOutput readOnly="true" type="blockml:type.Boolean">
          <documentation>
            Whether the capabality has an output and thus requires a specified outputType.
          </documentation>
          <value>false</value>
        </_hasOutput>
        <implementation >
          <documentation>
            Capability implementation body. The language attribute must reference a
                    Language from org.blockml.bml.language.* — unqualified names permitted.
          </documentation>
        </implementation>
      </properties>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — constraint branch root

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Constraint-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Constraint
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Constraint
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A semantic validation rule
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract universal root for all constraint types. Specialized by
            org.blockml.bml.constraint.ValueConstraint (property value rules) and
            org.blockml.bml.constraint.StructuralConstraint (definition-shape rules on Blocks and members).
            Every concrete constraint extends one of the branch types; the &lt;constraints> embedding
            context determines the target and automatic generic binding — authors never specify target or
            type arguments in BML. Constraints describe the condition only. Whether BlockML enforces a
            constraint is controlled by the optional validation property, which references an
            org.blockml.bml.core.Validation block. When validation is omitted, validateConstraints() does not
            enforce the rule — the constraint remains metadata for renderers, editors, runtimes, and custom
            validators. The constraint instance itself is still validated (type, properties, generic
            binding, branch compatibility).
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <properties>
        <validation type="blockml:core.Validation">
          <documentation>
            How the BlockML validator should report a violation of this constraint.
                    When omitted, the validator ignores the constraint rule — the instance remains part of the
                    BOM as semantic metadata.
          </documentation>
        </validation>
      </properties>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — Definition base type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Definition

  Abstract base type for all BlockML definition elements.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Definition
