      <type>
        blockml:core.Group
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A named grouping category
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract base type for groups — named categories that organize related elements
            for editors and tooling. Intentionally empty — no properties, aggregations, or Sub-Blocks.
            Concrete group kinds extend this branch (e.g. org.blockml.bml.group.MemberGroup for Block
            members, org.blockml.bml.group.BlockGroup for Blocks).
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — keyword branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Keyword-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Keyword
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Keyword
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Entity
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A search and discovery tag
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract branch marker for keyword Entity instances in a keywords aggregation on
            org.blockml.bml.definition.StructuralDefinition elements. Follows Everything-is-a-Block —
            domain libraries define concrete keyword blocks extending this branch for controlled vocabulary
            tags (for example org.example.keyword.Inheritance extending org.blockml.bml.meta.FixedKeyword).
            The core library provides org.blockml.bml.meta.FreeTextKeyword for ad-hoc text tags and
            org.blockml.bml.meta.FixedKeyword as the protected base for catalogue keywords whose members are
            set on derived keyword block definitions; comma-separated shorthand on &lt;keywords> expands
            to FreeTextKeyword instances only — not to domain or FixedKeyword catalogue blocks.
            Distinct from org.blockml.bml.core.Alias (alternative Block type names on BlockDefinition).
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:core.Entity" relation="SeeAlso">
          Keyword instances are Entity children inside keywords aggregations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Alias" relation="ContrastsWith">
          Parallel branch for alternative Block type names — not search tags.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — Language branch (capability implementation contract)

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Language-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Language
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Language
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        An implementation language
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Language branch of BlockML. Declares an implementation language permitted on
            Capability implementations (OpenSCAD, TypeScript, English, …). Natural languages for
            documentation prose extend org.blockml.bml.language.NaturalLanguage; formal code and markup
            languages extend ProgrammingLanguage and MarkupLanguage. ProgrammingLanguage and MarkupLanguage
            declare optional generic L (NaturalLanguage, default English) for the content/text language of
            formal bodies — omit L unless the content language must be declared.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — mapping branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Mapping-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Mapping
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Mapping
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A value-transfer Mapping
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract branch for explicit value mapping between representations. Mapping is not a property
        of StructType — it belongs at the site where a function-like representation is used.
        
        Concrete mappings extend this branch and declare how authored syntax maps to BlockML types and
        how BlockML types map to target-language signatures.
        
        Component responsibilities:
        • Parser — stores authored syntax only; no mapping in Canonical BOM
        • Expression parser — AST with positional, named, object, and array literal arguments
        • Validator — resolves capability, selects FunctionMapping, validates mapped input against
          inputType
        • Renderer — may emit authored form, evaluate FunctionMapping, or interpret mapping for the
          target system; Canonical BOM stays unchanged
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.FunctionMappingModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Function parameter mapping rules.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:mapping.FunctionMapping" relation="SeeAlso">
          Concrete mapping for function-like call syntax.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — primitive value as Entity

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.PrimitiveEntity-->
    <block>
      <name>
        PrimitiveEntity
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.PrimitiveEntity
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Entity
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A primitive value as an Entity
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract generic Entity for primitive values that must appear as Block instances
            inside a composition or aggregation. Follows the general generic resolution rule: T belongs
            exclusively to this Block and is never inherited. Derived Blocks bind or forward T explicitly
            through baseType (for example baseType = PrimitiveEntity(Integer) binds T = Integer and yields
            value : Integer). On instances that member is authored as value="…" (or &lt;value>…&lt;/value>
            as the member itself) because the Property is named value — not because of the
            PropertyDefinition value field. Do not wrap other instance Properties in &lt;value>; see
            doc:MemberAuthoring.
        
            Cannot be instantiated directly — concrete specializations (IntegerEntity, TextEntity,
            DecimalEntity, …) bind all generic parameters and become normal, instantiable Blocks.
        
            Properties continue to store primitive values directly — PrimitiveEntity exists only when the
            primitive value itself must become an Entity with identity, documentation, or metadata.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeSystemOverview" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          PrimitiveEntity and generic resolution.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberAuthoring" relation="SeeAlso">
          Member named value on instances vs PropertyDefinition value wrapper.
        </see>
      </references>
      <genericTypes>
        <T baseType="blockml:type.PrimitiveType" />
      </genericTypes>
      <properties>
        <value type="T">
          <documentation>
            The primitive value owned by this Entity instance. Instance syntax is
                    value="…" or &lt;value>…&lt;/value> on the Entity because this member's name is value
                    — that is ordinary instance authoring, not a PropertyDefinition wrapper. See
                    doc:MemberAuthoring.
          </documentation>
        </value>
      </properties>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — library reading entry-point role

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.ReadMe-->
    <block>
      <name>
        ReadMe
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.ReadMe
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A library reading-entry role
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract branch for ReadMe blocks. A ReadMe declares where humans and LLMs start
            reading a library — curated learning order, vocabulary, and orientation. Distinct from
            org.blockml.bml.core.Bootstrap, which is the optional single point of entry (SPOE) for automated
            startup (tasks, agents, CI pipelines).
        
            org.blockml.bml.README is the standard ReadMe for the core library. Library descriptors point
            here via the required readMe aggregation (1..1).
        
            ReadMe blocks carry the normal documentation model (is, documentation, references). Curated
            reading order is expressed via &lt;references> with relation=ReadFirst; available task
            catalog entries via relation=TaskIndex — not via separate aggregations on ReadMe itself. The
            abstract base declares no members.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:core.Bootstrap" relation="ContrastsWith">
          Automation SPOE branch — separate from human/LLM reading entry.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.BlockLibrary" relation="Requires">
          readMe aggregation requires a ReadMe reference.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:README" relation="SeeAlso">
          Concrete ReadMe for the BlockML core library.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — documentation reference relation branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Relation-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Relation
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Relation
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A cross-reference relationship kind
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract branch for relation blocks on &lt;see> entries in &lt;references>.
            Relation values are Block references following the Everything-is-a-Block principle.
            Standard implementations live in org.blockml.bml.relation.*; domain libraries may define
            additional relation blocks. Implicit default when omitted on &lt;see>:
            org.blockml.bml.relation.SeeAlso. Standard relations include ReadFirst (README learning path),
            ReadNext (forward edge between documentation blocks), ThenContinueWith (Library bootstrap after
            reading — TypeRelation(core:Bootstrap)), TaskIndex (task catalog), TaskStep and Prerequisite
            (procedural guides), and Resolves (diagnostic to fix task). Unqualified names on relation=
            resolve via implicit namespace org.blockml.bml.relation.*.
        
            TypeRelation is the constrained subtype for relations that may only target see type= and whose
            target must satisfy a generic Block branch T (default core:Block).
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — maturity / lifecycle status branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Status-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Status
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Status
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A lifecycle maturity marker
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract branch for status blocks. Status values are Block references following the
            Everything-is-a-Block principle. Standard implementations live in org.blockml.bml.status.*;
            domain libraries may define additional status blocks. Implicit default when omitted:
            org.blockml.bml.status.Stable. Status is inherited downward — declare &lt;status> or bml:status
            only when deviating from the effective inherited status.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — procedural task guide base type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.TaskGuide-->
    <block>
      <name>
        TaskGuide
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.TaskGuide
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A how-to task procedure
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract branch for org.blockml.bml.task.* blocks — step-by-step procedures
            distinct from org.blockml.bml.documentation.* concept blocks. TaskGuide blocks carry the normal
            documentation model (is, summary, keywords, documentation, references). Ordered procedure
            steps use relation=TaskStep on &lt;see> entries; prerequisites use relation=Prerequisite.
            Task catalogs on README / ReadMe blocks list available tasks via relation=TaskIndex.
            Tooling filters by relation to build learn paths (ReadFirst), task paths (TaskStep), or error
            fix paths (Diagnostic blocks with relation=Resolves).
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:core.Documentation" relation="SeeAlso">
          Concept documentation blocks — mental model; TaskGuide blocks are procedural complements.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.ReadMe" relation="SeeAlso">
          Library reading entry — TaskIndex entries on README list available tasks.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — type-target-constrained relation branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.TypeRelation-->
    <block>
      <name>
        TypeRelation
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.TypeRelation
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Relation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A type-target-only Relation
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract Relation subtype that may be used only on &lt;see type="…"> entries —
            never on instance, file, url, property, aggregation, association, or capability targets.
        
            Generic T is the allowed target Block branch. The resolved type of see type= must satisfy
            satisfiesTypeBound(resolve(type), T) — otherwise BML_TYPE_BOUND_NOT_SATISFIED. Default
            T = core:Block — any Block type reference is allowed until a concrete subtype binds T more
            narrowly (e.g. ThenContinueWith binds T = core:Bootstrap).
        
            Plain Relation (SeeAlso, AuthoringGuide, …) remains unrestricted across target kinds.
            TypeRelation is for relations whose semantics require a Block type identity target.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:core.Relation" relation="Extends">
          Unrestricted relation branch — TypeRelation adds type-target + T bound.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.ThenContinueWith" relation="SeeAlso">
          Concrete TypeRelation(core:Bootstrap) — Library bootstrap after ReadFirst.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.TypeReference" relation="Requires">
          see type= target ValueType — only allowed target kind for TypeRelation.
        </see>
      </references>
      <genericTypes>
        <T baseType="blockml:core.Block" defaultType="core:Block" />
      </genericTypes>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — measurement unit branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Unit-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Unit
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Unit
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A measurement unit
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract Block branch for measurement units — a taxonomy/catalog like Language
            or Status, not a ValueType. Unit blocks describe a unit of measure; they never store concrete
            magnitudes and are not valid as Property type or Capability inputType/outputType. They appear
            as generic type arguments on Number (and domain types such as MultiAxis) — e.g.
            Decimal(unit:Millimeter), Float(unit:Degree). Standard implementations live in
            org.blockml.bml.unit.*; domain libraries may define additional unit blocks.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:core.Language" relation="SeeAlso">
          Parallel non-ValueType Block branch used as type/reference catalog.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.ValueType" relation="ContrastsWith">
          ValueType is for property payloads; Unit constrains numeric generics only.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — validation semantics branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.Validation-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Validation
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.Validation
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A constraint-violation severity
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract branch for validation semantics. A Validation block describes whether and
            how BlockML itself should enforce an attached constraint. Constraints reference Validation via
            the optional validation property on org.blockml.bml.core.Constraint. When validation is omitted,
            the BlockML validator ignores the constraint — it remains metadata for renderers, editors,
            runtimes, and custom validators. Standard implementations live in org.blockml.bml.validation.*;
            domain libraries may define additional Validation blocks (for example
            com.machineblocks.bml.validation.ManufacturingWarning). The BlockML validator only interprets
            Validation types it understands; other tools may interpret additional Validation blocks.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — value type branch (property / payload types)

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.core.ValueType-->
    <block>
      <name>
        ValueType
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:core.ValueType
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Block
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A typed value domain
      </is>
      <documentation>
        ValueType branch of BlockML. Semantic unit for property and Capability types
            (primitives, structs, enums). A ValueType never stores data — it describes the nature of a
            value. All concrete values are stored exclusively in Properties. Only ValueTypes may appear as
            property type or Capability inputType/outputType.
        
            Top of the value assignability hierarchy — every PrimitiveType, StructType, EnumType, and
            ConstantType is assignable to ValueType. With multiplicity, compatibility is evaluated against
            the referenced element type: ValueType[] accepts zero or more values whose concrete ValueType is
            assignable to ValueType, each element validated independently, so element types may differ
            within one collection. See doc:TypeExpressions — Collection compatibility ·
            Heterogeneous collections.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — aggregation member meta-model type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.AggregationDefinition
  See docs/12_bom_object_model.md — AggregationDefinition · 02_mbml.md — Aggregations.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        AggregationDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.AggregationDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:definition.MemberDefinition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        An owned collection member
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Describes a Block member representing an owned collection. Children are always
            Block instances; the aggregation type attribute selects the payload type:
        
            • Plain Block type (for example core:Block[], bricks:Brick[]) — composition ownership of those
              Block types. Children are Block instances: member values, nested aggregations filled without
              a composition wrapper, and bml:id.
            • TypeReferenceEntity application (for example type:TypeReferenceEntity(core:Block)[]) —
              owned TypeReferenceEntity instances whose value holds an FQN / TypeExpression. Empty children
              whose element QName is not TypeReferenceEntity are syntactic sugar for
              &lt;type:TypeReferenceEntity value="…QName…" /> (B/D bound by the aggregation type).
        
            Rendered as a child element inside the aggregations container; the XML element name is the
            aggregation name (bml:xml="${ #this.name }"). An optional XML prefix on the element name supplies
            the member namespace — resolved via xmlns to BOM namespace; unprefixed elements use the default
            namespace (empty string). Member identity is (resolvedNamespace, localName). Maps to BOM
            AggregationDefinition (kind: "aggregation") with type expression, cardinality (from type
            multiplicity), optional default flag, and optional nested composition template. Standalone
            composition is never allowed — &lt;composition> must be nested inside the aggregation member
            (BML_STANDALONE_COMPOSITION). Scalar member fields (type, default) follow
            org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition placement rules — element preferred on aggregation
            declarations. Structured composition remains a child element.
            Composition instance ID reachability for bml:id references is controlled by this aggregation
            member's visibility (inherited from org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition) — public
            aggregations export ids for cross-document {EnclosingBlockFQN}::{id} references; protected
            limits reach to the declaring Block and its child Blocks; private limits reach to the declaring
            Block only. Service binding on composition children uses bml:bean and bml:scope on Block
            instances — not aggregation member fields.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberAuthoring" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Definition vs instance — composition wrapper only on Aggregation declarations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompositionModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Composition placement, recursion, and instance ID reachability.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberVisibility" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Aggregation visibility controls which bml:id values may be referenced.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Block" relation="Requires">
          Required branch for aggregation payload types.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity" relation="Requires">
          Entity payload for type-reference aggregations (contracts, Library lists).
        </see>
      </references>
      <properties>
        <type bml:xml="element|attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.Block)">
          <documentation>
            Aggregation payload type (TypeExpression). Collections of domain Blocks use a
                    plain Block FQN with multiplicity — for example core:Block[], core:Block[0..n],
                    bricks:Brick[1..1]. Type-reference collections use a TypeReferenceEntity application — for
                    example type:TypeReferenceEntity(core:Block)[],
                    type:TypeReferenceEntity(core:Language)[1..1]. Each child is a TypeReferenceEntity instance
                    (or empty-QName sugar); value must satisfy satisfiesTypeBound(resolve(value), B). Required on
                    new aggregation declarations; may be omitted when filling an inherited aggregation (partial
                    override). On definitions, element form is preferred; on Block instances, attribute form is
                    preferred.
          </documentation>
        </type>
        <default bml:xml="element|attribute">
          <documentation>
            When true, marks this aggregation as the primary aggregation of the enclosing
                    Block for tooling and render targets. Does not change composition placement —
                    &lt;composition> remains nested inside the aggregation member. At most one
                    default="true" aggregation per Block. Maps to BOM AggregationDefinition.default. Default
                    false.
          </documentation>
          <value>false</value>
        </default>
        <composition bml:xml="element" type="blockml:definition.AggregationDefinition.Composition">
          <documentation>
            Optional nested composition template filling this aggregation on the Block
                    definition only. Rendered as a composition child element inside the aggregation member
                    element. Maps to BOM AggregationDefinition.composition (CompositionTemplate). May be omitted
                    on override declarations that only refine metadata. Not an instance wrapper — on Block
                    instances, aggregation children sit directly inside the aggregation element with no
                    composition wrapper, including when that instance is nested inside another composition.
                    See doc:MemberAuthoring and doc:CompositionModel.
          </documentation>
        </composition>
      </properties>
      <subBlocks>
        <block>
          <name>
            Composition
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:definition.AggregationDefinition.Composition
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Entity
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A nested composition template
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Describes the composition element on an aggregation member declaration only.
                    Rendered as composition with optional bml:with directives and a tree of composition child
                    nodes (Block instances and bml:container virtual containers). Not an instance wrapper —
                    nested Block instances in this tree fill their own aggregations directly, without a nested
                    composition element. Maps to BOM
                    CompositionTemplate { aggregationName (from enclosing aggregation member name), directives,
                    children }. Instance ID reachability is determined by the enclosing aggregation member's
                    visibility — not by attributes on the composition element. Standalone composition outside an
                    aggregation member element is invalid (BML_STANDALONE_COMPOSITION). Declared as the
                    composition property on org.blockml.bml.definition.AggregationDefinition.
          </documentation>
          <bml:xml>composition</bml:xml>
        </block>
      </subBlocks>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — association member meta-model type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.AssociationDefinition
  See docs/12_bom_object_model.md — AssociationDefinition · 02_mbml.md — Associations.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        AssociationDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.AssociationDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:definition.MemberDefinition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        An instance-to-instance link member
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Describes a Block member that connects instances to instances — non-owning model
            links between concrete Block instances, without lifecycle ownership. Distinct from documentation
            References (&lt;see> on StructuralDefinition.references), which connect types to types or
            types to instances for documentation only. Rendered as a child element inside the associations
            container; the XML element name is the association name (bml:xml="${ #this.name }"). An optional
            XML prefix on the element name supplies the member namespace — resolved via xmlns to BOM
            namespace; unprefixed elements use the default namespace (empty string). Member identity is
            (resolvedNamespace, localName). Maps to BOM AssociationDefinition (kind: "association") with
            type (InstanceReference TypeExpression) and cardinality (from type multiplicity). Association
            type must be InstanceReference(T) or InstanceReference(T)[] with T extending core:Block — bare
            Block FQNs (e.g. core:Block, bml:Self[]) are invalid. Instance values are authored on Block
            instances as member attributes or elements (space-separated InstanceReference tokens, or
            expressions). Associations do not own composition children. Scalar member field type follows
            org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition placement rules — element preferred on definitions,
            attribute preferred on Block instances. Domain examples include basePresets on MachineBlocks
            Block types (type InstanceReference(bml:Self)[]).
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BlockAndInstance" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Associations vs references; instance links.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.InstanceReference" relation="Requires">
          Required type shape for association payload — InstanceReference(T) or InstanceReference(T)[].
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Block" relation="Requires">
          Required branch for InstanceReference type argument T.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition" relation="ContrastsWith">
          Documentation References (&lt;see>) connect types to types or types to instances — not
          instance-to-instance Association edges.
        </see>
      </references>
      <properties>
        <type bml:xml="element|attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:type.InstanceReference)">
          <documentation>
            TypeExpression InstanceReference(T) or InstanceReference(T)[] where T extends
                    core:Block. Multiplicity in the type string defines cardinality — 0..1 (single optional,
                    e.g. InstanceReference(core:Block)) or 0..n (collection, e.g.
                    InstanceReference(bml:Self)[]). Association cardinality is limited to 0..1 and 0..n —
                    required-single notations (1..1, 1..n) are invalid on associations. Bare Block FQNs
                    without InstanceReference wrapper are invalid. Required on new association declarations;
                    may be omitted when overriding an inherited association (partial override — type inherited
                    from baseType chain). On definitions, element form is preferred
                    (&lt;type>InstanceReference(bml:Self)[]&lt;/type>); on Block instances, attribute
                    form is preferred (type="InstanceReference(bml:Self)[]" on the association element).
                    General type arguments require FQN or xmlns prefix — no implicit namespace fallback.
          </documentation>
        </type>
      </properties>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — block definition meta-model base type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.BlockDefinition
  Describes the shared parse-time shape of all <block>

 definition documents.
  See docs/12_bom_object_model.md — BlockDefinition · 16_framework_base_types.md — Definition Branch.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        BlockDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.BlockDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:definition.ConstrainableDefinition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        The shape of a Block document
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract meta-model base for all Block definition documents — root blocks,
            Sub-Blocks, and embedded virtual files. Semantically a Class: declarative type shape without instance
            state. Parsed into BomDocument.block and nested block definition trees. Generic type parameter B
            binds this definition to the Block type it describes — conceptually BlockDefinition of
            com.foo.Bar when B resolves to com.foo.Bar.
        
            Declares identity first (name, type, baseType, version), then semantic and discovery fields
            (is, isNot, aliases), generic parameters, contracts, and
            member declaration containers (properties, aggregations, associations, capabilities). Inherits
            summary and keywords aggregation (core.Keyword[]) from
            org.blockml.bml.definition.StructuralDefinition. isNot is block-level only; aliases aggregation
            (core.Alias[]) lists domain Alias blocks or org.blockml.bml.meta.FreeTextAlias instances. Omitted when unnecessary. Type modifiers abstract and final are inherited from
            org.blockml.bml.definition.StructuralDefinition. Every definition element is addressable via
            its canonical structural path within the Block FQN (for example com.foo.Bar.properties.width).
            Member declaration order in BML is preserved in BOM and is significant for StructType
            positional mapping and render targets.
        
            Declares definition (meta-model role selector) and subBlocks (recursive nested type hierarchy).
            Specialized as three sibling kinds that each override definition with a tighter TypeReference and
            default: SubBlockDefinition (nested), RootBlockDefinition (document root + embeddedBlocks),
            EmbeddedBlockDefinition (virtual .bml under root embeddedBlocks). Identity properties type and
            baseType are TypeReference strings (not TypeReferenceEntity aggregations) — type names the Block
            being declared; baseType carries the parent TypeExpression including generic applications. Other
            type references (composition tags, member type attributes, contracts) accept FQN, xmlns QName,
            context-bound unqualified fallback, or Sub-Block-relative names — all normalized to a canonical
            FQN during resolution (BML_UNKNOWN_TYPE, BML_AMBIGUOUS_TYPE, BML_UNKNOWN_NAMESPACE_PREFIX).
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeIdentityDerivation" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Type references and root / Sub-Block identity derivation.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ContractsModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          contracts aggregation — merge order and type bounds.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BlockNameConvention" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          PascalCase &lt;name> rules.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Block" relation="Extends">
          Sole inheritance root — empty &lt;baseType /> only (org.blockml.bml.core.Block).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.SubBlockDefinition" relation="Extends">
          Nested Sub-Block definition shape — overrides definition.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.RootBlockDefinition" relation="Extends">
          Document root — overrides definition; adds embeddedBlocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition" relation="Extends">
          Virtual .bml under root embeddedBlocks — overrides definition.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity" relation="Defines">
          Entity payload for contracts and language aggregations — not
          type/baseType (those stay scalar TypeReference text).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.TypeReference" relation="Defines">
          ValueType for identity type and baseType properties (FQN / TypeExpression text).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:group.BlockGroup" relation="Defines">
          Narrows inherited StructuralDefinition.groups to BlockGroup references.
        </see>
      </references>
      <genericTypes>
        <B baseType="blockml:core.Block" />
      </genericTypes>
      <properties>
        <name bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.Text">
          <documentation>
            Short class name (PascalCase). Required on Sub-Blocks. Optional on root blocks —
                    when omitted, derived as lastSegment(resolve(type)). When present on a root block, must
                    equal lastSegment(resolve(type)) (BML_BLOCK_NAME_TYPE_MISMATCH). Format: [A-Z][a-zA-Z0-9]*
                    (BML_INVALID_BLOCK_NAME). First child of every Block definition document (with type /
                    baseType) — definition identity comes before is.
          </documentation>
        </name>
        <type bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.Block)">
          <documentation>
            Canonical Block type identity — scalar TypeReference (FQN) text, not a
                    TypeReferenceEntity aggregation. Required on root blocks (BML_MISSING_BLOCK_TYPE). Authored
                    as element text — FQN, xmlns QName, or Sub-Block-relative name (for example foo:MyBlock or
                    com.foo.MyBlock). The named type need not exist yet — this property declares identity for the
                    Block being defined. Optional on Sub-Blocks — when omitted, derived as
                    resolve(parent.type) + "." + name; when present, must equal that expected value
                    (BML_SUBBLOCK_TYPE_MISMATCH). Stored in BOM as block.type (FQN string). Immediately after
                    name, before is.
          </documentation>
        </type>
        <baseType bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.Block)">
          <documentation>
            Parent type TypeReference / TypeExpression. Required on all definitions
                    except the sole inheritance root (org.blockml.bml.core.Block), which omits &lt;baseType>
                    entirely (BML_MISSING_BASE_TYPE elsewhere). Authored as element text — FQN or xmlns QName,
                    including generic applications that bind type parameters (for example core:Entity or
                    core:PrimitiveEntity(type:Integer)). This is the site where generic applications on the
                    parent are resolved. Stored in BOM as block.baseType (FQN string). Immediately after type,
                    before is.
          </documentation>
        </baseType>
        <is bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.Text">
          <documentation>
            Required: names the Idea (Urbild) — what this Block semantically represents.
                    Noun phrase that clearly conveys meaning; may be several words and must say more than bare
                    name or type when those alone are ambiguous (for example "A version number", not merely
                    "A version"). Not a purpose clause and not full technical detail — use summary and
                    documentation for those. Required on every Block definition (root blocks and Sub-Blocks).
                    Follows definition identity (name / type / baseType) — never before them. Stored in BOM
                    block.is. Addressable as com.foo.Bar.is. Definition reflection: bml:Self.is. The Block itself
                    is the WOT Idea (Urbild); &lt;is> names that Idea (Urbild) in prose for humans and LLMs —
                    see 02_mbml.md § Terminology — Urbild. Omitted → BML_MISSING_IS.
          </documentation>
        </is>
        <isNot bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.Text">
          <documentation>
            What this Block is not — explicit disambiguation from easily confused concepts
                    (for example Sub-Block vs embedded Block, contract vs baseType, Alias vs Keyword). Required
                    when confusion risk exists; optional only when no realistic nearby concept invites
                    misreading. Block-level only — not declared on member definitions. Plain trimmed text body
                    stored in BOM block.isNot. Addressable as com.foo.Bar.isNot. Definition reflection:
                    bml:Self.isNot. Prefer relation=ContrastsWith on references when pointing at a specific
                    confusing type; isNot states the negation inline on the current definition. Missing when
                    required → BML_MISSING_IS_NOT.
          </documentation>
        </isNot>
        <version bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.Version">
          <documentation>
            Semantic version of this Block definition. Required on root blocks.
                    Addressable as com.foo.Bar.version.
          </documentation>
        </version>
        <definition abstract="true" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:definition.BlockDefinition)">
          <documentation>
            Definition meta-model type for this Block document. Selects which fields exist
                    when accessing the definition via bml:Self. Abstract on BlockDefinition — each concrete
                    specialization (SubBlockDefinition, RootBlockDefinition, EmbeddedBlockDefinition) must
                    override with a tighter TypeReference bound and a default value. Authored as FQN, xmlns
                    QName, or unqualified name — unqualified names resolve via implicit namespace
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.*. Addressable as &lt;blockFQN>.definition.
          </documentation>
        </definition>
      </properties>
      <aggregations>
        <aliases type="blockml:core.Alias[]">
          <documentation>
            Alternative names for this Block type. Each child is a org.blockml.bml.core.Alias
                    Entity instance — domain catalogue blocks extending org.blockml.bml.meta.FixedAlias (fixed
                    block reference, for example &lt;vocab:SubBlockAlias />), or org.blockml.bml.meta.FreeTextAlias
                    for ad-hoc names (for example &lt;meta:FreeTextAlias name="Sub-Block" />). Block-level only.
                    Comma-separated plain text
                    on &lt;aliases> is shorthand that expands to FreeTextAlias instances only. Addressable as
                    com.foo.Bar.aliases.
          </documentation>
        </aliases>
        <language type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity(blockml:language.NaturalLanguage)">
          <documentation>
            Definition meta — natural language for documentation prose on this Block
                    document and its members (not a core:Block / BlockLibrary catalog member). Singular
                    TypeReferenceEntity aggregation — one child (for example &lt;German/> sugar for
                    &lt;type:TypeReferenceEntity value="lang:German" />). Omitted = inherit from enclosing
                    Library document &lt;language>, then the English default authored on
                    org.blockml.bml.core.Block. Unqualified child names resolve via implicit namespace
                    org.blockml.bml.language.*. Stored in BOM as block.language (TypeReferenceEntity instance);
                    languageFqn is the resolved value string. Bare TypeReferenceEntity(T) = optional single
                    (0..1). Authored at document root — not inside &lt;aggregations> / &lt;composition>.
          </documentation>
        </language>
        <genericTypes type="blockml:definition.GenericTypeDefinition[]">
          <documentation>
            Generic type parameters declared on this Block (root, Sub-Block, or embedded —
                    Java static nested model). Each entry has a name and a baseType constraint for concrete type
                    arguments at reference sites. Sub-Blocks do not see enclosing generics — forward explicitly
                    (e.g. type="Selector(T)"). Name must not equal this Block's name or a direct Sub-Block name
                    (BML_GENERIC_BLOCK_NAME_COLLISION / BML_GENERIC_SUBBLOCK_NAME_COLLISION).
          </documentation>
        </genericTypes>
        <contracts type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity(blockml:core.Block)[]">
          <documentation>
            Contract Block references merged into this Block after the baseType chain
                  (root block only). Each child is a TypeReferenceEntity instance — for example
                  &lt;ac:Wall /> expands to &lt;type:TypeReferenceEntity value="ac:Wall" />. Declaration
                  order is significant — later contracts override earlier ones; local Block declarations
                  override all contracts. Stored in BOM as block.contracts[].
          </documentation>
        </contracts>
        <groups override="true" bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity(blockml:group.BlockGroup)[]">
          <documentation>
            Optional BlockGroup references that organize this Block for editors and
                    tooling — a Block may belong to several groups at once. Overrides
                    StructuralDefinition.groups, narrowing the Group branch to
                    org.blockml.bml.group.BlockGroup. Each child is a TypeReferenceEntity instance — empty
                    children whose element QName is not TypeReferenceEntity are sugar, e.g.
                    &lt;mbgroup:SomeBlockGroup /> expands to
                    &lt;type:TypeReferenceEntity value="mbgroup:SomeBlockGroup" />. Document order preserved
                    in BOM groups[].
          </documentation>
        </groups>
        <properties type="blockml:definition.PropertyDefinition[]">
          <documentation>
            Property member declarations. Mapped to members[] in XML document order.
                    Order is significant for StructType field indices and positional array literals.
          </documentation>
        </properties>
        <aggregations type="blockml:definition.AggregationDefinition[]">
          <documentation>
            Aggregation member declarations — owned collections that may hold composition
                    children. Mapped to members[] in XML document order.
          </documentation>
        </aggregations>
        <associations type="blockml:definition.AssociationDefinition[]">
          <documentation>
            Association member declarations — instance-to-instance links without
                    composition ownership. Distinct from documentation References (type↔type / type↔instance).
                    Mapped to members[] in XML document order.
          </documentation>
        </associations>
        <capabilities type="blockml:definition.CapabilityDefinition[]">
          <documentation>
            Capability member declarations — communication endpoints (inbound, outbound,
                    transformation). Mapped to members[] in XML document order.
          </documentation>
        </capabilities>
        <subBlocks type="blockml:definition.SubBlockDefinition[]">
          <documentation>
            Sub-Block definitions nested under this block. Each Sub-Block is a
                    SubBlockDefinition that may declare its own subBlocks to arbitrary depth. Canonical type is
                    derived from resolve(parent.type) + "." + name when type is omitted; explicit type must match
                    (BML_SUBBLOCK_TYPE_MISMATCH). Sub-Block name is required (BML_MISSING_SUBBLOCK_NAME).
                    Sub-Blocks must not declare embeddedBlocks (BML_EMBEDDED_BLOCKS_ON_SUBBLOCK). Allowed on
                    root blocks, embedded blocks, and nested Sub-Blocks.
          </documentation>
        </subBlocks>
      </aggregations>
      <bml:xml>block</bml:xml>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — capability member meta-model type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.CapabilityDefinition
  See docs/12_bom_object_model.md — CapabilityDefinition · 02_mbml.md — Capabilities.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        CapabilityDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.CapabilityDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:definition.MemberDefinition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A Capability member declaration
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Describes a Block member defining how the Block communicates. Rendered as a child
            element inside the capabilities container; the XML element name is the capability name
            (bml:xml="${ #this.name }"). An optional XML prefix on the element name supplies the member
            namespace — resolved via xmlns to BOM namespace; unprefixed elements use the default namespace
            (empty string). Member identity is (resolvedNamespace, localName). Maps to BOM
            CapabilityDefinition (kind: "capability") with typeFqn, inputType, outputType, and optional
            implementation binding. Inbound requires inputType; Outbound requires
            outputType; Transformation requires both. inputType and outputType use TypeExpression syntax and
            must resolve to ValueTypes. Scalar capability fields (type, inputType, outputType) follow
            org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition placement rules — element preferred on definitions,
            attribute preferred on Block instances. Structured implementation remains a child element.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CapabilitySemantics" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Capabilities authoring rules.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:capability.Inbound" relation="Requires">
          Capability typeFqn — requires inputType.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:capability.Outbound" relation="Requires">
          Capability typeFqn — requires outputType.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:capability.Transformation" relation="Requires">
          Capability typeFqn — requires inputType and outputType.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Language" relation="SeeAlso">
          Implementation language bindings.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.ValueType" relation="SeeAlso">
          inputType and outputType payload types.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Entity" relation="ContrastsWith">
          Implementation sub-block branch — structured language binding, not a ValueType.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.MemberDefinition" relation="Extends">
          Parent type — override/abstract/final are local declaration properties on MemberDefinition;
          capability adds an optional implementation binding element.
        </see>
      </references>
      <properties>
        <type bml:xml="element|attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.Capability)">
          <documentation>
            Capability kind FQN — Inbound, Outbound, or Transformation. Unqualified names
                    resolve via org.blockml.bml.capability.*. On definitions, element preferred; on Block
                    instances, attribute preferred.
          </documentation>
        </type>
        <inputType bml:xml="element|attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.ValueType)">
          <documentation>
            TypeExpression for the inbound payload. Required on Inbound and Transformation;
                    forbidden on Outbound. Must resolve to a ValueType branch. On definitions, element preferred;
                    on Block instances, attribute preferred.
          </documentation>
        </inputType>
        <outputType bml:xml="element|attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.ValueType)">
          <documentation>
            TypeExpression for the outbound payload. Required on Outbound and Transformation;
                    forbidden on Inbound. Must resolve to a ValueType branch. On definitions, element preferred;
                    on Block instances, attribute preferred.
          </documentation>
        </outputType>
        <implementation bml:xml="element" type="blockml:definition.CapabilityDefinition.Implementation">
          <documentation>
            Optional target-language implementation binding for this capability. Rendered as
                    an implementation child element with required language attribute and CDATA body. Maps to BOM
                    ImplementationBlock.
          </documentation>
        </implementation>
      </properties>
      <subBlocks>
        <block>
          <name>
            Implementation
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:definition.CapabilityDefinition.Implementation
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Entity
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A Capability code body
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Describes the implementation element on a capability member. Rendered as
                    implementation with a required language attribute referencing org.blockml.bml.language.*
                    and a CDATA source body. Maps to BOM ImplementationBlock { languageFqn, source,
                    embeddedExpressions }. Not a ValueType — structured metadata on the Entity branch. Capability
                    references inside the body use BML Embedded Expression syntax (&lt;$ … $>). Declared as
                    the implementation property on org.blockml.bml.definition.CapabilityDefinition.
          </documentation>
          <properties>
            <language bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.Language)">
              <documentation>
                Required implementation Language FQN (org.blockml.bml.language.TypeScript,
                            OpenSCAD, …). Unqualified names resolve via implicit namespace org.blockml.bml.language.*.
                            Must resolve to a registered Language type; the active Target/renderer must support it.
              </documentation>
            </language>
            <content bml:xml="text" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Target-language source body. Stored as character data on the host
                            &lt;implementation> element (BOM ImplementationBlock.source). Text node and CDATA
                            both accepted; authored form stored (authoredCharacterDataForm). Serializer emits CDATA
                            when escape requires it or when authored as CDATA (19 D8). May contain embedded BlockML
                            expressions between &lt;$ and $> delimiters.
              </documentation>
            </content>
          </properties>
          <bml:xml>implementation</bml:xml>
        </block>
      </subBlocks>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — constrainable definition base type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.ConstrainableDefinition
  See docs/18_constraints_and_suggestions.md — Constraint placement.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        ConstrainableDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.ConstrainableDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A definition that can carry Constraints
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract base for Block and member definitions that support a &lt;constraints>
            container. The embedding context determines the constraint target and binds the generic type
            parameter automatically — no target property in BML. Validator checks branch compatibility via
            the constraint baseType chain (BML_CONSTRAINT_TARGET_MISMATCH). Constraint order in BML is
            preserved in BOM. Target binding and generic resolution are not stored in the Canonical BOM.
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <aggregations>
        <constraints bml:xml="element" type="blockml:core.Constraint[]">
          <documentation>
            Constraint Block instances attached to this definition element. Each child is a
                    direct Constraint reference (element name = FQN via xmlns prefix). The embedding context
                    (Block, Property, Aggregation, Association, or Capability) determines the constraint target
                    and automatic generic binding. Without validation on the constraint instance, the validator
                    does not enforce the rule — the instance remains semantic metadata.
          </documentation>
        </constraints>
      </aggregations>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — embedded block definition meta-model type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition
  Describes the parse-time shape of virtual .bml documents in root <embeddedBlocks>.
  See docs/12_bom_object_model.md — Embedded Blocks.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        EmbeddedBlockDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:definition.BlockDefinition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        The shape of an embedded Block document
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Meta-model type for embedded Block documents — virtual .bml files inlined under
            the document root's &lt;embeddedBlocks> aggregation. Extends
            org.blockml.bml.definition.BlockDefinition (subBlocks, identity, members) and overrides
            definition to default EmbeddedBlockDefinition.
        
            Embedded blocks are full Block documents: mandatory fully qualified &lt;type> is the FQN
            (not nested under the root). They may declare subBlocks and generics like any Block. They must
            not declare &lt;embeddedBlocks> — nesting is forbidden (BML_NESTED_EMBEDDED_BLOCKS). Only the
            document root (RootBlockDefinition) may own embeddedBlocks; compose fat artifacts by listing
            virtual files flat at the root (bundlers flatten nested packages when merging).
        
            Within the BomDocument, each embedded FQN shadows the corresponding library or file-based
            BlockDefinition. Duplicate FQNs among embeds → BML_DUPLICATE_EMBEDDED_BLOCK. Externally,
            embeds are invisible.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.SubBlockModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Embedded Blocks — root-only Inline Library rules.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" relation="Extends">
          Shared block definition shape — identity, members, definition, and subBlocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.RootBlockDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Document root — sole owner of the embeddedBlocks aggregation.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.SubBlockDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Nested Sub-Block shape — sibling specialization.
        </see>
      </references>
      <properties>
        <definition override="true" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition)">
          <documentation>
            Definition meta-model type for this embedded Block document. Omitted =
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition. Must extend EmbeddedBlockDefinition
                    (BML_DEFINITION_TYPE_MISMATCH otherwise). Unqualified names at this site resolve via
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.*. Addressable as &lt;embeddedFQN>.definition.
          </documentation>
          <value>def:EmbeddedBlockDefinition</value>
        </definition>
      </properties>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — generic type parameter meta-model type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.GenericTypeDefinition
  See docs/16_framework_base_types.md — genericTypes · 12_bom_object_model.md — GenericType.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        GenericTypeDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.GenericTypeDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Definition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A generic type parameter
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Declares one generic type parameter on a root Block definition. Rendered inside
            the genericTypes container as an element whose local name is the parameter identifier (root
            block only). Maps to BOM GenericType { name, baseType, defaultType? } — name is derived from
            the XML element name, not from a child or attribute. The name is substituted in type references
            within this Block (e.g. type="T" → GenericParameterReference). baseType is the FQN constraint —
            each concrete type argument at reference sites must inherit from that branch
            (BML_GENERIC_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION on mismatch). Optional defaultType supplies the binding when
            a generic application omits the corresponding type argument: a concrete TypeExpression, or the
            name of an earlier sibling generic parameter (declaration-order only; cycles and forward
            references forbidden). Must satisfy baseType after resolution. See 17_type_system_bml.md —
            defaultType. Parameter name must differ from the Block name and every Sub-Block name.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.GenericsModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Generics — inheritance binding, defaultType, attribute syntax.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeExpressions" relation="SeeAlso">
          Generic application as a type expression.
        </see>
      </references>
      <properties>
        <name type="blockml:type.Text">
          <documentation>
            Parameter identifier — derived from the XML element name at parse time (e.g.
                    &lt;T baseType="…"/> → name = "T"). Must not collide with the Block name or any
                    Sub-Block name in the same document. Not authored as an attribute or child element.
          </documentation>
        </name>
        <baseType bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.Block)">
          <documentation>
            FQN constraint for concrete type arguments. Each supplied type argument at
                    reference sites (e.g. Box(Integer)) must inherit from this baseType branch.
          </documentation>
        </baseType>
        <defaultType bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.Block)">
          <documentation>
            Optional default when a generic application omits this parameter's type
                    argument. Either a concrete TypeExpression (FQN / configured type) that satisfies baseType,
                    or the bare name of an earlier sibling generic parameter (e.g. defaultType="N"). Sibling
                    references are declaration-order only — forward refs and cycles are errors
                    (BML_GENERIC_DEFAULT_FORWARD_REF / BML_GENERIC_DEFAULT_CYCLE). After resolution the
                    effective binding must satisfy baseType. Authored as the defaultType attribute on the
                    genericTypes entry element (e.g. &lt;Q baseType="core:ValueType" defaultType="N"/>).
                    See 17_type_system_bml.md — defaultType. Omitted when every reference site must supply an
                    explicit type argument.
          </documentation>
        </defaultType>
      </properties>
      <bml:xml>${ #this.name }</bml:xml>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — block member meta-model base type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition
  See docs/12_bom_object_model.md — MemberDefinitionBase · 02_mbml.md — Members.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        MemberDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.MemberDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:definition.ConstrainableDefinition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A declared Block member
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract base type for Block member declarations — properties, aggregations,
            associations, and capabilities. Rendered as a child element inside the corresponding member
            container on BlockDefinition; the XML element name is the member name (bml:xml="${ #this.name }").
            An optional XML prefix on the element name supplies the member namespace — resolved via xmlns to
            BOM namespace; unprefixed elements use the default namespace (empty string). Member identity is
            (resolvedNamespace, normalizedLogicalName) — visibility prefixes (_ / __) are stripped from the
            element localName before match or collision checks. Declaration properties abstract, final, and
            override are local to each member declaration — not inherited. Only their semantic effects
            propagate (implementation obligations, override prohibitions).
        
            Member value placement (scalar members on PropertyDefinition, CapabilityDefinition, and shared
            MemberDefinition fields):
            • On member declarations (Block definition documents): each declaration field may render as a
              child element or as an attribute on the member element — both are valid; element form is
              preferred when serializing definitions (e.g. &lt;type>Integer&lt;/type> over
              type="Integer"; &lt;value>…&lt;/value> over value="…").
            • On Block instances (composition): the member itself renders as an attribute or child element
              of the Block — both are valid; attribute form is preferred (e.g. width="50" over
              &lt;width>50&lt;/width>). Do not wrap the payload in &lt;value> or &lt;composition>
              — those are PropertyDefinition / AggregationDefinition declaration fields only. Exception: a
              member whose name is value uses value="…" / &lt;value> as the member itself. See
              doc:MemberAuthoring.
            Structured children (constraints, groups, suggestions, composition, implementation) remain
            elements.
        
            Member declaration modes are orthogonal:
            • new — introduces a new member; must not set override="true"
            • override="true" — refines an inherited member of the same identity (namespace +
              normalizedLogicalName). Required on every local redeclaration that merges with an existing
              member from the baseType chain or from contracts[] — including contract member completion. Each
              redeclaration layer must set override="true" again — override is not inherited. Omission when a
              parent member exists → BML_OVERRIDE_REQUIRED. Visibility may widen on override (e.g. protected
              `_name` → public `name`); narrowing → BML_VISIBILITY_NARROWING. Member-level bml:xml may change
              on the same override. Private members cannot be overridden → BML_FINAL_MEMBER_OVERRIDE.
        
            Declaration properties abstract, final, and override are boolean properties local to the current
            declaration (default false). They are never inherited; only semantic effects apply across layers:
            - abstract="true" creates an implementation obligation for concrete descendants
            - final="true" prevents redeclaration in child blocks (validator checks all declaring layers)
            - override="true" confirms a deliberate redefinition of an inherited member
        
            A locally concrete redeclaration (abstract=false, the default) with override="true" satisfies an
            inherited implementation obligation when it is complete for the member kind (Property: type;
            Aggregation: type; Association: type; Capability: type + implementation). To keep a member
            abstract in a child block, explicitly declare abstract="true" on that redeclaration.
        
            Maps to BOM MemberDefinitionBase fields shared by all member kinds — including visibility and
            readOnly attributes, plus the groups aggregation (MemberGroup type references).
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" property="name" relation="Defines">
          Example property member reference — split form resolves to
          org.blockml.bml.definition.BlockDefinition.properties.name.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberAuthoring" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Definition fields vs instance payloads — no &lt;value> / &lt;composition> wrappers on
          instances.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberVisibility" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Visibility naming convention, access rules, and aggregation ID reachability.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.PartialMemberOverrides" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Partial overrides — merge semantics, abstract/final/override, readOnly.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Parent block shape — properties, aggregations, associations, capabilities containers.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.ConstrainableDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Shared constraints container for Block and member definitions.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:group.MemberGroup" relation="Defines">
          Narrows inherited StructuralDefinition.groups to MemberGroup references.
        </see>
      </references>
      <properties>
        <abstract bml:xml="element|attribute" type="blockml:type.Boolean">
          <documentation>
            Local declaration property — not inherited. When true, this member declaration is
                    semantically incomplete and creates an implementation obligation for concrete descendants.
                    bml:xml="element|attribute" — on definitions, element form is preferred; on Block instances,
                    attribute form is preferred. Boolean with explicit true or false; empty elements and attribute
                    shorthand without value are not permitted. Default false when omitted.
          </documentation>
          <value>false</value>
        </abstract>
        <final bml:xml="element|attribute" type="blockml:type.Boolean">
          <documentation>
            Local declaration property — not inherited. When true on this declaration, child
                    blocks cannot redeclare this member. The validator enforces this effect regardless of whether
                    final is set on descendant declarations. bml:xml="element|attribute" — on definitions, element
                    form is preferred; on Block instances, attribute form is preferred. Boolean with explicit true
                    or false; empty elements and attribute shorthand without value are not permitted. Default false
                    when omitted.
          </documentation>
          <value>false</value>
        </final>
        <override bml:xml="element|attribute" type="blockml:type.Boolean">
          <documentation>
            Local declaration property — not inherited. When true, this member declaration
                    deliberately redefines an inherited member of the same identity (namespace +
                    normalizedLogicalName; visibility prefixes stripped) from the baseType chain or from a merged
                    contract. Required on every local redeclaration that merges with such a parent member —
                    including contract member completion. Each redeclaration in the chain must set
                    override="true" again. Omission when a parent member exists → BML_OVERRIDE_REQUIRED.
                    bml:xml="element|attribute" — on definitions, element form is preferred; on Block instances,
                    attribute form is preferred. The validator requires a matching parent member in the
                    accumulated effective set (baseType chain and contracts, before local declarations). A new
                    member must not set override="true" → BML_OVERRIDE_WITHOUT_PARENT. Boolean with explicit true
                    or false; empty elements and attribute shorthand without value are not permitted. Default
                    false when omitted.
            
                    Visibility may widen on override (e.g. protected `_name` → public `name`); narrowing →
                    BML_VISIBILITY_NARROWING. Member-level bml:xml may change on the same override. Unchanged
                    type should be omitted (inherited) — restate type only when refining it. Private members
                    cannot be overridden → BML_FINAL_MEMBER_OVERRIDE. override does not modify abstract or final
                    on other layers. It may be used to change documentation or other optional metadata without
                    implementing an abstract member.
          </documentation>
          <value>false</value>
        </override>
        <visibility bml:xml="element|attribute">
          <documentation>
            Optional BlockML access visibility — private, protected, or public. Omitted =
                    derived from the member element name: __ prefix → private, _ prefix → protected, otherwise
                    public (BML_VISIBILITY_MISMATCH when explicit visibility does not match the naming
                    convention). On override, visibility may widen (protected → public) but not narrow
                    (BML_VISIBILITY_NARROWING). bml:xml="element|attribute" — on definitions, element form is
                    preferred; on Block instances, attribute form is preferred. Controls BlockML authoring access
                    — not renderer BOM access. On aggregations, visibility additionally controls composition
                    instance ID reachability: public — bml:id values referencable via {EnclosingBlockFQN}::{id}
                    from other documents; protected — ids reachable within the declaring Block and its child
                    Blocks only; private — ids reachable within the declaring Block only. Maps to BOM
                    MemberDefinitionBase.visibility.
          </documentation>
        </visibility>
        <readOnly bml:xml="element|attribute">
          <documentation>
            When true, Block instances must not set this member (BML_READONLY_MEMBER_WRITE).
                    Default false. bml:xml="element|attribute" — on definitions, element form is preferred; on
                    Block instances, attribute form is preferred. Definition and child override may still supply
                    values on the member declaration; readOnly forbids instance authoring only. Maps to BOM
                    MemberDefinitionBase.readOnly.
          </documentation>
          <value>false</value>
        </readOnly>
      </properties>
      <aggregations>
        <groups override="true" bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity(blockml:group.MemberGroup)[]">
          <documentation>
            Optional MemberGroup references that organize this member for editors and
                    tooling — a member may belong to several groups at once (e.g. a UI category and a usage
                    facet). Overrides StructuralDefinition.groups, narrowing the Group branch to
                    org.blockml.bml.group.MemberGroup. Each child is a TypeReferenceEntity instance — empty
                    children whose element QName is not TypeReferenceEntity are sugar, e.g.
                    &lt;mbgroup:Body /> expands to &lt;type:TypeReferenceEntity value="mbgroup:Body" />.
                    Document order preserved in BOM groups[]. Maps to BOM MemberDefinitionBase.groups.
          </documentation>
        </groups>
      </aggregations>
      <bml:xml>${ #this.name }</bml:xml>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — property member meta-model type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.PropertyDefinition
  See docs/12_bom_object_model.md — PropertyDefinition · 02_mbml.md — Members.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        PropertyDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.PropertyDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:definition.MemberDefinition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A typed value member
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Describes a Block member that carries a scalar or structured value. Rendered as a
            child element inside the properties container; the XML element name is the property name
            (bml:xml="${ #this.name }"). An optional XML prefix on the element name supplies the member
            namespace — for example ui5:width resolves via xmlns:ui5 to the canonical namespace stored in
            BOM namespace; unprefixed elements belong to the default namespace (empty string). The prefix
            itself is never stored. Member identity is (resolvedNamespace, localName). Maps to BOM
            PropertyDefinition (kind: "property") with typeFqn, values[] (value-field entries with
            namespace), optional groups (inherited from MemberDefinition), and optional constraints (via
            ConstrainableDefinition) and suggestions. Convenience path value is the default-namespace entry.
            Property type must resolve to a ValueType branch. Scalar member fields (type, value) follow
            org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition placement rules on the PropertyDefinition
            declaration — element preferred on definitions. The value field is definition syntax only;
            it is not a wrapper around a Property assignment on a domain Block instance. On instances the
            Property is a Block attribute or the Property element's direct content
            (myProperty="…" / &lt;myProperty>…&lt;/myProperty>) — see doc:MemberAuthoring. Structured
            children (groups, suggestions) remain elements. Every Property behaves as Optional — it has a
            value or has no value; there is no null. Declaration order among properties is significant for
            StructType positional mapping and Capability parameter order.
        
            Value-field Annotation facets: nested under this property element, an Annotation-prefixed
            value element (e.g. scad:value from Annotation.Value) multiplies the value field along the
            Annotation namespace axis — same property identity, not a member QName and not scad:myProp.
            Parent context disambiguates from top-level &lt;properties>&lt;scad:value/> members.
            See doc:AnnotationModel — Property value facets.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberAuthoring" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Definition value field vs instance Property payload — do not wrap instances in &lt;value>.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.AnnotationModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Annotation value facets, parent-context rules, duplicate-namespace diagnostics.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.ValueType" relation="Requires">
          Required branch for property payload types.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ConstraintsModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Constraints and suggestions on properties.
        </see>
      </references>
      <properties>
        <type bml:xml="element|attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.ValueType)">
          <documentation>
            FQN of the property's ValueType (TypeExpression). Non-ValueType FQNs are invalid
                    (BML_INVALID_PROPERTY_TYPE). Multiplicity, unions, generic applications, and instance
                    configuration are expressed in the type string — e.g. Text{maxLength: 100}. On the
                    PropertyDefinition declaration, element form is preferred (&lt;type>Integer&lt;/type>);
                    attribute form is also valid (type="Integer" on the property declaration element).
          </documentation>
        </type>
        <value bml:xml="element|attribute" type="blockml:type.Text">
          <documentation>
            Default or computed value on the PropertyDefinition declaration inside
                    &lt;properties> — definition syntax only. May be a literal or a ${ … } expression.
                    Element form is preferred (&lt;value>…&lt;/value>); compact attribute form on the
                    declaration is also valid (value="…"). Do not specify both on the same declaration.
                    Absent value means Optional.empty — not null.
            
                    This field is not a wrapper used when assigning the Property on a domain Block instance.
                    Instances use the member name as a Block attribute or as the Property element's direct
                    content (width="50" or &lt;width>50&lt;/width>) — never
                    &lt;width>&lt;value>50&lt;/value>&lt;/width> and never &lt;width value="50"/>
                    unless the member itself is named value (e.g. PrimitiveEntity.value,
                    TypeReferenceEntity.value). See doc:MemberAuthoring.
            
                    Multiplicity: the value field may repeat along the Annotation namespace axis — canonical
                    &lt;value> (default namespace) plus optional Annotation Element facets such as
                    &lt;scad:value>…&lt;/scad:value> (Annotation.Value, name="value") nested under this
                    property element. Stored in BOM values[] as PropertyValueEntry { namespace, value };
                    convenience path value is the default-namespace entry. Facets do not change member
                    identity (still this property's name) and are not the same as a top-level member
                    &lt;scad:value/> under &lt;properties>. Cross-namespace faceting is valid; same-namespace
                    self-faceting is forbidden. Duplicate (namespace) → BML_DUPLICATE_PROPERTY_VALUE.
                    See 02_mbml.md — Property value facets.
          </documentation>
        </value>
      </properties>
      <aggregations>
        <suggestions bml:xml="element" type="blockml:core.Block[]">
          <documentation>
            Property-only metadata — suggested values for editors and renderers. Each child
                    is a Block instance compatible with this property's typeFqn (Stage [8] —
                    BML_SUGGESTION_TYPE_MISMATCH). Coarse aggregation bound is Block[] (BOM BlockInstance[]);
                    exact compatibility is contextual: StructType / EnumType-adjacent instances use the
                    ValueType itself (e.g. SizeXYZ); PrimitiveType properties use the matching *Entity wrapper
                    (e.g. TextEntity for Text). Document order preserved in BOM suggestions[]. Must not appear
                    on properties typed with an EnumType (BML_SUGGESTIONS_ON_ENUM). Suggestions are not
                    enforced — authors may enter any compatible value. Always rendered as a suggestions child
                    element; children use their declared type tag (same pattern as composition instances).
          </documentation>
        </suggestions>
      </aggregations>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — root block definition meta-model type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.RootBlockDefinition
  Describes the parse-time shape of root <block> documents.
  See docs/12_bom_object_model.md — BlockDefinition.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        RootBlockDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.RootBlockDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:definition.BlockDefinition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        The shape of a root Block document
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Meta-model type for document-root block documents. Extends
            org.blockml.bml.definition.BlockDefinition, overrides definition (default RootBlockDefinition),
            and adds the embeddedBlocks aggregation (Inline Library — virtual .bml files co-located in one
            BomDocument). subBlocks is inherited from BlockDefinition.
        
            The definition property defaults to org.blockml.bml.definition.RootBlockDefinition when
            &lt;definition> is omitted on the document root. Embedded entries use
            org.blockml.bml.definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition (see EmbeddedBlockDefinition) — they are not
            RootBlockDefinition instances. Sub-Blocks use SubBlockDefinition — sibling specialization.
        
            embeddedBlocks is allowed only on the document root. Each child is an EmbeddedBlockDefinition
            (virtual file): own FQN from mandatory &lt;type>, own members and subBlocks, no nested
            embeddedBlocks. Nested embeds are forbidden (BML_NESTED_EMBEDDED_BLOCKS). Within this document,
            embedded FQNs shadow library/file definitions; duplicate embed FQNs →
            BML_DUPLICATE_EMBEDDED_BLOCK; externally embeds remain invisible.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.SubBlockModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Embedded Blocks (Inline Library) and Sub-Block nesting.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeIdentityDerivation" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Root Block identity — type required; name optional short form.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" relation="Extends">
          Shared block definition shape — identity, members, definition, and subBlocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.SubBlockDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Nested Sub-Block shape — sibling specialization.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Virtual .bml entries in embeddedBlocks — no nested embeds.
        </see>
      </references>
      <properties>
        <definition override="true" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:definition.RootBlockDefinition)">
          <documentation>
            Definition meta-model type for the document-root Block. Selects which fields
                    exist when accessing the definition via bml:Self. Omitted on the document root =
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.RootBlockDefinition.
            
                    Authored as FQN, xmlns QName, or unqualified name — unqualified names resolve via
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.* (for example RootBlockDefinition). The referenced type must
                    extend org.blockml.bml.definition.RootBlockDefinition (BML_DEFINITION_TYPE_MISMATCH when
                    declared on a Sub-Block, EmbeddedBlock, or when the type does not extend
                    RootBlockDefinition). Addressable as com.foo.Bar.definition.
          </documentation>
          <value>def:RootBlockDefinition</value>
        </definition>
      </properties>
      <aggregations>
        <embeddedBlocks type="blockml:definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition[]">
          <documentation>
            Inline Library — virtual .bml files embedded in this BomDocument. Allowed only
                    on the document root (RootBlockDefinition). Forbidden on Sub-Blocks
                    (BML_EMBEDDED_BLOCKS_ON_SUBBLOCK) and on EmbeddedBlockDefinition children
                    (BML_NESTED_EMBEDDED_BLOCKS).
            
                    Gedankenstütze: each entry is a virtual file — multiple BML documents in one source,
                    including packages outside the root's namespace. Child type is EmbeddedBlockDefinition with
                    mandatory fully qualified &lt;type> (BML_MISSING_EMBEDDED_BLOCK_TYPE,
                    BML_UNQUALIFIED_EMBEDDED_BLOCK_TYPE). FQN = resolve(type), not nested under the root FQN.
                    Sub-Blocks on an embedded block derive FQNs from the embedded type. Embeds may declare
                    subBlocks and generics; they must not declare embeddedBlocks. Duplicate FQNs among embeds
                    → BML_DUPLICATE_EMBEDDED_BLOCK. Within this document, embeds shadow library/file
                    definitions; externally invisible. Composition references must use fully qualified Block
                    types. Addressable as &lt;embeddedFQN>.* .
          </documentation>
        </embeddedBlocks>
      </aggregations>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — structural definition metadata

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.StructuralDefinition

  Abstract base type for BlockML definition elements that describe block structure.
  Defines the common documentation model shared by blocks,
  properties, aggregations, associations, capabilities, and
  future structural definition elements.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        StructuralDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Definition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A reusable structural definition
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract base type for BlockML structural definition elements. It provides the common
        documentation model consisting of summaries[], keywords, groups, documentations[] (i18n via
        language= and optional QName facets — facets are not for locales), version information, change
        history, examples, and references, plus local declaration properties (abstract, final) for block
        definitions. Concrete structural definition types inherit these members to describe themselves
        consistently. Member declarations declare abstract, final, and override locally via
        org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition. BlockDefinition and MemberDefinition override
        groups to narrow the Group branch (BlockGroup and MemberGroup respectively).
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
      <properties>
        <since bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.Version">
          <documentation>
            The version in which this definition became available.
          </documentation>
        </since>
        <status bml:xml="element" type="blockml:core.Status">
          <documentation>
            The status of the definition.
          </documentation>
        </status>
        <summary bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.Text">
          <documentation>
            Short prose expansion of semantic meaning — typically three to five sentences
                    between is and full documentation. Primary input for LLM context bundles and quick human
                    orientation; is names the Idea (Urbild), summary adds scope and boundaries without full technical
                    detail.
            
                    Content model: plain text. summary carries no markup language and does not support Markdown —
                    it is presentation-independent so that lists, search results, explorers, tooltips, and IDE
                    tooling can render it verbatim. Keep it to one short paragraph; structure belongs in
                    documentation. Its language= names a NaturalLanguage only — there is no markup default to
                    override (contrast documentation, whose default is Markdown(English)).
            
                    May repeat for i18n (language=) and optional QName facets (e.g. scad:summary) —
                    facet namespaces resolve to Annotations (not NaturalLanguage). Required: default-namespace entry when canonical
                    documentation is too long; optional only when that documentation alone remains easy to read.
                    Missing when required → BML_MISSING_SUMMARY. Duplicate (namespace, languageFqn) →
                    BML_DUPLICATE_SUMMARY. Stored in BOM summaries[] as DocumentationProseEntry. Primary path
                    com.foo.Bar.summary (default NS + effective language); collection com.foo.Bar.summaries.
          </documentation>
        </summary>
        <documentation bml:xml="element" type="blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition.Documentation">
          <documentation>
            Technical documentation prose for this definition element.
            
                    Content model: Markdown by default — the effective default is Markdown(English), declared as
                    the default value of the language property on the Documentation Sub-Block. Authors therefore
                    do not write language= for ordinary Markdown prose; the attribute is only needed to name a
                    different natural language — as the generic argument of Markdown (Markdown(German)) or by the
                    bare-language shorthand (German), which means the same thing. Markdown
                    is expected to be used: give longer prose headings, paragraphs, lists, and inline code
                    instead of one unbroken block of text — human-friendly formatting is part of BlockML, not
                    decoration.
            
                    May repeat for i18n (language=) and optional QName facets (e.g. scad:document) — facet
                    namespaces resolve to Annotations (org.blockml.bml.core.Annotation), not locales. Always required: ≥1 default-namespace documentation on every
                    Block definition (root and Sub-Blocks). Omitted → BML_MISSING_DOCUMENTATION. When canonical
                    prose grows too long for a quick scan, authors must also supply default-namespace summary.
                    Duplicate (namespace, languageFqn) → BML_DUPLICATE_DOCUMENTATION. See 02_mbml.md —
                    Documentation facets and i18n · Annotations.
          </documentation>
        </documentation>
        <abstract bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.Boolean">
          <documentation>
            Local declaration property — not inherited. Describes only the block or member on
                    which it is declared. Semantic effects (implementation obligations) propagate to descendants.
            
                    When true on a block definition, the type is abstract — not usable as a composition instance
                    type. Abstract block types create implementation obligations for concrete descendants. A child
                    block of an abstract type is concrete by default unless it explicitly declares
                    &lt;abstract>true&lt;/abstract>.
            
                    When true on a member declaration, the member is semantically incomplete and must be implemented
                    by a descendant before the enclosing block can be concrete. See
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition for member placement.
            
                    Boolean property with explicit true or false. Empty elements (&lt;abstract/>) and attribute
                    shorthand without value are not permitted. Default false when omitted — do not write false
                    explicitly unless documenting intent. On block definitions rendered as a child element
                    (&lt;abstract>true&lt;/abstract>). On member declarations rendered as element or attribute
                    — see org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition.
          </documentation>
          <value>false</value>
        </abstract>
        <final bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.Boolean">
          <documentation>
            Local declaration property — not inherited. Describes only the block or member on
                    which it is declared. Semantic effects (extension and override prohibitions) are enforced by
                    the validator across the inheritance chain.
            
                    When true on a block definition, the type is final — cannot be used as baseType on other
                    definitions.
            
                    When true on a member declaration, child blocks cannot redeclare this member (regardless of
                    whether they would change type, constraints, documentation, or implementation).
            
                    An abstract member must not be final, because it would become impossible to implement it
                    (abstract="true" &amp;&amp; final="true" is invalid).
            
                    Boolean property with explicit true or false. Empty elements (&lt;final/>) and attribute
                    shorthand without value are not permitted. Default false when omitted. On block definitions
                    rendered as a child element (&lt;final>true&lt;/final>). On member declarations rendered
                    as element or attribute — see org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition.
          </documentation>
          <value>false</value>
        </final>
      </properties>
      <aggregations>
        <keywords type="blockml:core.Keyword[]">
          <documentation>
            Optional search and discovery tags for RAG indexing. Each child is a
                    org.blockml.bml.core.Keyword Entity instance — domain catalogue blocks extending
                    org.blockml.bml.meta.FixedKeyword (fixed block reference, for example
                    &lt;vocab:Inheritance />), or org.blockml.bml.meta.FreeTextKeyword for ad-hoc terms
                    (for example &lt;meta:FreeTextKeyword term="inheritance" />). Comma-separated plain text on
                    &lt;keywords> is shorthand that expands to FreeTextKeyword instances only — one term per
                    trimmed token. Addressable as com.foo.Bar.keywords on blocks and
                    com.foo.Bar.properties.width.keywords on members.
          </documentation>
        </keywords>
        <groups bml:xml="element" type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity(blockml:core.Group)[]">
          <documentation>
            Optional Group references that organize this definition element for editors and
                    tooling — a definition may belong to several groups at once. Each child is a
                    TypeReferenceEntity instance — empty children whose element QName is not TypeReferenceEntity
                    are sugar, e.g. &lt;mbgroup:Body /> expands to
                    &lt;type:TypeReferenceEntity value="mbgroup:Body" />. Document order preserved in BOM
                    groups[]. Bound to org.blockml.bml.core.Group; BlockDefinition overrides to
                    group:BlockGroup and MemberDefinition overrides to group:MemberGroup.
          </documentation>
        </groups>
        <changelog type="blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition.Change[]">
          <documentation>
            The changelog of the definition.
          </documentation>
        </changelog>
        <examples type="blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition.Example[]">
          <documentation>
            Usage examples for this definition. Default-namespace &lt;examples> is the
                    canonical container. Children may be unprefixed &lt;example> (StructuralDefinition.Example)
                    or Annotation-prefixed entries such as scad:example (Annotation.Example) — siblings in the
                    same container, paired by title. Optional Annotation container facets (e.g. scad:examples)
                    hold independent target-specific sample sets beside the canonical slot. Facet namespaces
                    resolve to Annotations, not locales. Facet containers never replace the canonical examples
                    aggregation.
          </documentation>
        </examples>
        <references type="blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition.Reference[]">
          <documentation>
            Documentation cross-references of the definition. Connects types to types or
                    types to instances (also definition members, files, URLs) — documentation metadata only,
                    not instance-to-instance model links. For instance graph edges use Association members
                    instead.
          </documentation>
        </references>
      </aggregations>
      <subBlocks>
        <block>
          <name>
            Documentation
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition.Documentation
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:type.StructType
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A documentation prose field
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Describes one documentation prose entry on any
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.StructuralDefinition (block, property, aggregation, …). Rendered
                    as a documentation element (optionally QName-prefixed for Annotation facets) with optional language
                    attribute and a trimmed text body. The body is Markdown(English) by default — see the
                    language and content properties for the content model and the CDATA serialization rules.
                    Authors may place multiple siblings: i18n via language=
                    (Markdown(NaturalLanguage) or a bare NaturalLanguage); facets via annotation element namespace
                    (e.g. scad:document from
                    Annotation.Document). Facet namespaces are Annotations, not locales. Maps to BOM documentations[] DocumentationProseEntry
                    { namespace, text, languageFqn }. Identity (namespace, languageFqn) within documentations[] —
                    duplicate → BML_DUPLICATE_DOCUMENTATION. Declared as a repeatable property shape on
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.StructuralDefinition.
          </documentation>
          <properties>
            <language bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:language.Markdown(blockml:language.NaturalLanguage) | blockml:language.NaturalLanguage)">
              <documentation>
                Content language of this documentation text — Markdown carrying its natural
                            language as the generic argument L. Default Markdown(English): documentation prose is
                            Markdown unless stated otherwise, so authors normally omit this attribute.
                            &lt;documentation>A **Block** is …&lt;/documentation> is semantically equivalent to
                            &lt;documentation language="Markdown(English)">A **Block** is …&lt;/documentation>.
                
                            Bound to Markdown(NaturalLanguage) | NaturalLanguage —
                            org.blockml.bml.language.Markdown forwards MarkupLanguage.L (NaturalLanguage, default
                            English), so the first alternative admits Markdown with any natural language argument,
                            while the second keeps the bare-language shorthand:
                            • omitted — Markdown in the effective natural language of the enclosing definition
                              element (Block, then Library, then the English default)
                            • language="Markdown" — same as Markdown(English) via the L defaultType
                            • language="Markdown(German)" — German Markdown prose
                            • language="German" — shorthand for Markdown(German): a NaturalLanguage names the prose
                              language and keeps the Markdown default
                
                            Unqualified names resolve via implicit namespace org.blockml.bml.language.*, generic
                            argument included. Identity is the resolved natural language, stored as
                            DocumentationProseEntry.languageFqn: German and Markdown(German) denote the same entry —
                            authoring both on one definition element is a duplicate
                            (BML_DUPLICATE_DOCUMENTATION) — whereas Markdown(German) beside Markdown(English) are
                            two distinct i18n entries.
              </documentation>
              <value>lang:Markdown(lang:English)</value>
            </language>
            <content bml:xml="text" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Technical description text. Stored as trimmed body in BOM
                            DocumentationProseEntry.text on the enclosing definition element's documentations[].
                
                            Markdown needs no CDATA — CDATA is an XML serialization mechanism with no semantic
                            meaning for documentation. Serialize as ordinary XML text when no escaping is required,
                            and prefer CDATA over entity escaping when the text contains characters that would
                            otherwise be escaped (notably &lt; and &amp;), because CDATA keeps the authored Markdown
                            readable. Ordinary text, one CDATA section, and split CDATA sections all parse to the
                            same content. A serializer must split CDATA when the text itself contains the terminator
                            sequence, since CDATA sections cannot nest. See doc:DocumentationModel — Documentation,
                            Summary, Markdown and CDATA.
              </documentation>
            </content>
          </properties>
          <bml:xml>documentation</bml:xml>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Change
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition.Change
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Entity
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A single changelog entry
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Describes a single changelog entry in a changelog container on any definition
                    element (block, property, aggregation, …). Rendered as a change element with a required
                    version attribute and a trimmed text body describing what changed. Maps to BOM
                    ChangelogEntry { version, text, languageFqn? }. Entries must be ordered chronologically by
                    version — oldest first (BML_CHANGELOG_VERSION_ORDER when out of order). Optional language
                    attribute overrides the effective natural language for this entry's text body.
          </documentation>
          <properties>
            <version bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.Version">
              <documentation>
                Semantic version this change applies to. Required attribute on every change
                            entry.
              </documentation>
            </version>
            <language bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:language.NaturalLanguage)">
              <documentation>
                Optional NaturalLanguage when this change description is written in a
                            language other than the effective language of the enclosing definition element. Unqualified
                            names resolve via implicit namespace org.blockml.bml.language.*. Omitted = inherit
                            effective language from Block or Library.
              </documentation>
            </language>
            <description bml:xml="text" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Human-readable description of what changed at this version. Stored as trimmed
                            text body in BOM ChangelogEntry.text.
              </documentation>
            </description>
          </properties>
          <bml:xml>change</bml:xml>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Example
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition.Example
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Entity
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A titled usage example
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Describes one usage example in an examples container on any definition element
                    (block, property, aggregation, …). Rendered as an example element with a required title
                    attribute and a character-data body (bml:xml="text" — Text node or CDATA equally valid;
                    serializer chooses CDATA vs text per escape policy, 19 D8). The enclosing container may be
                    default-namespace &lt;examples> or an Annotation facet (e.g. scad:examples). Paired
                    target-language samples use Annotation.Example as a sibling under the same &lt;examples>
                    (e.g. scad:example with matching title) — preferred over a parallel scad:examples container.
                    Maps to BOM DocumentationExample { title, content, contentLanguageFqn?, namespace?, snippet }.
                    Empty body → BML_EXAMPLE_EMPTY_BODY. The title is always written in the effective natural
                    language of the enclosing block definition. An optional language attribute on the example
                    element references a Language FQN — implementation languages (OpenSCAD, TypeScript, …) for
                    code samples, or NaturalLanguage (English, German, …) for prose bodies. Optional snippet
                    marks illustrative fragments that are not complete / not to be syntax-checked.
          </documentation>
          <properties>
            <title bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Short title identifying this example. Required attribute on every example
                            element (BML_EXAMPLE_MISSING_TITLE when omitted). Always written in the effective natural
                            language of the enclosing block definition — no separate language attribute on title.
                            Annotation Example siblings (e.g. scad:example) use the same title to form a pair.
              </documentation>
            </title>
            <language bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.Language)">
              <documentation>
                Optional Language for the CDATA body. Unqualified names resolve via implicit
                            namespace org.blockml.bml.language.*. Use an implementation Language (OpenSCAD, TypeScript,
                            …) for code samples, or a NaturalLanguage (English, German, …) for prose. Omitted when
                            content is BML/MBML source. Applies to content only — titles always use the enclosing
                            block's natural language.
              </documentation>
            </language>
            <snippet bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.Boolean">
              <documentation>
                When true, the body is an illustrative fragment — incomplete, with ellipsis,
                            or otherwise not a complete artifact in the declared language. Validators and tooling
                            must not require well-formed / parseable syntax. Applies to code samples
                            (language="OpenSCAD") and prose (language="English") alike. Default false when omitted
                            — do not write false explicitly unless documenting intent. Boolean with explicit true or
                            false; empty elements and attribute shorthand without value are not permitted.
              </documentation>
              <value>false</value>
            </snippet>
            <content bml:xml="text" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                BML, MBML, target-language source, or prose demonstrating usage. Stored as
                            character data on the host element (BOM DocumentationExample.content). Text node and
                            CDATA both accepted; authored form stored (authoredCharacterDataForm). Serializer emits
                            CDATA when escape requires it or when authored as CDATA (19 D8). When snippet is true,
                            content need not be complete or syntactically valid.
              </documentation>
            </content>
          </properties>
          <bml:xml>example</bml:xml>
        </block>
        <block>
          <name>
            Reference
          </name>
          <type>
            blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition.Reference
          </type>
          <baseType>
            blockml:core.Entity
          </baseType>
          <is>
            A documentation cross-reference
          </is>
          <documentation>
            Records a documentation link from the current definition to another Block type,
                    Block instance, definition element, repository markdown file, or external URL. References
                    connect types to types or types to instances for documentation purposes — they are not
                    instance-to-instance model links (those are Associations / AssociationDefinition). Rendered
                    as a see element inside the references container on any
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.StructuralDefinition (alongside changelog and examples). Maps to
                    BOM DocumentationReference { type?, instance?, file?, url?, property?, aggregation?,
                    association?, capability?, relationFqn?, context, languageFqn? }. Optional relation attribute
                    references org.blockml.bml.relation.* — default org.blockml.bml.relation.SeeAlso when omitted.
                    Optional language attribute overrides the effective natural language for the relationship
                    text body.
            
                    Eight mutually exclusive target attributes (type, instance, file, url, property, aggregation,
                    association, capability) instead of one generic target. Type targets use TypeReference;
                    instance targets use InstanceReference; member targets use typed MemberReference
                    specializations — property : PropertyReference, aggregation : AggregationReference,
                    association : AssociationReference, capability : CapabilityReference — and store the
                    canonical definition path in the BOM (for example com.foo.Bar.properties.width).
                    Split authoring type="com.foo.Bar" property="width" resolves to that path — type qualifies
                    the member name and is not stored as a separate type target. Split authoring
                    type="com.foo.Bar" instance="myId" resolves to com.foo.Bar::myId.
                    The attribute name encodes the target kind at the read site — see type (TypeReference),
                    see instance (InstanceReference), see property (PropertyReference), see file (repository
                    documentation), see url (web address). Humans and LLMs classify each reference from element
                    shape alone without inferring kind from value syntax. See 02_mbml.md — Associations vs
                    References · references — Why separate target attributes.
          </documentation>
          <properties>
            <type bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.Block)">
              <documentation>
                Block type reference via xmlns prefix (e.g. core:Block) — or Block FQN
                            qualifier when paired with property, aggregation, association, capability, or instance.
                            Standalone type= resolves to FQN at parse time. With a member attribute, type= supplies
                            the enclosing Block FQN and is not stored as a separate type target. With instance=,
                            type= qualifies the local id into {FQN}::{id}. Mutually exclusive with file, url, and
                            standalone member/instance targets — exactly one target kind must be set.
              </documentation>
            </type>
            <instance bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.InstanceReference">
              <documentation>
                Block instance pointer (InstanceReference) — local id (myId), qualified
                            {FQN}::{id}, or xmlns form (foo:Bar::myId). When paired with type=, type= supplies the
                            enclosing Block FQN and the value is stored as FQN::id. Mutually exclusive with file,
                            url, type (standalone), and member targets.
              </documentation>
            </instance>
            <file bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Repository documentation file (e.g. 02_mbml.md). Mutually exclusive with all
                            other target attributes.
              </documentation>
            </file>
            <url bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                External URL. Mutually exclusive with all other target attributes.
              </documentation>
            </url>
            <property bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.PropertyReference">
              <documentation>
                PropertyDefinition path (PropertyReference) — canonical path (e.g.
                            com.foo.Bar.properties.width) or local member name when paired with type= (type
                            property="width" → com.foo.Bar.properties.width). Stored in BOM as canonical definition
                            path. Mutually exclusive with file, url, type (standalone), instance, aggregation,
                            association, and capability.
              </documentation>
            </property>
            <aggregation bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.AggregationReference">
              <documentation>
                AggregationDefinition path (AggregationReference) — canonical path (e.g.
                            com.foo.Bar.aggregations.children) or local member name when paired with type=. Stored
                            in BOM as canonical definition path. Mutually exclusive with all other target kinds.
              </documentation>
            </aggregation>
            <association bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.AssociationReference">
              <documentation>
                AssociationDefinition path (AssociationReference) — canonical path (e.g.
                            com.foo.Bar.associations.basePresets) or local member name when paired with type=.
                            Stored in BOM as canonical definition path. Mutually exclusive with all other target
                            kinds.
              </documentation>
            </association>
            <capability bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.CapabilityReference">
              <documentation>
                CapabilityDefinition path (CapabilityReference) — canonical path (e.g.
                            com.foo.Bar.capabilities.render) or local member name when paired with type=. Stored in
                            BOM as canonical definition path. Mutually exclusive with all other target kinds.
              </documentation>
            </capability>
            <relation bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:core.Relation)">
              <documentation>
                Relation block describing how the target relates to the current definition.
                            Block reference to org.blockml.bml.relation.* — unqualified names resolve via implicit
                            namespace org.blockml.bml.relation.*. Omitted = org.blockml.bml.relation.SeeAlso.
              </documentation>
            </relation>
            <language bml:xml="attribute" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:language.NaturalLanguage)">
              <documentation>
                Optional NaturalLanguage when the relationship text is written in a language
                            other than the effective language of the enclosing definition element. Unqualified names
                            resolve via implicit namespace org.blockml.bml.language.*. Omitted = inherit effective
                            language from Block or Library.
              </documentation>
            </language>
            <context bml:xml="text" type="blockml:type.Text">
              <documentation>
                Human-readable explanation of why or how the referenced element relates to
                            this definition. Required on every see entry.
              </documentation>
            </context>
          </properties>
          <bml:xml>see</bml:xml>
        </block>
      </subBlocks>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Definition — Sub-Block definition meta-model type

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.definition.SubBlockDefinition
  Describes the parse-time shape of nested Sub-Block <block> documents.
  See docs/12_bom_object_model.md — BlockDefinition.-->
    <block>
      <name>
        SubBlockDefinition
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:definition.SubBlockDefinition
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:definition.BlockDefinition
      </baseType>
      <is>
        The shape of a nested Sub-Block
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Meta-model type for nested Sub-Block documents within a BomDocument. Extends
            org.blockml.bml.definition.BlockDefinition and overrides definition (default SubBlockDefinition).
            Shared identity, members, and subBlocks are inherited from BlockDefinition — Sub-Block-specific
            identity rules live here.
        
            Block identity conventions for Sub-Blocks (see 02_mbml.md — Type References and Block Identity):
            • Sub-Block: semantically nested type within the same BomDocument as the root block — does not
              open its own document or library scope. Analogous to a Java static nested class: may declare
              own &lt;genericTypes>; does not see enclosing Block generics (forward with applications).
              Uses org.blockml.bml.definition.SubBlockDefinition
              as definition meta-model — default org.blockml.bml.definition.SubBlockDefinition when
              &lt;definition> is omitted. name is the local identity (required). type is normally
              omitted and derived recursively from the immediate enclosing block as
              resolve(parent.type) + "." + name (BML_SUBBLOCK_TYPE_MISMATCH when an explicit type does not
              match). Sub-Blocks may declare further Sub-Blocks to any depth (for example
              com.foo.Vehicle.Engine.Cylinder). Unqualified nested names resolve enclosing-chain first
              (02_mbml.md — Unqualified nested type resolution). Sub-Blocks must not declare embeddedBlocks
              (BML_EMBEDDED_BLOCKS_ON_SUBBLOCK).
        
            Root blocks use org.blockml.bml.definition.RootBlockDefinition; embedded virtual files use
            org.blockml.bml.definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition — sibling specializations of
            BlockDefinition.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.SubBlockModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Sub-Block visibility, embeds distinction, and reference scope.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeIdentityDerivation" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Sub-Block identity — name required; type derived from parent.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" relation="Extends">
          Shared block definition shape — identity, members, definition, and subBlocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.RootBlockDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Document root shape — sibling specialization; adds embeddedBlocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Virtual .bml entries under root embeddedBlocks — sibling specialization.
        </see>
      </references>
      <properties>
        <definition override="true" type="blockml:type.TypeReference(blockml:definition.SubBlockDefinition)">
          <documentation>
            Definition meta-model type for this Sub-Block's definition document. Selects
                    which fields exist when accessing the definition via bml:Self in definition reflection —
                    for example com.foo.Bar.Engine.is. Omitted on Sub-Blocks =
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.SubBlockDefinition.
            
                    Authored as FQN, xmlns QName, or unqualified name — unqualified names at this site resolve
                    via implicit namespace org.blockml.bml.definition.* (for example SubBlockDefinition or
                    definition:SubBlockDefinition). The referenced type must extend
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.SubBlockDefinition and must not extend
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.RootBlockDefinition or
                    org.blockml.bml.definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition (BML_DEFINITION_TYPE_MISMATCH). Addressable
                    as com.foo.Bar.Engine.definition.
          </documentation>
          <value>def:SubBlockDefinition</value>
        </definition>
      </properties>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — BML_ABSTRACT_MEMBER_NOT_IMPLEMENTED diagnostic

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.diagnostic.BML_ABSTRACT_MEMBER_NOT_IMPLEMENTED-->
    <block>
      <name>
        BML_ABSTRACT_MEMBER_NOT_IMPLEMENTED
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:diagnostic.BML_ABSTRACT_MEMBER_NOT_IMPLEMENTED
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Diagnostic
      </baseType>
      <is>
        An unimplemented abstract-member diagnostic
      </is>
      <summary>
        Emitted when a concrete Block still has an abstract member obligation from the inheritance/contract merge.
      </summary>
      <documentation>
        Implement with override="true" and a locally complete declaration (abstract defaults to false). To keep abstract, redeclare with abstract="true".
      </documentation>
      <keywords>
        <blockml:meta.FreeTextKeyword term="BML_ABSTRACT_MEMBER_NOT_IMPLEMENTED" />
      </keywords>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.PartialMemberOverrides" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Abstract → implementation.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:task.FixMemberOverride" relation="Resolves">
          Complete or re-abstract the inherited member.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
