    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.AnnotationModel-->
    <block>
      <name>
        AnnotationModel
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.AnnotationModel
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to Annotation namespaces and facets
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Annotations formalize directive namespaces. They are orthogonal to the Definition meta-model
        (org.blockml.bml.definition.*) — they do not describe block structure, members, or constraints.
        Naming overlap (Property, Aggregation, …) classifies directive form only;
        Annotation.Property is not def:PropertyDefinition.
        
        Every BML-semantic xmlns prefix resolves to either a Package (type addressing) or a concrete
        Annotation (directives). preferredAlias is authoring convention only (bml, scad) — the BOM
        stores the Annotation FQN, never the XML prefix. Document default xmlns and xsi:schemaLocation
        use http://blockml.org/bml (→ bml.xsd); legacy schema URIs are obsolete. BML-semantic xmlns:bml
        must target ann:BmlAnnotation — never a schema URL.
        
        Branch shape (core:Annotation):
        • preferredAlias : Text
        • directives : Directive[]
        • Directive — name = local name
          · Member (name="*") → Property | Aggregation | Association | Capability
          · Element → Document | Summary | Examples | Example | Value (fixed names)
          · Attribute — fixed names (id, each, with, …)
        
        Concrete annotations:
        • org.blockml.bml.annotation.BmlAnnotation (bml) — language directives; see doc:BmlDirectives
        • Domain annotations (e.g. com.machineblocks.bml.annotation.ScadAnnotation, scad) — members,
          value facets, prose facets, example facets
        
        Directive match:
        • Attribute / Element (incl. fixed Document, Summary, Examples, Example, Value) —
          localName == directive.name
        • Member subtypes — name="*"; any local name under the annotation; kind from the Member subtype
        
        Parent context for *:value:
        • Direct child of &lt;properties> → Member (Annotation.Property) when declared
        • Nested under a property element → Value facet (Annotation.Value) — same property identity,
          not member scad:myProp
        Never means a renamed member QName.
        
        Parent context for *:example:
        • Prefixed child of &lt;examples> → Annotation.Example (sibling of canonical &lt;example>)
        • Unprefixed &lt;example> → StructuralDefinition.Example
        Prefer sibling *:example for 1:1 BML↔target pairs; use *:examples for independent target-only
        sets. Annotation facets never replace required canonical documentation / value / examples slots.
        
        Property value facets multiply PropertyDefinition.value along the Annotation namespace axis
        ("" | Annotation FQN). Cross-namespace faceting is valid; same-namespace self-faceting is
        forbidden. Duplicate namespace on one property → BML_DUPLICATE_PROPERTY_VALUE. Facets do not
        satisfy or replace the canonical &lt;value> for BML instance semantics unless a renderer
        explicitly prefers an Annotation facet.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:core.Annotation" relation="Defines">
          Annotation branch — Directive taxonomy and preferredAlias.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:annotation.BmlAnnotation" relation="SeeAlso">
          Concrete language Annotation (preferredAlias bml).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.PropertyDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Value-field Annotation facets on properties.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BmlNamespace" relation="SeeAlso">
          Package vs Annotation xmlns targets and domain.bml.* layout.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BmlDirectives" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — bml: language directive placement and semantics.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_DUPLICATE_PROPERTY_VALUE" relation="SeeAlso">
          Duplicate value facet on the same Annotation namespace.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.BlockAndInstance-->
    <block>
      <name>
        BlockAndInstance
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.BlockAndInstance
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to Block vs instance
      </is>
      <documentation>
        The closest familiar model: a Block is a declarative semantic Class; a Block Instance is an
        Object. This is a semantic analogy — not a mandate for runtime OOP. The compiler may represent
        definitions and instances as plain data; the parser nonetheless distinguishes block documents
        (classes) from composition children (objects).
        
        Block (definition):
        • .bml file · BlockDefinition
        • FQN · members · composition schema
        • Describes shape and behaviour
        • No runtime · no address
        
        Block Instance (in composition):
        • Element such as ac:Wall with bml:id and member values
        • typeFqn + bml:id + member values
        • One concrete occurrence
        • May become a Thing in Runtime (WOT vocabulary)
        
        Composition:
        • Aggregations hold Block Instances as children
        • Instance identity uses bml:id (not the type FQN)
        • Nested paths compose with :: (wall::bottom::inner)
        • bml:version pins external references to exact library versions
        
        Instance serialization (authoring) — definition fields are not instance wrappers:
        • On Block definitions — Property default uses the value field (&lt;value> preferred);
          Aggregation default uses &lt;composition> nested inside the aggregation member
        • On Block instances — NEVER wrap a Property in &lt;value> and NEVER wrap Aggregation
          children in &lt;composition>. The Property is a Block attribute or the Property element's
          direct content (myProperty="…" or &lt;myProperty>…&lt;/myProperty>). Aggregation children
          sit directly inside the aggregation element (or bml:container for shared directives)
        • Exception: a member whose name is value (PrimitiveEntity, TypeReferenceEntity, …) uses
          value="…" as ordinary instance syntax for that member — not as a PropertyDefinition wrapper
        • readOnly effective members must not be authored on instances → BML_READONLY_MEMBER_WRITE
        • Empty child meaning depends on aggregation type (instance vs TypeReferenceEntity sugar) —
          doc:MemberAuthoring / doc:CompositionModel / doc:SubBlockModel
        
        Context-dependent interpretation:
        • ValueTypes as type-classes for Property values and Capability input/output
        • Languages as meta-classes for implementation contracts
        • Sub-Blocks in the same .bml file as nested classes (derived types, inner scope)
        • Presets as named default bindings on a class
        
        Relationships: Library contains Blocks; Block has Members, Presets, Compositions; Catalog
        contains top-level composite Blocks.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberAuthoring" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Definition vs instance member syntax — no &lt;value> / &lt;composition> wrappers on
          instances.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompositionModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Aggregation fill — definition composition vs instance children.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.PartialMemberOverrides" relation="SeeAlso">
          readOnly — instance authoring forbidden.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Block" relation="Extends">
          Root type — abstract definition; not used directly as composition instance type.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Meta-model for block definition documents.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.DefinitionModel" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — how definitions describe types and support reflection.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.BlockNameConvention-->
    <block>
      <name>
        BlockNameConvention
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.BlockNameConvention
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to Block name formatting
      </is>
      <documentation>
        &lt;name> is a class name — PascalCase, letters and digits only:
        
        Format: [A-Z][a-zA-Z0-9]*
        Valid: Wall, StandardBrick, Calibrator, NfcPlayer
        Invalid: my-wall, wall_v2, 2Wall, My Wall
        Error: BML_INVALID_BLOCK_NAME
        
        PascalCase ensures composition instance tags with xmlns prefixes are unambiguous — no Property
        or member name starts with an uppercase letter (members use camelCase; visibility prefixes _ /
        __ do not change that rule for the logical name).
        
        Relationship to type identity (doc:TypeIdentityDerivation):
        • Root Blocks — type is canonical; name is optional and must equal lastSegment(resolve(type))
          when present (BML_BLOCK_NAME_TYPE_MISMATCH)
        • Sub-Blocks — name is required local identity; type is normally derived as
          parentType + "." + name
        • Visibility prefixes on Sub-Block names (_Utils, __Helper) are allowed; the PascalCase rule
          applies to the logical name after stripping visibility prefixes for collision checks — authored
          names still follow the visibility naming convention (doc:MemberVisibility /
          doc:SubBlockModel)
        
        Prefer distinctive Sub-Block names that differ from the enclosing root Block name to avoid
        ambiguous short tags (doc:SubBlockModel — naming vs parent).
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          name and type identity fields on block definition documents.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeIdentityDerivation" relation="SeeAlso">
          How name relates to canonical type on root and Sub-Blocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.SubBlockModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Sub-Block naming vs parent Block name.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.DocumentationModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Definition identity first — name / type / baseType before prose.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.PseudoCode" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — PseudoCode as semantic capability implementation dialect.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.BmlDirectives-->
    <block>
      <name>
        BmlDirectives
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.BmlDirectives
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to BML language directives
      </is>
      <documentation>
        BML Directives are attributes and elements in the bml: namespace, formalized by
        org.blockml.bml.annotation.BmlAnnotation (preferredAlias bml). Bind with
        xmlns:bml="org.blockml.bml.annotation.BmlAnnotation". Directives are not Block properties and not
        Definition members. Flow-control directives structure composition; serialization directives
        (bml:xml) declare how Definition blocks map to canonical BML XML.
        
        Flow-control follows the Thymeleaf model — attributes on the element they apply to. Whether a
        directive is pre-processed at compile time or translated into target constructs is the
        BlockProcessor's decision.
        
        Placement:
        • bml:id — Block instances; composition identity (literal or ${ expr }); required when
          referenced or on bml:each
        • bml:version — Block instances; external type version pin
        • bml:bean — Block instances; service bean name for @bean expressions
        • bml:scope — Block instances; bean visibility "local" (default) or "context"
        • bml:with — composition, bml:container, Block instances; local variable scope
        • bml:each — bml:container, Block instances; loop
        • bml:if / bml:unless — bml:container, Block instances; conditional include / exclude
        • &lt;bml:xml>…&lt;/bml:xml> — core:Block and Definition-branch blocks; root XML element recipe
        • bml:xml="…" on members — Definition-branch member serialization shape
        Bare id= / version= on instances are ordinary member values — use bml:id / bml:version for
        composition meta. Do not bind xmlns:bml to a Package or use bml: as a member Annotation prefix.
        
        bml:each — expands the element once per iteration (numeric or collection). Forms:
        i : ${ n } · i, stat : ${ n } · item, stat : ${ collection }. Stat fields: index, count, size,
        first, last, even, odd (0-based index/even/odd). For numeric loops i equals stat.index.
        
        bml:if / bml:unless — include when expression is true / false respectively.
        
        bml:container — virtual element; produces no Block output; groups children for shared directives
        (each, if, with).
        
        bml:with — defines local variables on the element and descendants. Comma-separated assignments;
        evaluated left-to-right; later variables may reference earlier ones; shadows outer names of the
        same name; may reference Properties of the current Block and override Properties within local
        scope. Primary mechanism for sharing values between sibling composition children.
        
        Dynamic instance IDs: combine bml:each with bml:id="${ 'prefix_' + i }" (or equivalent).
        
        bml:xml (serialization — orthogonal to flow control):
        • Element form on core:Block / Definition branch: literal local name, ${ bml:Self }, or
          ${ #this.name } only — other recipes → BML_XML_RENDERING_INVALID
        • Ordinary composable / ValueType blocks must not redeclare block-level &lt;bml:xml> →
          BML_XML_DIRECTIVE_NOT_ALLOWED (Stage 2)
        • Member attribute form selects node kind (element | attribute | text | cdata, including
          element|attribute alternatives). May change on override="true"
        Shared XML grammar metadata for serializer, FatBlock, XSD, and similar targets — not a
        serializer-private detail. Meta-driven emit is WIP; Definition blocks already declare shapes.
        
        Catalogue of directive Sub-Blocks: ann:BmlAnnotation.Id, Version, Bean, Scope, Status, Each, If,
        Unless, With, Xml, Container. Composition usage: doc:CompositionModel.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:annotation.BmlAnnotation" relation="Defines">
          Concrete Annotation — directive catalogue and preferredAlias bml.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.AnnotationModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Annotation branch — facets, match rules, Package vs Annotation.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Annotation" relation="Extends">
          Annotation branch — Package vs Annotation xmlns rule.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompositionModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Where composition and bml:container host flow-control directives.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.Expressions" relation="SeeAlso">
          Expression language inside directive attributes and member values.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.MemberDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Member-level bml:xml on Definition declarations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.DefinitionModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Definition branch that carries bml:xml serialization metadata.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeIdentityDerivation" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — type references and Block identity derivation.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.BmlNamespace-->
    <block>
      <name>
        BmlNamespace
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.BmlNamespace
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to the BML namespace
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Every BML definition — Blocks, Libraries, Types, Languages, Services, Capabilities, and all
        other BlockML definitions — must reside below the owning domain's bml package.
        
        General form:
        • &lt;domain>.bml.*
        
        Examples:
        • org.blockml.bml.*
        • com.machineblocks.bml.*
        • com.example.bml.*
        
        No BML Block, Library, or other BlockML definition may exist outside the domain's bml
        namespace (BML_OUTSIDE_BML_NAMESPACE). Physical source layout mirrors the FQN:
        blocks/org/blockml/bml/core/Block.bml defines org.blockml.bml.core.Block.
        
        Typical BlockML branches (all under org.blockml.bml.*):
        • org.blockml.bml.core.* — framework base types (Block, Annotation, ValueType, Status, Library, …)
        • org.blockml.bml.annotation.* — concrete Annotations (BmlAnnotation)
        • org.blockml.bml.type.* — bootstrap ValueTypes
        • org.blockml.bml.service.* — built-in expression services (#math, #strings, …)
        • org.blockml.bml.language.* — implementation language types
        • org.blockml.bml.capability.* — Capability type branches
        • org.blockml.bml.definition.* — Definition meta-model (orthogonal to Annotations)
        • org.blockml.bml.documentation.* — bootstrap documentation blocks
        • org.blockml.bml.status.* / constraint.* / validation.* / relation.* / unit.* — matching branches
        
        xmlns prefixes are BML-semantic only when they target a Package or an Annotation:
        • Package — type addressing (xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core")
        • Annotation — directives (xmlns:bml="org.blockml.bml.annotation.BmlAnnotation")
        Other URI bindings (schemaLocation keys) are not BML-semantic type namespaces. Undeclared
        prefix → BML_UNKNOWN_NAMESPACE_PREFIX; non-Package/Annotation target →
        BML_INVALID_NAMESPACE_TARGET; colliding prefixes across a bundle →
        BML_NAMESPACE_PREFIX_COLLISION.
        
        XML prefix convention (required authoring practice — BOM stores FQNs; compiler does not
        rewrite aliases). Rule of thumb: a bare short prefix always means BlockML. Domain mirrors of
        the same segment use a domain token in front (MachineBlocks → mb):
        • core → org.blockml.bml.core · type → org.blockml.bml.type · unit → org.blockml.bml.unit
        • lang → org.blockml.bml.language · status / def / constraint / validation / … → matching
          org.blockml.bml.*
        • mbcore → com.machineblocks.bml.core · mbt → …type · mbunit → …unit
        • bricks / cal / scad — collective or annotation packages (descriptive OK when no collision)
        Do not steal framework aliases (xmlns:core="com.machineblocks.bml.core" is wrong). Do not use
        legacy bcore / btype. Other domains use their own short token (ac → acore / atype / aunit).
        
        Member and facet QNames: optional XML prefix on member element names supplies the member
        namespace (resolved via xmlns; unprefixed → default empty namespace). Prefixed member QNames
        that are Annotation Member directives (name="*") resolve to that Annotation — see
        doc:AnnotationModel. There is no namespace= attribute on members.
        
        Domain libraries follow the same &lt;domain>.bml.* rule
        (com.machineblocks.bml.core / bricks / type / annotation.*, …).
        
        npm workspace packages (@blockml/core, @blockml/parser, …) are deployment units — not
        identical to FQN namespaces. TypeScript toolchain code ships in package src/ directories;
        it is not a BML definition and does not receive a domain.bml FQN.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.AnnotationModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Annotation xmlns targets vs Package prefixes.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.LibrarySystem" relation="SeeAlso">
          Libraries, packages, and namespace membership — related reading.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ImplicitNamespaceResolution" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — context-bound implicit namespace for unqualified references.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap / ReadMe documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.BootstrapModel-->
    <block>
      <name>
        BootstrapModel
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.BootstrapModel
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to library bootstrap
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Self-hosting bootstrap: the compiler loads framework BML from @blockml/core (and domain
        packages) before user content — TypeRegistry bootstrap, not special-cased type semantics
        in code.
        
        Bootstrap order (summary):
        1. Core branches — Block, ValueType, Language, Status, Relation, Constraint, Capability, Definition
        2. Concrete types — Integer, Decimal, languages, services, constraints, relations
        3. BlockLibrary — library descriptor base (readMe, bootstrap, dependencies, subLibraries,
           schemas, blocks)
        4. org.blockml.bml.Library — published catalog via blocks; readMe → README
        5. Domain framework BML (e.g. com.machineblocks.bml.core.*)
        
        Two library entry roles (do not conflate):
        • org.blockml.bml.core.ReadMe — human/LLM reading entry. Required on every Library via
          readMe (exactly one TypeReferenceEntity(ReadMe)). org.blockml.bml.README is the standard
          core-library ReadMe.
        • org.blockml.bml.core.Bootstrap — optional automation SPOE (tasks, agents, CI). At most one
          per Library via bootstrap (0..1). Empty abstract branch; concrete subtypes supply process logic.
        
        Concrete Library.bml &lt;references> — at most two &lt;see> entries on the catalog
        descriptor (not on documentation blocks):
        1. Required relation=ReadFirst → the ReadMe named in readMe
        2. Optional relation=ThenContinueWith → the Bootstrap named in bootstrap (only when present)
        Signals: after reading, continue by executing the automation SPOE. No AuthoringGuide /
        DeepDive / Extends / SeeAlso on concrete Libraries.
        
        Curated reading order lives in &lt;references> on the ReadMe block — each entry uses
        relation=ReadFirst with a context note. Available tasks use relation=TaskIndex on the same
        references container. One mechanism for all cross-links; tooling filters by relation. No
        separate reading-order aggregation on ReadMe itself.
        
        Task-oriented paths:
        • org.blockml.bml.core.TaskGuide — procedural org.blockml.bml.task.* blocks
        • relation=TaskStep — ordered steps inside a TaskGuide (document order)
        • relation=Prerequisite — before starting a task
        • org.blockml.bml.core.Diagnostic — org.blockml.bml.diagnostic.* validator codes
        • relation=Resolves — Diagnostic → TaskGuide fix procedure
        
        Interpretation context:
        • Framework BML defines what types ARE — TypeRegistry SSOT
        • Renderers interpret what types MEAN for their target — free manifestation
        • README references with ReadFirst establish shared vocabulary before diving into definitions
        • After the first ten ReadFirst documentation blocks, most of the core library can be derived
          directly from Block definitions themselves
        
        Each registered BlockDefinition from TypeRegistry bootstrap is an immutable BomDocument parse
        result.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompilerArchitecture" relation="SeeAlso">
          TypeRegistry bootstrap and BML registration.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompilerArchitecture" relation="SeeAlso">
          CLI bootstrap — framework BML before user projects.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.BlockLibrary" relation="Defines">
          Library descriptor — readMe (ReadMe, 1..1) and bootstrap (Bootstrap, 0..1).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.ReadMe" relation="Defines">
          Abstract ReadMe role — required Library reading entry.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Bootstrap" relation="Defines">
          Abstract Bootstrap role — optional automation SPOE.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.ThenContinueWith" relation="SeeAlso">
          Library &lt;see> after ReadFirst when bootstrap is present.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:README" relation="SeeAlso">
          Standard human/LLM library ReadMe — ReadFirst references in mental learning order.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Documentation" relation="Defines">
          Base type for documentation blocks listed with relation=ReadFirst.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.ReadFirst" relation="Defines">
          Relation block for curated reading path entries on ReadMe references.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.TaskIndex" relation="Defines">
          Relation block for task catalog entries on ReadMe blocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TaskGuideModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          TaskGuide blocks, task relations, and diagnostic fix paths.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.Introduction" relation="ReadFirst">
          First ReadFirst entry on org.blockml.bml.README — BlockML goals and overview.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ModelingGuidelines" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — recommended patterns for modeling Blocks.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.CapabilitySemantics-->
    <block>
      <name>
        CapabilitySemantics
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.CapabilitySemantics
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to Capability meaning
      </is>
      <documentation>
        BlockML describes semantics only. There are no special language concepts for constructor,
        getter, setter, or render — all behaviour is expressed as ordinary Capabilities: init(),
        getValue(), setValue(), render(), and domain-specific operations. Package:
        org.blockml.bml.capability.* (base org.blockml.bml.core.Capability).
        
        Capability kinds describe intent — they are not compiler rules or runtime objects:
        • Inbound — receives input from outside (setValue, configure); requires inputType; no outputType
        • Outbound — produces output to outside (getValue, emit); requires outputType; no inputType
        • Transformation — maps input to output (compute, format, render); requires both
        Async reply via replyTo is a runtime/message pattern — not modeled on the Capability.
        
        Unqualified Capability type resolves to org.blockml.bml.capability.*; unqualified inputType /
        outputType resolve to org.blockml.bml.type.* (doc:ImplicitNamespaceResolution). General block
        references (aggregation type, association type, baseType) have no implicit namespace.
        
        Authoring: declare under &lt;capabilities> with a camelCase name (visibility prefixes apply —
        doc:MemberVisibility). In a Composition the Capability value is the address or binding —
        attribute on the child Block instance, like a Property value. Private Capabilities (__name)
        cannot be bound from outside the declaring Block; protected (_name) follow the formal access
        rule including derived Blocks and the direct composition owner.
        
        Each Capability may carry an implementation whose language attribute references a Language FQN
        (org.blockml.bml.language.*). utility Sub-Blocks (baseType Entity) commonly host Transformation
        implementations — doc:SubBlockModel. Preferred semantic dialect: PseudoCode
        (doc:PseudoCode). The renderer decides how to realise each Capability in the target system:
        
        init()     → Java constructor · UI5 onInit() · React useEffect()
        getValue() → direct field access · generated getter · reflection
        setValue() → direct field assignment · generated setter
        render()   → SCAD module · JSX component · may be ignored
        
        Interpretation happens only in the renderer — by reading Capability definitions and member
        values from the immutable BOM. The BOM never contains implementation concepts such as
        constructors, getters, setters, runtime values, or executable behaviour.
        
        A Block's public surface is Capabilities — internal structure (Properties, Aggregations)
        is not the outward API; the renderer decides concrete manifestation.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ImplicitNamespaceResolution" relation="SeeAlso">
          Unqualified Capability type vs inputType / outputType sites.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.SubBlockModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          utility Sub-Blocks hosting Transformation implementations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.PseudoCode" relation="SeeAlso">
          Preferred Capability implementation dialect for semantic prose.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Capability" relation="Extends">
          Capability branch — implementation member and input/output type rules.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:capability.Inbound" relation="Defines">
          Inbound capability kind — receives external input.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:capability.Outbound" relation="Defines">
          Outbound capability kind — produces external output.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:capability.Transformation" relation="Defines">
          Transformation capability kind — maps input to output.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:language.PseudoCode" relation="SeeAlso">
          Free-form pseudocode — preferred for semantic descriptions readable by LLMs.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.Expressions" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — dynamic values and bean calls in expressions.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.CompilerArchitecture-->
    <block>
      <name>
        CompilerArchitecture
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.CompilerArchitecture
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to the compiler pipeline
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Overview only — normative Urmodell / Pragmatic / BomBuilder rules live in docs/01_bom_core.md;
        pipeline stages and packages in docs/06_compiler.md; system layout in docs/00_system.md.
        
        Sketch: BML → Parser → Urmodell (immutable Canonical BOM) → Validator pragmatic fork /
        Framework Utilities / Render Target pipeline → target output. Serializer reads the Urmodell only.
        Validation Stages 1–3 (Parser / Validator / Renderer) — glossary in 06_compiler.md.
        Self-hosting: CLI bootstraps TypeRegistry from framework BML before user projects.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.RendererFreedom" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — renderer decides manifestation, not BlockML.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.CompositionModel-->
    <block>
      <name>
        CompositionModel
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.CompositionModel
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to composition and aggregation fill
      </is>
      <documentation>
        A Composition fills an Aggregation with concrete child Block instances. On Block definitions,
        &lt;composition> is nested inside the aggregation member. On Block instances, aggregations are
        filled directly by member name — no &lt;composition> wrapper. Do not treat composition as a
        general-purpose container; the parallel Property mistake is wrapping instance values in
        &lt;value> — both are definition fields, not instance wrappers (doc:MemberAuthoring). The
        composition aggregation="…" attribute is removed.
        
        Unified container concept — one idea, two forms:
        • composition — initial fill of an aggregation inside a Block definition only
        • bml:container — general virtual container everywhere else (Block instances)
        Both support the same flow-control directives: bml:if, bml:each, bml:with (and future container
        directives). See doc:BmlDirectives.
        
        Placement rules:
        • &lt;composition> must always be nested inside an aggregation member — never standalone →
          BML_STANDALONE_COMPOSITION
        • New aggregation declaration → type required
        • Inherited aggregation override → override="true" required; type may be omitted (identical
          restated type → BML_REDUNDANT_AGGREGATION_TYPE)
        • Block instances open aggregations directly — recursive nested structure is first-class
        
        Child elements inside composition (or instance aggregation content) are Block instances:
        • Tag name — Block type (FQN / xmlns QName)
        • bml:id — composition instance identity; required when referenced or on bml:each (literal or
          Expression). Omitted on anonymous static children → auto-ID after directive expansion
        • bml:version — optional exact-version assert on external type references; omitted → TypeRegistry
          / library context; no match → BML_UNKNOWN_VERSION. Same-document Sub-Block refs omit version
        • bml:bean / bml:scope — optional service bean registration (local | context; default local)
        • Member attributes / child elements — property and nested aggregation values (literals or
          ${ … } expressions)
        
        Instance ID reachability is controlled by the aggregation member's visibility — not by flags on
        composition or bml:container. Public aggregations export ids for cross-document
        {EnclosingBlockFQN}::{id}; protected and private narrow reach as in doc:MemberVisibility.
        Qualified cross-document references require a public target aggregation and a literal static
        bml:id.
        
        Recursive composition: every Block instance may contain aggregation members directly — complex
        object structures nest wherever instances appear.
        
        Sibling value sharing: use bml:with on a common ancestor (composition or bml:container). Variables
        are visible to the element and all descendants — no separate registry. Full expression grammar
        lives in doc:Expressions; V1 implements property references, #built-in calls, and bml: directives
        (including bml:with). @bean / @model: / capability linking are specified but not V1-implemented.
        
        Meta-model: AggregationDefinition.composition (Sub-Block Composition) maps to BOM
        CompositionTemplate. Service binding uses bml:bean and bml:scope on instances — not aggregation
        member fields.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:definition.AggregationDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Aggregation member — composition property and default flag.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.AggregationDefinition" property="composition" relation="Defines">
          Nested composition template on aggregation declarations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:annotation.BmlAnnotation" relation="SeeAlso">
          Language directives used on composition and instances.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberVisibility" relation="SeeAlso">
          Aggregation visibility controls bml:id reachability.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.PartialMemberOverrides" relation="SeeAlso">
          Inherited aggregation overrides — omit unchanged type.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberAuthoring" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Definition vs instance — composition and value are declaration fields only.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BlockAndInstance" relation="SeeAlso">
          Definition vs instance mental model.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.Expressions" relation="SeeAlso">
          Expression language used in composition member values.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.AnnotationModel" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — Annotation namespaces and facets (then bml: directives).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BmlDirectives" relation="SeeAlso">
          bml:each, bml:if, bml:container, bml:with, bml:xml on composition hosts.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.ConstantValueType-->
    <block>
      <name>
        ConstantValueType
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.ConstantValueType
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to Constant singleton types
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Constant provides singleton (constant) value domains in type expressions without a dedicated
        literal-type language feature. Branch: ConstantType. Standard implementation:
        org.blockml.bml.type.Constant.
        
        Surface syntax — Constant(T){value: …}:
        • (…) binds generic T to any ValueType (mandatory — bare Constant or Constant{value: …}
          without (T) is invalid)
        • {value: …} configures the single allowed literal on the internal ValueType instance
        
        External representation equals the representation of T — Constant(Text){value: "auto"} accepts
        the external value "auto", not { value: "auto" }. Validation: first against bound type T, then
        equality with the configured constant. No type inference — T is always authored explicitly.
        
        Primary use — union alternatives:
        Boolean | Constant(Text){value: "auto"}
        Float | Constant(Text){value: "auto"}
        
        Future literal shorthand Boolean | "auto" desugars to the Constant form; until enabled, author
        Constant explicitly. Legacy literal unions remain parseable; normalize to Constant at map time.
        
        Boolean.True / Boolean.False — Sub-Blocks of org.blockml.bml.type.Boolean; prefer these shortcuts
        over spelling Constant(Boolean){value: true/false} in type expressions.
        
        Obsolete wrappers migrate to Constant unions (doc:TypeExpressions): AutoOr&lt;T> →
        Constant(Text){value: "auto"} | T, FalseOr&lt;T> → Boolean.False | T, etc.
        
        Orthogonal mechanisms on one reference: Constant(SizeXY){value: {x: 1, y: 2}} — generic bind +
        instance configuration (doc:ValueTypeConfiguration). value is required (ValuePresent).
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:type.Constant" relation="Defines">
          Standard Constant(T){value: …} implementation.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.ConstantType" relation="Extends">
          ConstantType branch — singleton value domain semantics.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.Boolean" relation="SeeAlso">
          Boolean.True / Boolean.False shortcuts.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeExpressions" relation="SeeAlso">
          Unions, match order, obsolete wrapper migration.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ValueTypeConfiguration" relation="SeeAlso">
          {value: …} instance configuration rules.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.FunctionMappingModel" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — positional/named mapping sharing declaration-order rules.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.ConstraintsModel-->
    <block>
      <name>
        ConstraintsModel
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.ConstraintsModel
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to constraints and suggestions
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Constraints are reusable validation rules attached via a single &lt;constraints> container on
        ConstrainableDefinition elements (Blocks and members). There is no target= property — the
        embedding context binds the constraint automatically. Constraint branches declare genericTypes;
        the compiler binds T from context (never authored):
        • ValueConstraint on Property → T = effective ValueType
        • PropertyConstraint → T = PropertyDefinition
        • AggregationConstraint / AssociationConstraint / CapabilityConstraint → corresponding Definition
        • BlockConstraint → BlockDefinition (incl. Sub-/Root-)
        Wrong branch for context → BML_CONSTRAINT_TARGET_MISMATCH. Unknown constraint type →
        BML_CONSTRAINT_UNKNOWN. Generic T incompatible → BML_CONSTRAINT_TYPE_MISMATCH. Instance property
        incompatible with T → BML_CONSTRAINT_VALUE_MISMATCH.
        
        Validation property (optional) references org.blockml.bml.validation.*:
        • Error → BML_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION (fail compile)
        • Warning → BML_CONSTRAINT_WARNING
        • Information → BML_CONSTRAINT_INFORMATION
        Omitted validation → constraint is metadata only (not value-enforced by the BlockML validator).
        
        Standard value constraints include Min, Max, MinLength, MaxLength, MinItems, MaxItems, Pattern,
        ValuePresent. Structural examples: RequiresMember, AllowedChildType, NoSelfReference,
        MustBeReadOnly, RequiresImplementation. Prefer ValueType instance configuration for
        type-intrinsic thresholds (Text{maxLength: 100}); keep Constraints for contextual/composable
        rules (doc:ValueTypeConfiguration).
        
        Suggestions — Property-only metadata: Block instances compatible with the property type for
        editors/renderers. Not validation. Forbidden on EnumType properties → BML_SUGGESTIONS_ON_ENUM.
        Incompatible suggestion instance → BML_SUGGESTION_TYPE_MISMATCH (PrimitiveType properties use
        matching *Entity wrappers).
        
        Procedure: task:AddConstraint.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:definition.ConstrainableDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Shared constraints container on Blocks and members.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Constraint" relation="Extends">
          Constraint branch root.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Validation" relation="Defines">
          Error / Warning / Information enforcement markers.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:constraint.Min" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example scalar constraint.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:constraint.Pattern" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example textual constraint.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.PropertyDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          suggestions aggregation and property constraints.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ValueTypeConfiguration" relation="SeeAlso">
          Prefer inline configuration for type-intrinsic thresholds.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.GenericsModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Constraint generic binding from embedding context.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ModelingGuidelines" relation="SeeAlso">
          When to use constraints in modeling.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:task.AddConstraint" relation="SeeAlso">
          Procedural how-to for attaching constraints.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_CONSTRAINT_TARGET_MISMATCH" relation="SeeAlso">
          Branch ↔ context incompatibility.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_SUGGESTIONS_ON_ENUM" relation="SeeAlso">
          Suggestions forbidden on EnumType properties.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberVisibility" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — member and Sub-Block visibility (README learning order).
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.ContractsModel-->
    <block>
      <name>
        ContractsModel
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.ContractsModel
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to contracts and type bounds
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Blocks may declare &lt;contracts> at root level to compose one or more contract Blocks —
        reusable partial definitions whose member fragments merge into the effective Block definition.
        Declared only on the document root (not on nested Sub-Blocks). Child elements are direct Block
        references (TypeReferenceEntity sugar — empty element is not an instance of the named Block).
        
        baseType vs contracts:
        • baseType — framework taxonomy and branch detection (extends); single inheritance; inBranch()
          uses the baseType chain only
        • contracts — domain cross-cutting member mixins and type-bound identifiers (implements) via
          satisfiesTypeBound
        Framework core branch types (ValueType, Entity, Language, Constraint, …) remain baseType markers
        only — not duplicated as interface contracts in org.blockml.bml.*.
        
        Semantics:
        • Role — reusable partial Block definition (replaces interfaces / traits / mixins) plus type-bound
          identifier
        • Scope — Properties, Aggregations, Associations, Capabilities, Constraints, Suggestions,
          documentation, examples, references, optional Capability defaults
        • Ignored on contract types — subBlocks, embeddedBlocks, and compositions
        • Partial members — contracts may declare incomplete members; the concrete Block completes them
          with override="true" (same rule as baseType refinement) → omission BML_OVERRIDE_REQUIRED
        • Merge order — baseType chain → contracts (declaration order) → local Block declarations
        • Conflict — later contract wins; Block overrides all contracts
        • Override — only explicitly specified attributes replace merged values; final members from any
          layer cannot be overridden → BML_FINAL_MEMBER_OVERRIDE
        • Validation — resolveEffectiveMembers() and satisfiesTypeBound() at Stage 2
        
        Type bounds — satisfiesTypeBound(T, G) is true when G is on T's baseType chain OR G appears
        directly in T.contracts[] (V1: no transitive closure through a contract's own contracts[]).
        Used for generic bounds and polymorphic reference checks. Branch rules (inBranch) ignore
        contracts.
        
        Authoring tip: keep contract Blocks abstract with focused member fragments; complete defaults,
        constraints, and documentation on the concrete Block with override="true". See
        doc:PartialMemberOverrides for merge details and doc:ModelingGuidelines for when to prefer
        baseType vs contracts.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          contracts aggregation — TypeReferenceEntity(core:Block)[]; root only.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity" relation="Defines">
          Empty-child sugar for contract references.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ModelingGuidelines" relation="SeeAlso">
          When to use baseType vs contracts in modeling.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.PartialMemberOverrides" relation="SeeAlso">
          override="true" required for contract member completion.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeSystemOverview" relation="SeeAlso">
          baseType vs contracts in the type system overview.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BlockNameConvention" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — PascalCase Block name rules.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.CoreUseCases-->
    <block>
      <name>
        CoreUseCases
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.CoreUseCases
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to the three core BlockML use cases
      </is>
      <summary>
        The three core use cases BlockML is designed around — structured project documentation,
            general-purpose domain modeling, and AI workspaces — all built on one principle: information is
            represented as a structured semantic model instead of unstructured text.
      </summary>
      <documentation>
        BlockML is designed around three core use cases. They build on the same principle:
        **information is represented as a structured, semantic model instead of unstructured text or
        implementation-specific data.**
        
        ## 1. Structured Project Documentation
        
        BlockML can be used as a structured alternative to Markdown for project documentation.
        
        Traditional documentation is primarily written for humans. Markdown improves readability and
        portability, but its structure remains largely implicit. BlockML makes that structure explicit
        while preserving human-readable content such as Markdown inside documentation blocks.
        
        A BlockML document can therefore serve simultaneously as:
        
        • documentation for humans,
        • context for AI systems,
        • structured input for software tools,
        • and a machine-processable source for generated documentation.
        
        Because BlockML has a defined object model and can be processed by parsers, validators,
        compilers, and renderers, documentation is no longer merely text attached to a project. It
        becomes part of the project's structured model.
        
            BML Documentation
                  │
                  ├──→ Human / AI
                  ├──→ Markdown
                  ├──→ HTML
                  ├──→ BlockDoc
                  └──→ other representations
        
        The important distinction is that the generated documentation is not the source of truth.
        **The BlockML model is.**
        
        ## 2. General-Purpose Domain Modeling
        
        BlockML can describe arbitrary domains.
        
        A domain defines its vocabulary as Blocks, types, properties, relationships, capabilities,
        constraints, and compositions. Concrete models can then be expressed using that vocabulary.
        
        BlockML itself does not prescribe what these models represent. They may describe software
        systems, infrastructure, IAM configurations, physical objects, user interfaces, business
        processes, documentation structures, or entirely new domains.
        
        Renderers interpret these semantic models for a particular target. The same BlockML model may
        therefore have multiple manifestations.
        
            Domain Model
                 │
                 ▼
                BML
                 │
                 ├──→ Renderer A → Code
                 ├──→ Renderer B → Configuration
                 ├──→ Renderer C → Documentation
                 ├──→ Renderer D → UI
                 └──→ Renderer E → Domain-specific output
        
        The renderer determines the output format and interpretation; the model remains independent of
        any particular target. This separation is fundamental to BlockML's renderer architecture.
        
        **BlockML describes what something is. Renderers decide how that description manifests in a
        particular environment.**
        
        ## 3. AI Workspaces
        
        BlockML can serve as the persistent model of an AI workspace.
        
        Instead of giving an AI a collection of documents and repeatedly asking it to produce new text,
        the AI works directly on a structured BlockML model.
        
        The model represents the current state of the work. The AI can inspect it, validate it, extend
        it, and transform it while preserving explicit structure and semantics.
        
        This makes it possible to represent not only the final result, but also intermediate states of a
        process:
        
            Initial Model
                 │
                 ▼
              Analysis
                 │
                 ▼
            Planning Model
                 │
                 ▼
            Transformation
                 │
                 ▼
            Refined Model
                 │
                 ▼
             Validation
                 │
                 ▼
             Final Model
                 │
                 ├──→ Renderer
                 ├──→ Compiler
                 └──→ further AI transformation
        
        Stages, tasks, decisions, constraints, intermediate results, and final artifacts can all become
        explicit parts of the workspace rather than disappearing into prompts and conversation history.
        
        The AI is therefore no longer primarily generating isolated outputs. **It is operating on and
        evolving a persistent model.**
        
        This turns BlockML from a representation format into a foundation for model-driven AI workflows:
        
        > **The model is the workspace. The AI works on the model. Renderers manifest its current
        > state.**
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.Introduction" relation="Prerequisite">
          BlockML goals, BML vs BOM, and the domain-agnostic compiler — read before the use cases.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Documentation" relation="Extends">
          Documentation as Blocks — the mechanism behind use case 1 (structured project documentation).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:language.Markdown" relation="SeeAlso">
          Markdown as documentation content language — human-readable prose inside a structured model.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.DocumentationModel" relation="DeepDive">
          Block documentation model — is, summary, documentation, references, examples.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.RendererFreedom" relation="DeepDive">
          Renderer freedom — the target decides how a model manifests (use case 2).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompilerArchitecture" relation="SeeAlso">
          Parser, validator, renderer pipeline that processes the model in all three use cases.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BootstrapModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Reading entry and context bundles — how humans and AI enter a BlockML model (use case 3).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.WOTFoundation" relation="SeeAlso">
          Optional background — Idea, Manifestation, and World behind the model/renderer split.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.EverythingIsABlock" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — the central ontological principle these use cases rest on.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.DefinitionModel-->
    <block>
      <name>
        DefinitionModel
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.DefinitionModel
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to the Definition branch
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Block definitions describe types. The Definition branch (org.blockml.bml.core.Definition) is the
        meta-model for describing block structure in BlockML's own language — definitions are normal
        Blocks that inherit from org.blockml.bml.core.Block like everything else.
        
        Definition branch hierarchy:
        • org.blockml.bml.core.Definition — abstract base of the Definition branch
        • org.blockml.bml.definition.StructuralDefinition — shared documentation model for structural definitions
        • org.blockml.bml.definition.BlockDefinition — shape of every block document
        • org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition — abstract base for block member declarations;
          override and boolean implementation; CapabilityDefinition overrides implementation
        • AssociationDefinition — instance-to-instance model links (not documentation References)
        • PropertyDefinition, AggregationDefinition, CapabilityDefinition
        • GenericTypeDefinition
        
        org.blockml.bml.definition.StructuralDefinition declares:
        • Properties: documentation, since, status, abstract, final
        • Aggregations: changelog, examples, references
          (References connect types to types or types to instances for documentation —
          distinct from Associations)
        
        Definition addressing uses canonical structural paths within the Block FQN:
        com.foo.Bar.properties.width → PropertyDefinition
        com.foo.Bar.capabilities.render → CapabilityDefinition
        com.foo.Bar.references → Reference entries
        
        Reflection resolves these paths via structural containers at query time — no special
        definition-reference syntax. Definition blocks may declare bml:xml for how instances
        serialize to canonical BML XML.
        
        Definitions as Blocks means the same parser, TypeRegistry, and validation rules apply.
        Domain-specific definition types can extend the branch with additional Capabilities
        (validateDefinition(), generateDocumentation()) without core changes.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BmlDirectives" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          bml:xml self-description rules.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.DocumentationModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          is, documentation, references, examples.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Definition" relation="Extends">
          Abstract base of the Definition branch.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Shared documentation model; sub-blocks Documentation, Change, Example, and Reference.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Generic meta-model for block definition documents.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.MemberDefinition" relation="Extends">
          Abstract base for property, aggregation, association, and capability declarations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.PropertyDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Meta-model member shape for property declarations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Block" relation="Extends">
          Sole inheritance root — definitions inherit like all other Blocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeSystemOverview" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — ValueType kinds and type expressions.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.DocumentationModel-->
    <block>
      <name>
        DocumentationModel
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.DocumentationModel
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to Block documentation
      </is>
      <documentation>
        <![CDATA[
        Every root block definition carries a documentation model at the definition site — single
        source of truth inside BML files:
        
        Definition identity first (required at top of every Block document):
        name / type / baseType — FQN identity before prose (baseType omitted only on org.blockml.bml.core.Block).
        
        is (always required, after identity) — names the Idea (Urbild): what this Block semantically
          represents. Noun phrase; several words when name/type alone is too thin — not purpose and not
          full technical detail. Urbild is a fixed BlockML term (keep German explicit in meta-prose):
          English/French gloss + (Urbild); German docs may use Urbild alone. WOT: the Block itself is
          the Idea (Urbild); <is> names it in prose. Reflection: bml:Self.is. Omitted →
          BML_MISSING_IS. Motto: as simple as Lego at the designation; as powerful as SAP beneath.
        summary (required when documentation is too long) — short prose expansion between is and
          documentation; inherited from StructuralDefinition on blocks and members. Missing when
          required → BML_MISSING_SUMMARY.
        keywords aggregation (optional) — core.Keyword[]; domain FixedKeyword catalogue blocks, meta.FreeTextKeyword; shorthand → FreeTextKeyword only.
        language (optional Definition meta — def:BlockDefinition.language) — natural language for
          documentation prose; inherits enclosing Library document language, then the English default
          authored on org.blockml.bml.core.Block.
        documentation (always required on every Block definition) — technical description of the Block
          definition itself; Markdown(English) by default, with an optional language attribute
          (Markdown(NaturalLanguage) or a bare NaturalLanguage) when prose deviates from the effective
          block language. Omitted → BML_MISSING_DOCUMENTATION.
        isNot (required when confusion risk exists, blocks only) — what this Block is not; block-level
          disambiguation. Reflection: bml:Self.isNot. Missing when required → BML_MISSING_IS_NOT.
        aliases aggregation (optional, blocks only) — core.Alias[]; domain FixedAlias catalogue blocks, meta.FreeTextAlias; shorthand → FreeTextAlias only.
        Member elements carry their own summary, keywords, and documentation for individual Properties, Aggregations, etc.
        
        Optional extensions (wherever documentation is permitted):
        since — version from which the element is available
        changelog — container of change entries (oldest first)
        examples — container of example entries (title in block natural language + CDATA body)
        references — container of see entries (type, instance, file, url, or member path + relationship text)
        
        Natural language inheritance (documentation prose and example titles) — Definition meta only,
        not a core:Block / BlockLibrary member:
        Library document <language> → Block document <language> → documentation / change / see
        language attribute. Unqualified names inside <language> and on documentation, change, and
        see language= resolve via org.blockml.bml.language.*. On documentation the natural language is
        either the generic argument of Markdown (Markdown(German)) or the bare shorthand (German); on
        change and see language= it is always the NaturalLanguage itself, since those bodies carry no
        markup. Authors declare language only when deviating from the effective inherited language. Example title language always follows the
        enclosing block — example language attribute applies to CDATA content only (implementation
        dialect: OpenSCAD, TypeScript, …) and also resolves via org.blockml.bml.language.*.
        
        ## Documentation, Summary, Markdown and CDATA
        
        `summary` and `documentation` serve different purposes and therefore use different content
        models:
        
            summary         → plain text
            documentation   → Markdown(English) by default
        
        ### Summary
        
        `summary` is a short, presentation-independent description for contexts such as lists, search
        results, explorers, tooltips, and IDE tooling. It is **plain text** and does not support
        Markdown:
        
            <summary>A generic block for describing a reusable component.</summary>
        
        ### Documentation
        
        `documentation` is rich textual content and uses Markdown by default. The default is declared by
        the documentation definition itself as `Markdown(English)`
        (def:StructuralDefinition.Documentation.language), so authors normally **do not** write the
        `language` attribute:
        
            <documentation>
            A **Block** is the fundamental unit of BlockML.
            </documentation>
        
        is semantically equivalent to:
        
            <documentation language="Markdown(English)">
            A **Block** is the fundamental unit of BlockML.
            </documentation>
        
        The explicit `language` attribute is only necessary when overriding the default. It is bound to
        `Markdown(NaturalLanguage) | NaturalLanguage`: `Markdown` forwards `MarkupLanguage.L`
        (NaturalLanguage, default English), so the natural language travels as the generic argument —
        `language="Markdown(German)"` for German prose, `language="Markdown"` being the same as
        `Markdown(English)`. A bare natural language stays valid as shorthand: `language="German"` means
        `Markdown(German)`. Both forms denote the same i18n entry, so do not author them side by side on
        one definition element.
        
        ### CDATA usage
        
        Markdown itself does **not** require CDATA. CDATA is purely an XML serialization mechanism and
        has no semantic meaning for documentation. The serialization rule is:
        
            no XML escaping required   → ordinary XML text
            XML escaping required      → prefer CDATA over entity escaping
        
        Documentation should be ordinary XML text when nothing needs escaping. When the content contains
        characters that would require escaping — especially `<` and `&` — the body is wrapped in a
        `<![CDATA[` … section, because that keeps the authored Markdown readable. Preferred payload:
        
            Returns `true` when `value < maximum && enabled`.
        
        Avoid entity escaping when CDATA can preserve the original content verbatim — the same payload
        written as `value &lt; maximum &amp;&amp; enabled` is the form to avoid. This block itself is
        serialized as CDATA for exactly that reason.
        
        ### CDATA terminators inside CDATA
        
        XML CDATA sections cannot be nested: the sequence `]]` followed by `>` terminates the current
        section. This occurs when Markdown documentation contains XML or BML examples that themselves
        contain CDATA. A serializer must therefore split the CDATA section around such a sequence so
        that the original text is preserved losslessly.
        
        This is purely a serialization concern. Ordinary XML text, a single CDATA section, and split
        CDATA sections all represent the same documentation content after parsing.
        
        **Principle:** `Markdown(English)` is the default semantics of `documentation`; CDATA is only an
        XML representation detail, used when necessary to keep BML readable.
        
        ### Human-friendly formatting
        
        Block documentation is Markdown and should be written as such — in particular the main
        documentation of a Block, and in particular for longer texts. Use paragraphs, headings, lists,
        and inline code to make the structure visible. Long block documentation without paragraph breaks
        is to be avoided; readable formatting for humans and LLMs is a central part of BlockML, not
        decoration. `summary` stays a single plain-text paragraph — when documentation grows long enough
        to need structure, that is exactly when summary becomes required.
        
        Authoring split:
        • documentation carries technical prose only
        • references carries all documentation cross-links — types to types or types to instances
          (also Definition members, repository markdown files, and external URLs) — each with an
          explicit relationship note
        • Associations connect instances to instances (model links); References do not form the
          instance graph — use associations for that
        • Do not repeat cross-links in documentation prose
        
        see target attributes — exactly one target kind:
        type="core:ValueType" — Block or Definition type via xmlns prefix (TypeReference)
        instance="myId" / instance="foo:Bar::myId" — Block instance pointer (InstanceReference)
        file="guide.md" — optional companion markdown in the consuming repository (not Core SSOT)
        url="https://…" — external URL
        property="com.foo.Bar.properties.width" — PropertyDefinition canonical path
        aggregation="com.foo.Bar.aggregations.parts" — AggregationDefinition canonical path
        association="com.foo.Bar.associations.parent" — AssociationDefinition canonical path
        capability="com.foo.Bar.capabilities.render" — CapabilityDefinition canonical path
        Split forms (still one target): type="com.foo.Bar" property="width" →
        com.foo.Bar.properties.width in BOM; type="com.foo.Bar" instance="myId" → com.foo.Bar::myId.
        (Retired attribute name: block= — use type=.)
        
        relation attribute (optional) — Block reference to org.blockml.bml.relation.*; unqualified
        names resolve via implicit namespace org.blockml.bml.relation.*. Omitted =
        org.blockml.bml.relation.SeeAlso. Standard blocks: SeeAlso, ReadFirst, ReadNext,
        ThenContinueWith, Extends, Requires, ContrastsWith, AuthoringGuide, Defines, DeepDive,
        TaskIndex, TaskStep, Prerequisite, Resolves. README curates with ReadFirst; documentation
        blocks chain with ReadNext.
        
        README / ReadMe blocks (baseType org.blockml.bml.core.ReadMe) are library reading entry points
        for humans and LLMs (Library.readMe). Curated reading order is expressed via references with
        relation=ReadFirst; available tasks via relation=TaskIndex — not via separate aggregations on
        ReadMe. Documentation blocks (org.blockml.bml.documentation.*) inherit from
        org.blockml.bml.core.Documentation. TaskGuide blocks (org.blockml.bml.task.*) inherit from
        org.blockml.bml.core.TaskGuide. Diagnostic blocks (org.blockml.bml.diagnostic.*) inherit from
        org.blockml.bml.core.Diagnostic.
        ]]>
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.DefinitionModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          StructuralDefinition documentation model.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:language.NaturalLanguage" relation="Extends">
          Natural language branch — documentation prose and example titles.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:language.Markdown" relation="Defines">
          Default content language of documentation — Markdown(English) unless overridden.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Block" relation="SeeAlso">
          Root block — documents is, language, documentation, references on every definition.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Definition" relation="Extends">
          Definition branch — shared documentation aggregations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.StructuralDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Documentation property and Change, Example, Reference sub-blocks on StructuralDefinition.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Relation" relation="Defines">
          Relation branch — optional relation attribute on see entries.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Documentation" relation="Extends">
          Abstract base for narrative documentation blocks in readMe lists.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Bootstrap" relation="ContrastsWith">
          Abstract bootstrap role marker — ReadFirst lives on references, not on Bootstrap.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:README" relation="SeeAlso">
          Standard README entry point — references with relation=ReadFirst in learning order.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.ReadFirst" relation="Defines">
          Relation block for curated README reading path entries.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.ReadNext" relation="Defines">
          Relation block for forward links between documentation blocks (Next reading step).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CoreUseCases" relation="SeeAlso">
          Structured project documentation use case — BlockML as structured alternative to Markdown.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BootstrapModel" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — README.bml, ReadFirst, TaskIndex, bootstrap context.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TaskGuideModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Task-oriented paths — TaskGuide blocks and task relations.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.EmbeddedExpressions-->
    <block>
      <name>
        EmbeddedExpressions
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.EmbeddedExpressions
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to embedded expressions in implementations
      </is>
      <documentation>
        BML Embedded Expressions are language-neutral Capability references inside a Capability
        &lt;implementation> body. Delimiters: opening &lt;$ · closing $>.
        
        The implementation CDATA is source text of the language named by language= (OpenSCAD,
        TypeScript, …). Regions wrapped in &lt;$ … $> are BlockML Expression Language fragments. The
        renderer replaces each embedded region (including delimiters) with equivalent target-language
        syntax; surrounding text is copied unchanged. Embedded Expressions are not a separate
        implementation language and not a replacement for &lt;implementation>.
        
        Supported call forms inside embeds (at least):
        • #this.someCapability(...) — own Block Capability (required for self-refs; bare foo(...) not
          supported here)
        • #math.max(...) / #strings.concat(...) — built-in services (same registry as ${ })
        • @bean.someCapability(...) — Transformation Capability on a bound bean
        
        Property access, operators, literals, and nested calls follow the same rules as ${ }
        expressions unless noted otherwise.
        
        Delimiter rules:
        • Zero, one, or many regions per implementation body; multiline OK
        • Whitespace immediately after &lt;$ and before $> is ignored
        • Nested delimiters forbidden
        • Unclosed &lt;$ → BML_UNCLOSED_EMBEDDED_EXPRESSION
        • Invalid embed content → BML_INVALID_EMBEDDED_EXPRESSION
        • Literal &lt;$ or $> in implementation source must not appear outside a valid pair
        
        Contrast with ${ } (member values):
        • Host: BML attributes / &lt;value> / directives vs implementation CDATA
        • Own Capability: myCapa(...) or link vs #this.myCapa(...) only
        • BOM: ExpressionValue.raw on members vs EmbeddedExpression { raw, start, end } on
          ImplementationBlock
        
        See doc:Expressions for ${ } language, layers, V1 scope, and scope rules; doc:SubBlockModel
        for utility Sub-Block implementations that commonly host embeds.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.Expressions" relation="SeeAlso">
          ${ } expression language — shared grammar and V1 scope.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CapabilitySemantics" relation="SeeAlso">
          Capabilities and implementation contracts.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.SubBlockModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          utility Sub-Blocks — OpenSCAD/TypeScript implementations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:language.OpenSCAD" relation="SeeAlso">
          Common V1 implementation language for embeds.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_UNCLOSED_EMBEDDED_EXPRESSION" relation="SeeAlso">
          Unclosed &lt;$ delimiter.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_INVALID_EMBEDDED_EXPRESSION" relation="SeeAlso">
          Invalid embedded expression content.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompilerArchitecture" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — parser / validator / renderer pipeline.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.EverythingIsABlock-->
    <block>
      <name>
        EverythingIsABlock
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.EverythingIsABlock
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to Everything-is-a-Block
      </is>
      <documentation>
        BlockML describes everything that belongs to a World's grammar as Blocks. There is one root
        (org.blockml.bml.core.Block), one parser, and one TypeRegistry bootstrap path — branch-specific
        rules come from the baseType chain, not from parallel ontologies.
        
        Everything is a Block means:
        • Composable domain types (Wall, StandardBrick, Set) are Blocks
        • ValueTypes (Integer, Size3D, Decimal) are Blocks — type definitions, not runtime values
        • Languages (TypeScript, OpenSCAD, PseudoCode) are Blocks — capability implementation contracts
        • Definition meta-model types (PropertyDefinition, StructuralDefinition sub-blocks) are Blocks
        • Documentation blocks (this branch) are Blocks
        • Libraries, Services, Constraints, Capabilities — all Blocks
        
        Two layers must be distinguished:
        • Authoring ontology (BML / WOT): Block = named definable unit with FQN and baseType chain
        • Compiler internals: free representation — plain data, split indexes; no mandatory uniform
          runtime Block class hierarchy
        
        One root · one registry · one parser. ValueTypes never store data — Properties own all concrete
        values. The type describes nature; the Property owns the value.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeSystemOverview" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Type system layers.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.DefinitionModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Definition branch meta-model.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Block" relation="Extends">
          Sole inheritance root for all Block definitions.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.ValueType" relation="Extends">
          ValueType branch — Property and Capability payload types.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Language" relation="Extends">
          Language branch — implementation language bindings for Capabilities.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Definition" relation="Extends">
          Definition branch — blocks that describe block structure in BlockML's own language.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Meta-model shape of every block document.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Documentation" relation="Extends">
          Abstract base for narrative documentation blocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.Integer" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example ValueType block — primitive type definition.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:language.TypeScript" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example Language block — programming language implementation contract.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CoreUseCases" relation="SeeAlso">
          What the principle is used for — structured documentation, domain modeling, AI workspaces.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BlockAndInstance" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — definition vs instance vs composition.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.Expressions-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Expressions
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.Expressions
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to BlockML expressions
      </is>
      <documentation>
        The BML Expression Language is extended JSON inside ${ } delimiters. It appears wherever a
        Block member value needs to be dynamic — derived, computed, or bound to a service. Capability
        &lt;implementation> bodies use the same language inside &lt;$ $> embeds
        (doc:EmbeddedExpressions).
        
        Four additions beyond JSON:
        • Property references — myProp, myProp.x (StructType members by name only; no size[0] on structs)
        • Model bindings — @model:/path, @model:relativeAttr
        • Bean calls — @bean.capa(...) (call) · @bean.capa (capability linking, no parentheses)
        • Built-in calls — #math.min(a, b), #strings.trim(s)
        
        Everything else follows a JSON subset: double-quoted strings, numbers, booleans (true/True/TRUE
        accepted; BOM stores lowercase), arrays [...], objects {...}. null is not a BML literal —
        absent/empty is not typed as null.
        
        Expression vs BOM data layer (separate):
        • BOM stores LiteralValue and ExpressionValue.raw — immutable authored form
        • parseExpression() → AST + type inference — utility output, not BOM mutation
        • evaluateExpression() — renderer working model only (Stage 3)
        Validator checks correctness (members, visibility, types, capabilities) but never executes.
        
        Scope rules:
        • Bare property access — current Block instance only (no parent/sibling climb); top-down
        • @bean — walk composition hierarchy; bml:scope=context visible to descendants; local = declaring
          Block only
        • #built-ins — always available; cannot be overridden
        • @model: relative paths need binding context from nearest parent Model-bound Aggregation
        • bml:each / bml:with variables — element + descendants; shadow outer same names
          (doc:BmlDirectives)
        • &lt;value> expressions — own Properties and #built-ins; topological resolve; cycles →
          BML_PROPERTY_CYCLE
        
        FunctionMapping maps call arguments to Capability inputType (doc:FunctionMappingModel). Named
        and positional must not be mixed under default mapping.
        
        V1 supported: literals; operators + - * / % == != &lt; > &lt;= >= &amp;&amp; || ! ?:;
        property access myProp / myProp.x / myProp[i] (index only where valid — not StructType fields);
        #math.min/max/abs/floor/ceil/round/pow/sqrt/clamp; #numbers.formatDecimal; #strings.concat/length;
        #lists.size/get/first/last/sequence; bml:each/if/unless/container/with.
        V1 deferred: @bean execution, model bindings, capability linking, most #strings/#bools,
        TypeScript service transpilation.
        
        Diagnostics (selection): BML_EXPR_SYNTAX_ERROR, BML_EXPR_UNKNOWN_PROPERTY,
        BML_EXPR_UNKNOWN_BEAN, BML_EXPR_UNKNOWN_BUILTIN, BML_EXPR_TYPE_MISMATCH,
        BML_EXPR_PAYLOAD_MISMATCH, BML_EXPR_LINK_*, BML_EXPR_MODEL_NO_CONTEXT,
        BML_EXPR_BUILTIN_NOT_V1, BML_EXPR_DIVISION_BY_ZERO — plus FunctionMapping codes.
        Formal EBNF / AST shapes remain DeepDive in 10_expression_language.md (parser utility contract).
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.EmbeddedExpressions" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — &lt;$ $> embeds inside Capability implementations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.FunctionMappingModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Call argument mapping to Capability inputType.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BmlDirectives" relation="SeeAlso">
          bml:each / bml:with scopes that host expression variables.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompositionModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Expressions in composition member values.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:mapping.FunctionMapping" relation="SeeAlso">
          Default function mapping block.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:service.Math" relation="SeeAlso">
          Built-in #math.* utilities.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:service.Strings" relation="SeeAlso">
          Built-in #strings.* utilities.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:service.Lists" relation="SeeAlso">
          Built-in #lists.* utilities.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Capability" relation="SeeAlso">
          Bean calls target Capability definitions.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:task.FixExpressionErrors" relation="SeeAlso">
          Cluster task for expression diagnostics.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_EXPR_SYNTAX_ERROR" relation="SeeAlso">
          Expression parse failure.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.FunctionMappingModel-->
    <block>
      <name>
        FunctionMappingModel
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.FunctionMappingModel
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to FunctionMapping
      </is>
      <documentation>
        FunctionMapping translates between function-parameter lists and Capability inputType /
        outputType. Concrete Mapping: org.blockml.bml.mapping.FunctionMapping.
        
        Two application sites (opposite directions):
        1. Expression calls — function syntax → FunctionMapping → Capability inputType
        2. Capability implementation — inputType → FunctionMapping → target-language parameters
           (and reverse for returns)
        
        Capabilities have no separate parameter list — only inputType / outputType. FunctionMapping
        matters when a Capability is invoked as a function (built-ins, utility Sub-Blocks, expression
        calls). Messaging transport ignores struct shape.
        
        inputType shapes:
        • Primitive → single positional or named argument
        • Named StructType / anonymous struct { a: Float, b: Float } → arity = field count; names =
          declaration order
        • Collection field { values: T[0..n] } → exactly one positional sequence argument — not
          fixed-arity multi-arg expansion
        Anonymous struct field types are full TypeExpressions — qualified references, multiplicity,
        unions, and nested anonymous structs included ({ list: core:ValueType[], index: Integer }). Arity
        and named keys come from the field list; the field types themselves do not change the mapping
        (doc:TypeExpressions — Anonymous StructTypes).
        
        Nested collection inputType — for a grouped, nested multiplicity such as (core:ValueType[])[]
        (doc:TypeExpressions — Nested multiplicities) the collection rule above applies to the outermost
        collection. A positional/variadic function representation may therefore map each function
        argument to one element of the outer collection:
        
          inputType="(core:ValueType[])[]"
          #lists.concat([1, 2], [3, 4], ["a"])  →  [[1, 2], [3, 4], ["a"]]
        
        The inner collections stay ordinary typed values and are validated recursively against
        core:ValueType[] — nesting changes the shape of the logical input value, not the mapping rules.
        
        Canonical call forms normalize to the same logical input value (positional, named name=expr,
        or object literal). Default FunctionMapping (when no explicit mapping is declared):
        • Positional-only — arg i → field i in declaration order
        • Named-only / object literal — keys = field names
        • Mixed positional + named → BML_MIXED_FUNCTION_ARGUMENTS
        • Too few / too many / unknown / duplicate → BML_MISSING_FUNCTION_PARAMETER,
          BML_TOO_MANY_FUNCTION_PARAMETERS, BML_UNKNOWN_FUNCTION_PARAMETER,
          BML_DUPLICATE_FUNCTION_PARAMETER
        
        Declaration order — same contract as StructType positional literals and ValueType instance
        configuration {…} positional args (doc:ValueTypeConfiguration). Member access in expressions
        remains by name (point.x) — not numeric index on StructTypes.
        
        Custom FunctionMapping on an implementation may reorder/rename parameters or reject forms.
        Required custom mapping absent → BML_MISSING_FUNCTION_MAPPING (reserved until custom mappings
        ship). Ambiguous custom mapping → BML_AMBIGUOUS_FUNCTION_MAPPING.
        
        Default mapping properties on mapping:FunctionMapping: supportsPositional=true,
        supportsNamed=true, supportsMixed=false.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:mapping.FunctionMapping" relation="Defines">
          Default function-parameter mapping block and flags.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.Expressions" relation="SeeAlso">
          Expression call sites that use FunctionMapping.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ValueTypeConfiguration" relation="SeeAlso">
          Shared declaration-order rule for positional {…} configuration.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeExpressions" relation="SeeAlso">
          inputType shapes — anonymous StructTypes, multiplicity, and nested multiplicities.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CapabilitySemantics" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — Capability kinds and public behavioural surface.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.SubBlockModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          utility Sub-Blocks — common FunctionMapping call targets.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_MIXED_FUNCTION_ARGUMENTS" relation="SeeAlso">
          Mixed positional and named arguments.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.GenericsModel-->
    <block>
      <name>
        GenericsModel
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.GenericsModel
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to generics
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Generic parameters belong exclusively to the Block that declares them — never inherited
        implicitly. When a generic Block is used as baseType, the derived Block must account for every
        base parameter by either binding it to a concrete TypeExpression in baseType or forwarding it
        to a parameter declared on the derived Block. All bound → concrete; any forwarded → remains
        generic. Resolution runs during inheritance: bound parameters are substituted throughout the
        inherited definition. Validator checks: every base parameter accounted for, argument count
        matches, each bound argument satisfies the parameter baseType (satisfiesTypeBound), forwarded
        parameters are declared locally.
        
        Abstract generic Blocks cannot be instantiated until a derived Block binds all open parameters
        (PrimitiveEntity(T) → IntegerEntity with baseType PrimitiveEntity(Integer), etc.).
        
        Declaration — on the Block via &lt;genericTypes> (meta-model GenericTypeDefinition). Parameter
        name = XML element local name (&lt;T baseType="…"/>). Optional defaultType when an application
        omits the argument:
        1. Concrete TypeExpression (e.g. type:Decimal)
        2. Earlier sibling parameter name only (declaration-order DAG; no forward refs/cycles)
        Resolution: authored args win → unbound params get defaults in declaration order → every
        parameter must ground to a concrete TypeExpression → each binding satisfies baseType.
        Diagnostics: BML_GENERIC_DEFAULT_FORWARD_REF, BML_GENERIC_DEFAULT_CYCLE,
        BML_GENERIC_DEFAULT_UNRESOLVED, BML_GENERIC_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION.
        
        BML attribute syntax — round parentheses: Box(Integer), TypeReference(core:Block),
        InstanceReference(core:Block), SelectiveGrid(Boolean | type:HoleType). Each (…) argument is a
        TypeExpression. With multiple parameters, labels match generic parameter names. At validate
        time placeholders (T) are substituted; mismatch → BML_GENERIC_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION.
        
        Orthogonal to {…} ValueType instance configuration (doc:ValueTypeConfiguration) and to
        multiplicity brackets (doc:TypeExpressions).
        
        Sub-Blocks / embeds may declare own genericTypes (Java static nested class model) — they do not
        see enclosing generics; forward explicitly (Item(T)). Name collisions on the same declaring
        Block with Block name or direct Sub-Block → BML_GENERIC_BLOCK_NAME_COLLISION /
        BML_GENERIC_SUBBLOCK_NAME_COLLISION.
        
        Constraint generics — Constraint branches declare genericTypes but arguments are never authored;
        the compiler binds T from the &lt;constraints> embedding context (Property → ValueType,
        Aggregation → AggregationDefinition, Block → BlockDefinition, …). Branch ↔ context mismatch →
        BML_CONSTRAINT_TARGET_MISMATCH. Validation property (Error/Warning/Information) controls
        enforcement; omitted → metadata only.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:definition.GenericTypeDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Meta-model for one &lt;genericTypes> parameter entry.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.BlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          genericTypes aggregation on block documents.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeExpressions" relation="SeeAlso">
          Generic application as a type expression; union arguments.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ContractsModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          satisfiesTypeBound for bounds — baseType chain or direct contracts.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ValueTypeConfiguration" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — orthogonal {…} instance configuration.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.TypeReference" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example open generic TypeReference(B).
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_GENERIC_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION" relation="SeeAlso">
          Type argument fails parameter baseType.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.ImplicitNamespaceResolution-->
    <block>
      <name>
        ImplicitNamespaceResolution
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.ImplicitNamespaceResolution
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to implicit namespaces
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Unqualified block references are supported only at specialized, semantically unambiguous
        reference sites. Each site defines exactly one implicit namespace. The name expands directly:
        
        &lt;Name> → &lt;implicitNamespace>.&lt;Name>
        
        There is no multi-stage search, no global TypeRegistry fallback, and no document-scope
        heuristics. If the expanded FQN does not exist or the Block does not satisfy the expected
        base type for that site, the compiler emits an error.
        
        Reference sites and implicit namespaces:
        • Property type → org.blockml.bml.type.*
        • Capability inputType / outputType → org.blockml.bml.type.*
        • Implementation language attribute → org.blockml.bml.language.*
        • language element child → org.blockml.bml.language.* (NaturalLanguage)
        • language attribute on documentation → org.blockml.bml.language.*
          (Markdown(NaturalLanguage) | NaturalLanguage — the generic argument resolves in the same
          namespace)
        • language attribute on change → org.blockml.bml.language.* (NaturalLanguage)
        • language attribute on see → org.blockml.bml.language.* (NaturalLanguage)
        • relation attribute on see → org.blockml.bml.relation.* (Relation)
        • language attribute on example → org.blockml.bml.language.* (Language)
        • status element content · bml:status attribute → org.blockml.bml.status.*
        • Capability type → org.blockml.bml.capability.*
        • Constraint validation attribute → org.blockml.bml.validation.*
        
        Examples:
        • &lt;width type="Integer" /> → org.blockml.bml.type.Integer
        • &lt;implementation language="TypeScript"> → org.blockml.bml.language.TypeScript
        • &lt;language>&lt;English />&lt;/language> → org.blockml.bml.language.English
        • &lt;documentation language="Markdown(German)"> → org.blockml.bml.language.Markdown with
          L = org.blockml.bml.language.German (shorthand &lt;documentation language="German"> resolves
          to the same entry)
        • &lt;change version="2.0.0" language="German"> → org.blockml.bml.language.German
        • &lt;see file="guide.md" language="English"> → org.blockml.bml.language.English
        • &lt;see type="core:Block" relation="Extends"> → org.blockml.bml.relation.Extends
        • &lt;example language="OpenSCAD"> → org.blockml.bml.language.OpenSCAD
        • &lt;status>Draft&lt;/status> → org.blockml.bml.status.Draft
        • type="Transformation" on Capability → org.blockml.bml.capability.Transformation
        • validation="Error" on constraint → org.blockml.bml.validation.Error
        
        Resolution applies per type reference, including inside composed type expressions. In an
        anonymous StructType on inputType / outputType, every field type is resolved on its own —
        { list: core:ValueType[], index: Integer } resolves list via the xmlns prefix core and index via
        the site's implicit namespace to org.blockml.bml.type.Integer. A qualified field type is never
        ambiguous with the field-name separator (doc:TypeExpressions — Anonymous StructTypes).
        
        General block references have no implicit namespace — baseType, aggregation type,
        InstanceReference type arguments, and composition instance tags require a full FQN or xmlns prefix.
        Explicit FQNs and xmlns prefixes remain valid everywhere, including at specialized sites.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BmlNamespace" relation="SeeAlso">
          Mandatory domain.bml namespace — FQN layout.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeExpressions" relation="SeeAlso">
          Per-field resolution inside anonymous StructTypes and other composed type expressions.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.DocumentationModel" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — is, documentation, references on Block definitions.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.Introduction-->
    <block>
      <name>
        Introduction
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.Introduction
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A short introduction to BlockML
      </is>
      <documentation>
        BlockML is a universal declarative language for describing parametric, composable semantic
        units called Blocks. BML (Block Markup Language) is the human- and editor-friendly authoring
        format; the BOM (Block Object Model) is its canonical, immutable, lossless representation.
        
        The BOM Compiler is domain-agnostic. It understands BML and the BOM object model. Domain-specific
        knowledge lives exclusively in Render Targets — MachineBlocks (SCAD) is the reference
        implementation, not the only possible World.
        
        Key goals:
        • One grammar for types, domain blocks, languages, services, and documentation
        • Semantics in BML — behaviour as Capabilities, not compiler special cases
        • Lossless BML ↔ BOM — the parser makes no semantic decisions
        • Renderer freedom — each target decides how to manifest Blocks in its output
        • Self-hosting bootstrap — the compiler loads framework BML before user content
        
        BML file extension is .bml. XML is the sole BML serialization for V1 and V2 — a JSON
        authoring form is not planned as an equivalent format. Every document uses one root
        element: &lt;block> (legacy roots such as device / sign / game are withdrawn).
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:core.Block" relation="Extends">
          Universal semantic unit — sole inheritance root of the BlockML grammar.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CoreUseCases" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — structured documentation, domain modeling, and AI workspaces.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.EverythingIsABlock" relation="SeeAlso">
          The central ontological principle of BlockML.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.LibrarySystem-->
    <block>
      <name>
        LibrarySystem
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.LibrarySystem
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to libraries and packages
      </is>
      <documentation>
        A Library groups related Block and ValueType definitions into a publishable unit. Concrete
        libraries use name Library, filename Library.bml, baseType org.blockml.bml.core.BlockLibrary.
        FQN = {package}.Library from the file's package path.
        
        Membership is defined exclusively by Library.bml blocks — not by directory layout. A .bml
        file is a library member only if listed in that library's blocks. Without a Library block,
        all .bml source files in the directory are public.
        
        Structure (catalog aggregations are public and readOnly — inspectable from foreign Blocks;
        authored only on the Library definition):
        • readMe — required human/LLM reading entry (ReadMe, 1..1; typically README)
        • bootstrap — optional automation SPOE (Bootstrap, 0..1)
        • dependencies — other Library blocks (typical: inner library depends on outer)
        • subLibraries — nested child Library blocks grouped under this library
        • blocks — exported Block and ValueType definitions
        • schemas — optional legacy XSD paths as FilePathEntity instances during migration
        • version on Library.bml — framework convention: version only on Library for org.blockml.bml.*
        
        Concrete Library.bml &lt;references> — at most two &lt;see> entries:
        1. Required relation=ReadFirst → the ReadMe named in readMe
        2. Optional relation=ThenContinueWith → the Bootstrap named in bootstrap (only when present)
        No other relations on the catalog descriptor; deep links belong on documentation blocks.
        
        Nested libraries may coexist at different package levels (org.blockml.bml.Library and
        org.blockml.bml.compiler.Library are separate units). Typical pattern: inner library lists
        outer in dependencies; outer lists inner in subLibraries. Violation: outer blocks
        referencing a Block exported by an inner library → BML_LIBRARY_NESTING_VIOLATION.
        
        Namespaces: xmlns prefixes map to FQN package prefixes. BOM stores canonical FQNs only —
        prefixes are authoring convenience. Every BML definition must reside under
        &lt;domain>.bml.* — no Block, Library, or other BlockML definition may exist outside
        the domain's bml namespace. See doc:BmlNamespace.
        
        Version resolution:
        effectiveBlockVersion = own version if present, else library.version if exported via blocks,
        else error BML_MISSING_VERSION. bml:version on TypeReferenceEntity sugar (readMe, bootstrap,
        dependencies, subLibraries, blocks) is an optional exact-version assert against the referenced
        type (value); omitted → TypeRegistry / library context; no match → BML_UNKNOWN_VERSION.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompilerArchitecture" relation="SeeAlso">
          Library discovery and TypeRegistry bootstrap.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:Library" relation="SeeAlso">
          Published catalog of the BlockML core library.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.BlockLibrary" relation="Extends">
          Abstract base for publishable library descriptors — not exported via blocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.ReadMe" relation="Requires">
          Required readMe target branch.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Bootstrap" relation="SeeAlso">
          Optional bootstrap SPOE branch.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.ThenContinueWith" relation="SeeAlso">
          Library &lt;see> after ReadFirst when bootstrap is present.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BmlNamespace" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — mandatory domain.bml namespace for all BML definitions.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.MemberAuthoring-->
    <block>
      <name>
        MemberAuthoring
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.MemberAuthoring
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to definition versus instance member syntax
      </is>
      <isnot>
        A general-purpose XML value or container wrapper
      </isnot>
      <summary>
        &lt;value> and &lt;composition> belong only to member declarations. On a Block instance
        the Property element or attribute is already the value; the Aggregation element already holds
        the child Blocks. Do not wrap instance payloads in those definition fields.
      </summary>
      <documentation>
        LLMs repeatedly confuse member-declaration syntax with the syntax used when assigning members
        on Block instances. The two most common mistakes are wrapping an instance Property in
        &lt;value> and wrapping instance Aggregation children in &lt;composition>.
        
        Incorrect on a Block instance:
        &lt;myns:MyBlock>&lt;myProperty>&lt;value>Hello!&lt;/value>&lt;/myProperty>&lt;/myns:MyBlock>
        &lt;myns:MyBlock>&lt;content>&lt;composition>…&lt;/composition>&lt;/content>&lt;/myns:MyBlock>
        
        Two contexts, never mixed:
        
        1. Definition context — a Property or Aggregation is being declared inside &lt;properties>
           or &lt;aggregations> (or an inherited member is filled on a Block definition).
        2. Instance context — a concrete Block instance receives Property values or Aggregation
           children.
        
        &lt;value> is the value field of def:PropertyDefinition. &lt;composition> is the
        composition field of def:AggregationDefinition. Both are definition-context fields. They are
        not general-purpose wrappers around instance payloads.
        
        Critical authoring rule — inside a Block instance:
        • NEVER introduce &lt;value> as a wrapper around a Property assignment
        • NEVER introduce &lt;composition> as a wrapper around Aggregation children
        Instead: the Property element (or Block attribute) contains the value; the Aggregation element
        contains the child Blocks. Group instance children with bml:container, not composition.
        
        PROPERTIES — definition. Inside &lt;properties> the child element is a PropertyDefinition.
        Its default value is the value field of that declaration. Element form is preferred when
        serializing definitions:
        &lt;properties>&lt;description>&lt;value>BlockML core library&lt;/value>&lt;/description>&lt;/properties>
        Compact attribute form on the declaration is also valid:
        &lt;properties>&lt;description value="BlockML core library"/>&lt;/properties>
        Do not specify both forms on the same declaration (conflicting payloads). Other declaration
        fields (type, readOnly, override, constraints, suggestions) stay on this PropertyDefinition
        element — they are not authored on domain instances.
        
        PROPERTIES — Block instance. The instance is not a PropertyDefinition. It only supplies the
        payload. Attribute form is preferred:
        &lt;myns:MyBlock myProperty="Hello!"/>
        Element form is the Property element with direct character content — the element itself is
        already the value container:
        &lt;myns:MyBlock>&lt;myProperty>Hello!&lt;/myProperty>&lt;/myns:MyBlock>
        Incorrect — definition value field used as an instance wrapper:
        &lt;myns:MyBlock>&lt;myProperty>&lt;value>Hello!&lt;/value>&lt;/myProperty>&lt;/myns:MyBlock>
        Also incorrect as a wrapper (same category error):
        &lt;myns:MyBlock>&lt;myProperty value="Hello!"/>&lt;/myns:MyBlock>
        unless myProperty is actually named value (see exception below).
        
        Exception — member whose name is value. PrimitiveEntity, TypeReferenceEntity, FilePathEntity
        and similar types declare a Property named value. Then value="…" or &lt;value>…&lt;/value>
        on the Block instance is ordinary instance syntax for that member, not a PropertyDefinition
        wrapper:
        &lt;type:FilePathEntity value="blockml-library.xsd"/>
        Annotation value facets (e.g. &lt;scad:value> under a property declaration) are a separate
        namespace axis on PropertyDefinition.value — not instance wrappers. See doc:AnnotationModel.
        
        AGGREGATIONS — definition. Inside &lt;aggregations> the child element is an
        AggregationDefinition. Its initial/default child structure is the composition field:
        &lt;aggregations>&lt;content>&lt;composition>&lt;myns:Child/>&lt;/composition>&lt;/content>&lt;/aggregations>
        &lt;composition> is allowed only as that definition field, nested inside the aggregation
        member — never standalone (BML_STANDALONE_COMPOSITION).
        
        AGGREGATIONS — Block instance. Child Block instances are placed directly inside the
        Aggregation element. There is no composition wrapper:
        &lt;myns:MyBlock>&lt;content>&lt;myns:Child/>&lt;/content>&lt;/myns:MyBlock>
        Incorrect:
        &lt;myns:MyBlock>&lt;content>&lt;composition>&lt;myns:Child/>&lt;/composition>&lt;/content>&lt;/myns:MyBlock>
        The same rule applies recursively — a Block instance nested inside a definition composition
        still fills its own aggregations without a composition wrapper:
        &lt;aggregations>&lt;myAggr>&lt;composition>
          &lt;myns:MyBlock>&lt;content>&lt;myns:Child/>&lt;/content>&lt;/myns:MyBlock>
        &lt;/composition>&lt;/myAggr>&lt;/aggregations>
        Outer &lt;composition> is valid (myAggr is an Aggregation definition). Inner
        &lt;composition> under content is invalid (content is populated on a Block instance).
        
        On instances, the virtual grouping element is bml:container — not composition. Both
        composition (definitions) and bml:container (instances) host bml:if, bml:each, and bml:with.
        
        Unified pattern:
        Definition Property → value attribute or &lt;value> wrapper on the PropertyDefinition
        Definition Aggregation → &lt;composition> wrapper on the AggregationDefinition
        Instance Property → attribute on the Block, or direct Property element content
        Instance Aggregation → direct child Block instances (bml:container for grouping)
        
        Capabilities on instances follow the Property pattern: attribute or child element, no extra
        wrapper. readOnly effective members must not be authored on instances
        (BML_READONLY_MEMBER_WRITE).
      </documentation>
      <keywords>
        <blockml:meta.FreeTextKeyword term="value" />
        <blockml:meta.FreeTextKeyword term="composition" />
        <blockml:meta.FreeTextKeyword term="property" />
        <blockml:meta.FreeTextKeyword term="aggregation" />
        <blockml:meta.FreeTextKeyword term="instance" />
        <blockml:meta.FreeTextKeyword term="definition" />
        <blockml:meta.FreeTextKeyword term="member authoring" />
      </keywords>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BlockAndInstance" relation="SeeAlso">
          Definition vs instance mental model.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.PropertyDefinition" property="value" relation="Defines">
          Definition-only value field on a Property declaration.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.AggregationDefinition" property="composition" relation="Defines">
          Definition-only composition field on an Aggregation declaration.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.MemberDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Element preferred on declarations; member name as attribute preferred on instances.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompositionModel" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — composition placement, recursion, bml:container, and instance IDs.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BmlDirectives" relation="SeeAlso">
          bml:container, bml:with, bml:each, bml:if on composition and instances.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.AnnotationModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Annotation value facets — not instance value wrappers.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.PrimitiveEntity" relation="SeeAlso">
          Entity whose instance member is actually named value.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.TypeReferenceEntity" relation="SeeAlso">
          Instance value="…" because the member name is value — not a wrapper.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_STANDALONE_COMPOSITION" relation="SeeAlso">
          composition outside an aggregation member on a definition.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:task.FixCompositionPlacement" relation="SeeAlso">
          Procedure when composition is placed on an instance or left standalone.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.PartialMemberOverrides" relation="SeeAlso">
          Filling inherited members on definitions; readOnly forbids instance writes.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.MemberVisibility-->
    <block>
      <name>
        MemberVisibility
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.MemberVisibility
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to member and Sub-Block visibility
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Member and Sub-Block visibility controls BlockML access only — which definitions may reference,
        read, or set a member or Sub-Block. It does not prescribe how a renderer manifests visibility.
        Renderers always see the entire Canonical BOM (public, protected, and private). Visibility
        restricts authoring access, not technical BOM access.
        
        Naming convention (primary signal):
        • myVar — public
        • _myVar — protected
        • __myVar — private
        
        The prefix is BlockML visibility, not logical member identity. Collision checking uses the
        normalizedLogicalName with visibility prefixes stripped (myVar / _myVar / __myVar → myVar).
        Members that differ only by visibility prefix must not coexist in the same Block →
        BML_DUPLICATE_MEMBER_VISIBILITY_VARIANT. A logical member exists exactly once with exactly one
        visibility.
        
        Override matching uses normalizedLogicalName. A child may redeclare with a different visibility
        prefix only when the change widens access:
        • protected → public — allowed
        • private → any — forbidden (BML_FINAL_MEMBER_OVERRIDE)
        • public → protected/private, or protected → private — forbidden (BML_VISIBILITY_NARROWING)
        Effective visibility and authored name come from the child declaration. Member-level bml:xml may
        change on the same override.
        
        Formal access (D = declaring Block, C = Block whose definition contains the access, I = instance
        type, O = immediate composition owner of that instance):
        • public — always
        • private — only when C = D (owning composite does not gain access to private members of
          contained Sub-Block instances)
        • protected — when C = D, or C inherits from D, or C = O, or C inherits from I.
          Only the immediate composition owner grants protected configuration access on children — not
          the entire outer composition hierarchy.
        
        Aggregation members: visibility also controls which bml:id values may be referenced
        (basePresets, Step.part, {BlockFQN}::{id}, …):
        • public — cross-document {EnclosingBlockFQN}::{id}; unqualified ids within permitted access
        • protected — declaring Block and its child Blocks (derived / Sub-Blocks in the same tree)
        • private — declaring Block definition only
        No separate export flag on composition or bml:container — reachability follows the aggregation
        member's visibility. Qualified cross-document references require a public target aggregation and
        a literal static bml:id.
        
        Optional visibility property on members (and optional root-level visibility on Sub-Blocks) must
        match the naming convention → mismatch BML_VISIBILITY_MISMATCH. Root Blocks must not declare
        root-level visibility.
        
        Validator diagnostics (access checks do not alter the Canonical BOM):
        BML_PRIVATE_MEMBER_ACCESS, BML_PROTECTED_MEMBER_ACCESS,
        BML_DUPLICATE_MEMBER_VISIBILITY_VARIANT, BML_VISIBILITY_MISMATCH, BML_VISIBILITY_NARROWING,
        BML_FINAL_MEMBER_OVERRIDE (private cannot be overridden).
        
        Declared on org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition as visibility (and related override
        rules). Sub-Block visibility uses the same naming convention — see also Sub-Block authoring
        rules on org.blockml.bml.definition.SubBlockDefinition.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:definition.MemberDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Member visibility property and override widening rules.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.AggregationDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Aggregation visibility controls composition instance ID reachability.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.SubBlockDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Sub-Blocks share the same naming-convention visibility rules.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.PartialMemberOverrides" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — merge semantics when overriding inherited members.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompositionModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Composition placement; ID reachability follows aggregation visibility.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ModelingGuidelines" relation="SeeAlso">
          Inheritance, contracts, and member modeling patterns.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.ModelingGuidelines-->
    <block>
      <name>
        ModelingGuidelines
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.ModelingGuidelines
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to modeling patterns
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Inheritance (baseType):
        • Single inheritance — each Block has exactly one baseType (mandatory except org.blockml.bml.core.Block)
        • Use for branch membership (ValueType, Language, domain base) and shared member fragments
        • Framework branches inherit via org.blockml.bml.core.ValueType, Language, or domain bases
        • final on Block or member prevents further override → BML_FINAL_BASE_TYPE / BML_FINAL_MEMBER_OVERRIDE
        
        Contracts:
        • Reusable partial Block definitions — not pure interfaces; may include default implementations
        • Use when multiple Blocks share a member fragment without sharing a common baseType ancestor
        • Declared on root block only via contracts aggregation — direct Block references
        • Merge order: baseType chain → contracts (declaration order) → local declarations
        • Contract may supply partial members (e.g. id type only); concrete Block completes defaults,
          constraints, and documentation
        • subBlocks and compositions on contract types are ignored
        
        Capabilities:
        • All behaviour — init, get/set, render, domain operations — express as Capabilities
        • Prefer PseudoCode or natural language implementations for semantic descriptions
        • Use Inbound/Outbound/Transformation kinds to describe intent, not compiler behaviour
        • Public surface of a Block is Capabilities — Properties are internal configuration
        
        Constraints:
        • Declare on Properties via constraints aggregation — Constraint Block instances
        • Generic binding is automatic: Min/Max bind T from property type; MinItems/MaxItems from
          collection element type; MinLength/MaxLength from Text element type
        • Use for validation rules expressible as reusable Constraint blocks (Min, Max, Pattern, …)
        • Suggestions (optional Block instances of property type) guide editors — not validation
        
        General: keep &lt;is> stable; put cross-links in references; prefer explicit baseType over
        implicit defaults; document partial members in contracts clearly.
        
        Documentation formatting: block documentation is Markdown(English) by default — structure longer
        prose with paragraphs, headings, lists, and inline code instead of one unbroken text; keep
        summary plain text. Human-friendly formatting is a central part of BlockML
        (doc:DocumentationModel).
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ContractsModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Contracts and validation by branch.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ConstraintsModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Property constraints and suggestions.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Capability" relation="Extends">
          Capability branch — behaviour as the public semantic surface.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Constraint" relation="Extends">
          Constraint branch — reusable validation rules on Properties.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:capability.Inbound" relation="Defines">
          Inbound capability — when external input is received.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:constraint.Min" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example scalar constraint — binds T from enclosing property type.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ConstraintsModel" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — constraints and suggestions.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberVisibility" relation="SeeAlso">
          Member and Sub-Block visibility rules.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.PartialMemberOverrides" relation="SeeAlso">
          Partial overrides and abstract/final merge semantics.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ContractsModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Contracts merge order and satisfiesTypeBound.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.PartialMemberOverrides-->
    <block>
      <name>
        PartialMemberOverrides
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.PartialMemberOverrides
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to partial member overrides
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Child Blocks override inherited members with override="true", or satisfy abstract implementation
        obligations with a locally complete override. The flag is required whenever a local member
        redeclaration merges with an existing member from the baseType chain or from contracts[] —
        contract completion uses the same rule as baseType refinement.
        
        Local declaration properties (never inherited; only effects propagate):
        • abstract="true" — incomplete declaration; creates an implementation obligation
        • final="true" — seals the declaration; descendants cannot override
        • override="true" — confirms deliberate redefinition of an inherited member
        Boolean properties require an explicit true or false — empty elements and bare attributes are
        invalid. Preferred literals: lowercase true / false.
        
        Modes:
        • new — introduces a member; must not set override="true" → else BML_OVERRIDE_WITHOUT_PARENT
        • override — refines an inherited member; omit override when merging → BML_OVERRIDE_REQUIRED
        Each redeclaration layer must set override="true" again — override is not inherited.
        
        Partial authoring: only the member name, override mode, and changed attributes need to be
        specified — all other attributes are inherited from the parent definition. Omit unchanged type
        (and other unchanged attributes such as bml:xml or defaults). On aggregations, restating an
        identical type when only filling an inherited member → BML_REDUNDANT_AGGREGATION_TYPE (warning
        V1). Declare type on an override only when refining or replacing the inherited type.
        
        Abstract → implementation:
        • Parent may omit required parts (e.g. property without type or value) when abstract="true"
        • Child implements with override="true" and a locally complete declaration (abstract defaults
          to false → locally concrete)
        • To keep a member abstract in a child, redeclare with override="true" abstract="true"
        • Abstract member left unimplemented on a concrete block → BML_ABSTRACT_MEMBER_NOT_IMPLEMENTED
        Capability overrides supply a full implementation child element (not a boolean attribute).
        
        Merge rules (resolveEffectiveMembers — baseType chain → contracts declaration order → local):
        • Same member kind required (property overrides property, …)
        • Only specified attrs replaced; unspecified attrs inherited
        • Override matching key: (resolvedNamespace, normalizedLogicalName) — visibility prefixes
          stripped; xmlns prefix aliases may differ if they resolve to the same namespace
        • Visibility may widen (protected → public); narrowing → BML_VISIBILITY_NARROWING
        • Member-level bml:xml may change on the same override
        • Private members cannot be overridden → BML_FINAL_MEMBER_OVERRIDE
        • final="true" on parent prevents override → BML_FINAL_MEMBER_OVERRIDE
        
        readOnly is orthogonal to final / abstract / override: it controls instance authoring only.
        • Block definition — may declare and set readOnly members via value, composition, or partial
          override
        • Child Block definition — may override an inherited readOnly member (value/composition only;
          cannot override a final member at all)
        • Block instance — must not set a member whose effective definition has readOnly=true →
          BML_READONLY_MEMBER_WRITE (after resolveEffectiveMembers on every BlockInstance)
        • Expressions — may read readOnly members subject to visibility; renderers may read the BOM
        A public readOnly member remains publicly readable. final blocks value overrides entirely;
        readOnly does not.
        
        Declared on org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition (abstract, final, override, readOnly,
        visibility) and specialized on PropertyDefinition, AggregationDefinition, AssociationDefinition,
        and CapabilityDefinition.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:definition.MemberDefinition" relation="Defines">
          abstract, final, override, readOnly, visibility — local declaration properties.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.PropertyDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Property override completeness — type and value.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.AggregationDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Aggregation overrides — omit unchanged type; composition fill.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.CapabilityDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Capability overrides — implementation child element.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberVisibility" relation="SeeAlso">
          Visibility widening and private override prohibition.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ModelingGuidelines" relation="SeeAlso">
          Contracts merge order and abstract/final patterns.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberAuthoring" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — definition fields vs instance payloads (&lt;value> / &lt;composition>).
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.PseudoCode-->
    <block>
      <name>
        PseudoCode
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.PseudoCode
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to PseudoCode semantics
      </is>
      <documentation>
        PseudoCode (org.blockml.bml.language.PseudoCode) is a ProgrammingLanguage for free-form
        pseudocode Capability implementations. The code must be internally consistent but is
        otherwise unconstrained — an LLM may interpret it. Content/text language follows
        ProgrammingLanguage.L (NaturalLanguage, default English) — declare L only when the prose
        is not English.
        
        Why PseudoCode exists:
        • BlockML describes semantics, not a single target language
        • Capability implementations need human- and LLM-readable bodies before renderers map them
        • PseudoCode avoids premature binding to Java, TypeScript, SCAD, or Python syntax
        • Semantic descriptions in PseudoCode stay stable across Worlds and render targets
        
        When to use PseudoCode:
        • Documenting intended behaviour on Capabilities during design
        • Framework and domain blocks where the semantic contract matters more than executable code
        • Blocks read by LLMs for code generation — PseudoCode is the preferred semantic layer
        
        When to use concrete languages:
        • org.blockml.bml.language.TypeScript, OpenSCAD, Python, … when a target renderer transpiles
          or emits the implementation directly
        • Block definitions may restrict permitted languages via the languages aggregation
        
        Prefer describing semantics in Capability implementation bodies (PseudoCode or natural
        language blocks like English/German) rather than encoding behaviour in compiler special cases.
        The renderer interprets; BlockML declares intent.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CapabilitySemantics" relation="SeeAlso">
          Capability implementation languages.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CapabilitySemantics" relation="SeeAlso">
          languages aggregation on Block definitions.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:language.PseudoCode" relation="SeeAlso">
          Free-form pseudocode implementation language block.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:language.ProgrammingLanguage" relation="Extends">
          Abstract base for programming language implementations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Capability" relation="Extends">
          Capability branch — implementation member references Language FQN.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CapabilitySemantics" relation="SeeAlso">
          Capability semantics — behaviour expressed as Capabilities, interpreted by renderers.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.WOTFoundation" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — optional philosophical foundation (Idea, Manifestation, Grammar, World).
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.RendererFreedom-->
    <block>
      <name>
        RendererFreedom
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.RendererFreedom
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to renderer freedom
      </is>
      <documentation>
        The Render Target is free in what it produces and how it maps BML to output. No global rule
        requires output to be blocks in the target language, 1:1 mapping input Block → one output
        artifact, or any files at all (side-effects-only targets are valid).
        
        Cardinality, format, and semantics are target-defined. The framework provides utilities;
        each renderer decides which to use and in what sequence:
        
        SCAD Renderer: resolveInheritance → evaluateExpression → expandComposition → renderSCAD
        Java Renderer: resolveInheritance → resolveContracts → renderJava
        Documentation Renderer: renderRawBOM() → Markdown / HTML
        
        Each renderer owns an internal working model — optimised, cacheable, freely mutable:
        SCAD: Modules, Parameters, GeometryGraph
        Java: Classes, Fields, Methods
        React: Components, Props, JSX Tree
        
        The working model belongs exclusively to the renderer. The Canonical BOM remains unchanged.
        
        MachineBlocks SCAD re-creates Blocks in the target language via mb_block() — a common pattern,
        not a platform obligation. ScreenMachine may emit React JSX; EBOM may emit KiCad symbols.
        The renderer reads BML Blocks; it does not need to preserve Block as a runtime concept in
        its internal pipeline — only to emit correct target output.
        
        Platonic mapping: BML holds the Idea (Urbild) stable. The compiler is Manifestor tooling —
        it may represent the Idea internally as it chooses. Full Manifestation is the renderer's and
        runtime's job in a specific World.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.EverythingIsABlock" relation="SeeAlso">
          Semantic layer vs compiler; renderer freedom.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CoreUseCases" relation="SeeAlso">
          Domain modeling use case — one model, many renderer manifestations.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.LibrarySystem" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — libraries, packages, namespaces, versioning.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.SubBlockModel-->
    <block>
      <name>
        SubBlockModel
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.SubBlockModel
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to Sub-Blocks and embedded blocks
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Sub-Blocks are Block definitions nested inside a parent Block — Java static nested classes: they
        share the parent's FQN prefix, may declare own genericTypes, and have no enclosing-instance /
        outer-generic scope (forward type parameters explicitly). Declared in &lt;subBlocks> on a root
        block, parent Sub-Block, or embedded block; may nest to arbitrary depth. Identity and FQN
        derivation: doc:TypeIdentityDerivation. Unqualified nested names: innermost scope first.
        
        Format: same fields as root Blocks; prefer omitting &lt;type> (derived). baseType is required.
        Must not declare embeddedBlocks → BML_EMBEDDED_BLOCKS_ON_SUBBLOCK.
        
        Visibility — same naming convention and access model as members (doc:MemberVisibility):
        • SubBlock — public (inside and outside enclosing definition)
        • _SubBlock — protected (enclosing definition and derived Blocks)
        • __SubBlock — private (immediately declaring Block only)
        Differing only by visibility prefix → BML_DUPLICATE_MEMBER_VISIBILITY_VARIANT. Optional
        root-level &lt;visibility> on Sub-Blocks must match the name → else BML_VISIBILITY_MISMATCH;
        when omitted, derive from name. Root Blocks must not declare &lt;visibility> →
        BML_VISIBILITY_ON_ROOT_BLOCK. Access diagnostics: BML_PRIVATE_SUBBLOCK_ACCESS,
        BML_PROTECTED_SUBBLOCK_ACCESS.
        
        Embedded Blocks (Inline Library) — virtual .bml files under root &lt;embeddedBlocks>. Meta-model
        EmbeddedBlockDefinition. Each embed is a full Block document; FQN = resolve(type), not nested
        under the root. Root-only: forbidden on Sub-Blocks and on embeds themselves
        (BML_NESTED_EMBEDDED_BLOCKS). type required and fully qualified
        (BML_MISSING_EMBEDDED_BLOCK_TYPE / BML_UNQUALIFIED_EMBEDDED_BLOCK_TYPE). Within the document,
        embed FQNs shadow library/file definitions; duplicates → BML_DUPLICATE_EMBEDDED_BLOCK;
        externally invisible. Composition refs to embeds must use fully qualified Block types.
        
        Central distinction:
        • SubBlockDefinition — semantically nested type
        • EmbeddedBlockDefinition — virtual .bml in root embeddedBlocks
        • RootBlockDefinition — document root; sole owner of embeddedBlocks
        
        Composition Block references: empty child meaning depends on aggregation type (instance vs
        TypeReferenceEntity sugar). Domain types need FQN or xmlns. Sub-Blocks of the enclosing root may
        use unqualified local name inside that Block's composition; outside → qualified name / FQN else
        BML_UNQUALIFIED_BLOCK_REFERENCE.
        
        Naming vs parent: a Sub-Block should not share the root Block's name (…Calibrator.Calibrator);
        bare short tags become ambiguous → BML_SUBBLOCK_QUALIFIED_REFERENCE_REQUIRED.
        
        utility Sub-Blocks — baseType org.blockml.bml.core.Entity bind Transformation Capability
        implementations (language = org.blockml.bml.language.*). V1: OpenSCAD (main function name =
        Capability name; helpers allowed; compiler prefixes with block FQN) and TypeScript (compile-time
        literal eval when args are literals). One implementation child per Capability; language required.
        CDATA may use BML Embedded Expression (&lt;$ … $>).
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:definition.SubBlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Nested Sub-Block definition shape.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.RootBlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Document root — subBlocks and embeddedBlocks owner.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Virtual .bml entries under embeddedBlocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Entity" relation="SeeAlso">
          Typical baseType for utility Sub-Blocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeIdentityDerivation" relation="SeeAlso">
          FQN derivation and unqualified nested resolution.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.MemberVisibility" relation="SeeAlso">
          Naming-convention visibility shared with Sub-Blocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CompositionModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Composition children and aggregation fill.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ContractsModel" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — contracts merge and type bounds (root only; not on Sub-Blocks).
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — task guide documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.TaskGuideModel-->
    <block>
      <name>
        TaskGuideModel
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.TaskGuideModel
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to TaskGuide blocks
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Task-oriented paths complement ReadFirst learning paths on ReadMe entry points:
        
        ReadFirst (relation)     — mental learning order on ReadMe / README blocks
        TaskIndex (relation)   — catalog of accomplishable tasks on ReadMe
        TaskGuide (branch)     — org.blockml.bml.task.* procedural how-to blocks
        TaskStep (relation)    — ordered steps inside a TaskGuide (document order = step order)
        Prerequisite (relation)— understand before starting a TaskGuide
        Diagnostic (branch)    — org.blockml.bml.diagnostic.* validator code documentation
        Resolves (relation)    — Diagnostic → TaskGuide fix procedure
        
        One mechanism: all navigation uses &lt;references> and &lt;see> with typed relation
        attributes — no separate task aggregation on ReadMe. Automated process SPOE uses
        Library.bootstrap (core:Bootstrap), separate from reading/task catalog on ReadMe.
        
        Tooling (future):
        • filter relation=ReadFirst → learn context bundle
        • filter relation=TaskIndex → task menu
        • load Diagnostic by code → follow Resolves → TaskStep chain
        
        org.blockml.bml.README lists TaskIndex entries after ReadFirst entries. Future Bootstrap
        subtypes (AgentBootstrap, CIBootstrap) supply automation SPOE members — not reading paths.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:task.FixVisibilityAccess" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example cluster task — visibility diagnostics.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:task.FixMemberOverride" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example cluster task — override/merge diagnostics.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:task.FixCompositionPlacement" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example cluster task — composition placement.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:task.FixTypeIdentity" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example cluster task — type identity diagnostics.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.TaskGuide" relation="Defines">
          Abstract base for org.blockml.bml.task.* procedural blocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Diagnostic" relation="Defines">
          Abstract base for org.blockml.bml.diagnostic.* error code blocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.TaskIndex" relation="Defines">
          Catalog relation on README — lists available tasks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.TaskStep" relation="Defines">
          Ordered step relation inside TaskGuide references.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.Prerequisite" relation="Defines">
          Before-you-start relation on TaskGuide blocks.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:relation.Resolves" relation="Defines">
          Diagnostic → TaskGuide fix link.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:task.AuthorNewBlock" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example task — author a new Block definition.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_UNKNOWN_TYPE" relation="SeeAlso">
          Example diagnostic — links to DebugUnknownType task via Resolves.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BootstrapModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Bootstrap role, README, and ReadFirst learning paths.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:README" relation="SeeAlso">
          Standard entry point — TaskIndex entries list available tasks.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.TypeExpressions-->
    <block>
      <name>
        TypeExpressions
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.TypeExpressions
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to type expressions
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Everything beyond the four ValueType kinds is a type expression — a use or combination of named
        types, not a new BlockML type class.
        
        ValueType = named semantic meaning (Primitive · Enum · Struct · Constant)
        TypeExpression = usage or combination (multiplicity · union · generic application · instance
        configuration · anonymous StructType · bml:Self · T)
        
        Examples: Integer[] · Integer[3] · Integer[0..n] · Boolean | Integer · Box(Integer) ·
        InstanceReference(core:Block) · SelectiveGrid(Boolean | HoleType) · Text{maxLength: 100} ·
        { x: Integer, y: Integer } · bml:Self · T
        
        In type="…" XML attributes, generic applications use round parentheses (not angle brackets) so
        values stay valid XML. Generic type arguments may be full type expressions — unions,
        multiplicity, nested generics, and {…} configuration. Parentheses bind the argument:
        SelectiveGrid(Boolean | HoleType) means T = Boolean | HoleType, not SelectiveGrid(Boolean) | HoleType.
        When a parameter constrains baseType to ValueType (or another branch), every union alternative
        must satisfy satisfiesTypeBound → else BML_GENERIC_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION.
        
        Obsolete wrappers → write unions directly:
        • AutoOr&lt;T> → Constant(Text){value: "auto"} | T
        • FalseOr&lt;T> → Boolean.False | T
        • NoneOr&lt;T> → Constant(Text){value: "none"} | T
        • BooleanOr&lt;T> → Boolean | T
        • Array&lt;T> → T[]
        Boolean.True / Boolean.False are Sub-Block shortcuts for Constant(Boolean){value: true/false}.
        
        Multiplicity — no separate Array type. Bracket notation is a postfix operation on a
        TypeExpression, not an intrinsic property of a named ValueType; it is metadata on the type
        reference and the FQN is unchanged. Prefer Collection / Multiplicity terminology (not Array).
        Semantics only — ordered vs unordered and concrete storage are renderer-defined.
        • Integer → exactly one
        • Integer[] ≡ Integer[0..n]
        • Integer[3] · Integer[1..5] · Integer[1..]
        Applies commonly on aggregation/association types and capability input fields; bare properties
        are usually exactly one value.
        
        Nested multiplicities — multiplicity may be applied to any TypeExpression, including one that
        already carries a multiplicity. Parentheses group the inner TypeExpression before the outer
        multiplicity is applied, so nested collections need no separate Array or collection type:
        • Integer[] → collection of Integer
        • (Integer[])[] → collection of collections of Integer
        • (Integer[3])[] → collection of fixed-size Integer collections
        • (Integer[])[2] → fixed-size collection containing two Integer collections
        Integer[][] is intentionally not supported — nested multiplicities must use explicit grouping
        ((Integer[])[]), which keeps the binding of each multiplicity unambiguous. Ungrouped stacked
        brackets are ill-formed → BML_INVALID_TYPE_REFERENCE.
        
        Nested collections nest their rules too: compatibility, element-wise validation, and
        multiplicity bounds are applied recursively, outer collection first. (core:ValueType[])[]
        accepts [[1, 2], [3, 4], ["a"]] — three elements against the outer multiplicity, each element
        validated against core:ValueType[]; (Integer[])[2] rejects [[1], [2], [3]] with
        BML_CARDINALITY_VIOLATION on the outer collection. For Capability inputs the ordinary collection
        FunctionMapping rule applies to the outermost collection, so service:Lists concat with
        inputType="(core:ValueType[])[]" maps #lists.concat([1, 2], [3, 4], ["a"]) to
        [[1, 2], [3, 4], ["a"]] — one function argument per outer element, inner collections remaining
        ordinary typed values (doc:FunctionMappingModel).
        
        Collection compatibility — for a type reference with multiplicity, compatibility is evaluated
        against the referenced element type. If S is assignable to T (S &lt;: T), then a collection of S
        values is assignable to a collection of T values, provided the source multiplicity is compatible
        with the target multiplicity — collections are covariant in their element type:
        
          S &lt;: T
          ---------------
          S[] &lt;: T[]
        
        With Integer &lt;: Number &lt;: PrimitiveType &lt;: ValueType:
        • Integer[] &lt;: Number[] · Integer[] &lt;: PrimitiveType[] · Integer[] &lt;: ValueType[]
        • Text[] &lt;: ValueType[] · SomeStruct[] &lt;: ValueType[]
        • Integer[3] &lt;: Number[] — but Integer[] is not assignable to Number[1..2] (bounds do not fit)
        
        A value assigned to a collection type is validated element by element against the referenced
        element type. core:ValueType[] accepts zero or more values where every element's concrete
        ValueType is assignable to ValueType — therefore:
        • [1, 2, 3] → valid as Integer[], as Number[], and as ValueType[]
        • ["a", "b"] → valid as Text[] and as ValueType[]
        
        Heterogeneous collections — a collection type does not require all elements to have the same
        concrete subtype. Each element is validated independently against the declared element type:
        • ValueType[] : [1, "text", true] is valid, because Integer &lt;: ValueType, Text &lt;: ValueType,
          and Boolean &lt;: ValueType
        • Integer[] : [1, 2, 3] is valid — [1, "text", 3] is invalid, because Text is not assignable to
          Integer
        An element whose type is not assignable to the declared element type is rejected
        (BML_INVALID_PROPERTY on authored member values, BML_EXPR_TYPE_MISMATCH on expression payloads);
        an element count outside the multiplicity bounds → BML_CARDINALITY_VIOLATION.
        
        Unions — alternatives with |. Match order is left to right, first hit wins (no best-match).
        Prefer narrower / sentinel alternatives on the left (Boolean | Text, not Text | Boolean).
        Later alternatives that can never win → BML_UNREACHABLE_UNION_ALTERNATIVE (warning). Singleton
        alternatives use Constant(T){value: …} (doc:ConstantValueType). Future literal shorthand
        Boolean | "auto" desugars to Constant form.
        
        Anonymous StructTypes — { field: Type, … } is the type-expression form of a StructType without a
        declared name, and the mechanism for structured Capability inputs. The grammar is recursive:
        
          AnonymousStruct := "{" StructField ("," StructField)* "}"
          StructField     := Identifier ":" TypeExpression
        
        Because the right side of a field is a full TypeExpression — not merely an unqualified type name
        — qualified type references, multiplicity, generic applications, unions, instance configuration,
        and nested anonymous StructTypes are all valid field types:
        • { list: core:ValueType[], index: Integer } — qualified reference with multiplicity
        • { values: foo:Thing[1..n] } · { value: Integer | Text } · { nested: { x: Integer, y: Integer } }
        • { lists: (core:ValueType[])[] } — nested multiplicity as a field type
        
        The colon separating a field name from its type and the colon inside a qualified type reference
        belong to different grammar levels and are unambiguous:
        
          list : core:ValueType[]
               ^     ^
               |     +-- prefix colon of a qualified TypeReference (xmlns prefix + local name)
               +-- field name / type separator
        
        So { list: core:ValueType[], index: Integer } is one anonymous StructType with field list of type
        core:ValueType[] and field index of type Integer. Field declaration order is significant — same
        contract as named StructTypes (doc:FunctionMappingModel).
        
        Braces are disambiguated by position: a { opening a type expression starts an anonymous
        StructType whose field values are TypeExpressions; a { directly following a type reference is
        ValueType instance configuration whose field values are literals — Text{maxLength: 100}
        (doc:ValueTypeConfiguration). Each field type resolves like any other type reference at that
        site: unqualified names via the site's implicit namespace (Integer → org.blockml.bml.type.Integer
        on inputType / outputType), prefixed names via xmlns, full FQNs always
        (doc:ImplicitNamespaceResolution).
        
        Conformance note — an implementation that accepts { list: Integer[], index: Integer } but rejects
        { list: core:ValueType[], index: Integer } is missing support for qualified type references
        inside anonymous StructType fields. That is a parser / mapper limitation, not a semantic
        ambiguity in the type expression.
        
        Bare type name sites (Property type, Capability inputType/outputType) require ValueTypes (plus
        type expressions over them). Aggregation type may be any Block FQN (plain composition or
        TypeReferenceEntity application). Association type must be InstanceReference(T) or
        InstanceReference(T)[] with T extending core:Block — bare Block FQNs are invalid.
        Non-ValueType on a property → BML_INVALID_PROPERTY_TYPE.
        
        Compiler-internal descriptors (UnionTypeDescriptor, …) are not BML Block classes.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeSystemOverview" relation="SeeAlso">
          Four ValueType kinds — named semantic meanings.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.GenericsModel" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — generic declarations, inheritance binding, and attribute syntax.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ConstantValueType" relation="SeeAlso">
          Singleton union alternatives via Constant(T){value: …}.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ValueTypeConfiguration" relation="SeeAlso">
          Orthogonal {…} instance configuration on type references.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.Constant" relation="SeeAlso">
          Standard ConstantType implementation.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_INVALID_PROPERTY_TYPE" relation="SeeAlso">
          Non-ValueType used where a ValueType is required.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.ValueType" relation="SeeAlso">
          Top of the assignability hierarchy — ValueType[] accepts any collection of values.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.StructType" relation="SeeAlso">
          Named StructType branch — anonymous { field: Type } is its unnamed type-expression form.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ImplicitNamespaceResolution" relation="SeeAlso">
          Per-field name resolution inside anonymous StructTypes — implicit namespace vs xmlns vs FQN.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.FunctionMappingModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Anonymous StructType field order — call arity and named argument keys; nested collection
          inputs map on the outermost multiplicity.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_CARDINALITY_VIOLATION" relation="SeeAlso">
          Element count outside the multiplicity bounds.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:diagnostic.BML_INVALID_TYPE_REFERENCE" relation="SeeAlso">
          Ill-formed type expressions — ungrouped stacked brackets such as Integer[][].
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:service.Lists" relation="SeeAlso">
          core:ValueType[] capability inputs — worked examples of collection compatibility and of the
          nested (core:ValueType[])[] input on concat.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.InstanceReference" relation="SeeAlso">
          Association member types — InstanceReference(T) or InstanceReference(T)[].
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.TypeIdentityDerivation-->
    <block>
      <name>
        TypeIdentityDerivation
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.TypeIdentityDerivation
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to type references and Block identity
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Every type reference in BML — composition tags, type / baseType on definitions, member type
        attributes — may be written in one of four forms. All normalize to a canonical FQN during
        resolution:
        1. Fully qualified name — com.foo.Bar
        2. QName (xmlns prefix) — foo:Bar with xmlns:foo="com.foo"
        3. Unqualified fallback — Integer at a context-bound site (doc:ImplicitNamespaceResolution)
        4. Sub-Block-relative name — MySubBlock or foo:Bar.MySubBlock
        
        General block references (baseType, aggregation type, composition tags outside specialized
        sites) require an explicit FQN or xmlns prefix — no implicit fallback. Unresolvable →
        BML_UNKNOWN_TYPE. Ambiguous unqualified → BML_AMBIGUOUS_TYPE. Undeclared QName prefix →
        BML_UNKNOWN_NAMESPACE_PREFIX.
        
        Sub-Block type references — Java static nested classes: own FQN prefix, optional own
        genericTypes, no enclosing-instance and no outer type-parameter scope (forward explicitly, e.g.
        Item(T)). Reference unqualified when in scope, as FQN, or as QName. The local part of a QName may
        include a relative Sub-Block path (foo:Bar.MySubBlock).
        
        Unqualified nested type resolution (innermost scope first, then enclosing chain):
        On the current Block, in order: (1) own Block name, (2) own genericTypes, (3) own direct
        Sub-Blocks. Then repeat for the immediate enclosing Block up to the document root. Grandchildren
        are never unqualified — from Grid, Range does not resolve to Grid.Selector.Range; write
        Selector.Range or Grid.Selector.Range. After the nested walk fails, context-bound implicit
        namespace / FQN / xmlns rules apply. Generic / Sub-Block name collisions on the same declaring
        Block remain errors (BML_GENERIC_*_COLLISION).
        
        Root Block identity:
        • type — required (BML_MISSING_BLOCK_TYPE); canonical identity
        • name — optional; when omitted effectiveName = lastSegment(resolve(type)); when present must
          equal lastSegment(resolve(type)) → else BML_BLOCK_NAME_TYPE_MISMATCH
        • definition — optional; default RootBlockDefinition; unqualified via org.blockml.bml.definition.*
        
        Sub-Block identity (opposite convention):
        • name — required (BML_MISSING_SUBBLOCK_NAME)
        • type — normally omitted; expectedType = resolve(parent.type) + "." + name; when present must
          equal expectedType (full parent path check, not last segment alone) →
          BML_SUBBLOCK_TYPE_MISMATCH
        • definition — default SubBlockDefinition
        • genericTypes / subBlocks — allowed; embeddedBlocks — forbidden
          (BML_EMBEDDED_BLOCKS_ON_SUBBLOCK)
        
        Canonical derivation summary:
        • Root: canonicalType = resolve(type); effectiveName = name ?? lastSegment(canonicalType);
          if name present → name == lastSegment(canonicalType)
        • Sub-Block: effectiveName = name; expectedType = canonicalParentType + "." + name;
          canonicalType = type ? resolve(type) : expectedType; canonicalType == expectedType
        
        See also doc:SubBlockModel (visibility, embeds, reference scope) and doc:BlockNameConvention.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:type.TypeReference" relation="Defines">
          ValueType for FQN / TypeExpression text on type and baseType.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.RootBlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Document-root identity — type required; name optional short form.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.SubBlockDefinition" relation="Defines">
          Sub-Block identity — name required; type derived from parent.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:definition.EmbeddedBlockDefinition" relation="SeeAlso">
          Embedded virtual files — FQN from mandatory fully qualified type.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ImplicitNamespaceResolution" relation="SeeAlso">
          Context-bound unqualified fallback at specialized sites.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.SubBlockModel" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — Sub-Block visibility, embeds, and composition reference scope.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.BlockNameConvention" relation="SeeAlso">
          PascalCase rules for &lt;name>.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.TypeSystemOverview-->
    <block>
      <name>
        TypeSystemOverview
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.TypeSystemOverview
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to the type system
      </is>
      <documentation>
        BlockML knows four ValueType kinds at language level — each names a semantic meaning.
        They are sibling branches under ValueType (not subtypes of each other):
        
        PrimitiveType — atomic scalar semantics; no stored values on the type itself.
        Examples: Boolean, Integer, Decimal, Float, Text, Color (Text subtype), URL, Version,
        TypeReference, InstanceReference.
        
        StructType — custom value objects; structure via properties.
        Examples: Size3D, Address, Transform. Parameterized types (Box(T)) are StructTypes
        with genericTypes — not a separate branch.
        
        EnumType — closed set of named members; own ValueType branch (not a StructType). Allowed
        members live in the values aggregation; BOM stores id + typeFqn only.
        Examples: Direction, Face, StudType.
        
        ConstantType — singleton value domain; one configured literal.
        Example: Constant(Text){value: "auto"} for union alternatives.
        
        Type expressions combine named types — they are not new BlockML type classes:
        • Multiplicity: Integer[], Integer[3], Text[0..n] — postfix on a TypeExpression, so it nests via
          explicit grouping: (Integer[])[] (never Integer[][])
        • Unions: Boolean | Integer, Constant(Text){value: "auto"} | Float
        • Generic application: Box(Integer) · InstanceReference(core:Block) — round parentheses in type attributes
        • Instance configuration: Text{maxLength: 100}, Decimal{precision: 10, scale: 2}
        • bml:Self — resolves to effective current Block type after inheritance
        • T — generic parameter reference from declaring Block's genericTypes
        
        Assignability follows the branch hierarchy — a value of a subtype is accepted wherever a
        supertype is required, with ValueType as the top of the value hierarchy:
        Integer &lt;: Number &lt;: PrimitiveType &lt;: ValueType. A type reference with multiplicity is
        compatible per element type: Integer[] &lt;: Number[] &lt;: ValueType[], and each element of a
        collection is validated independently, so ValueType[] also accepts mixed element types
        (doc:TypeExpressions — Collection compatibility · Heterogeneous collections).
        
        Only ValueTypes may appear where a bare type name is required (Property type,
        Capability inputType/outputType). Aggregation type may be any Block FQN. Association type
        must be InstanceReference(T) or InstanceReference(T)[] — bare Block FQNs are invalid.
        ValueTypes never store data — Properties own all concrete values.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeExpressions" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Multiplicity, unions, obsolete wrappers — type expressions.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.GenericsModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
          Generic declarations, inheritance binding, defaultType.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.ValueType" relation="Extends">
          Abstract base for all value blocks used in Property and Capability types.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.PrimitiveType" relation="SeeAlso">
          Branch base for atomic scalar types.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.StructType" relation="SeeAlso">
          Branch base for custom value objects.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.EnumType" relation="SeeAlso">
          Branch base for finite value sets — sibling of StructType under ValueType.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.ConstantType" relation="SeeAlso">
          Branch base for singleton value domains.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.Constant" relation="SeeAlso">
          Generic Constant(T){value: …} for union singleton alternatives.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.InstanceReference" relation="SeeAlso">
          Association member types — instance pointers, not owned Block instances.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.Color" relation="SeeAlso">
          Textual primitive subtype — not a StructType.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.Decimal" relation="SeeAlso">
          Default primitive for non-integer numeric values.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeExpressions" relation="ReadNext">
          Next reading step — multiplicity, unions, and type-expression forms.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ContractsModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          contracts merge and satisfiesTypeBound — baseType vs implements.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.ValueTypeConfiguration-->
    <block>
      <name>
        ValueTypeConfiguration
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.ValueTypeConfiguration
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to ValueType configuration
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Curly braces configure the Property's owned ValueType instance — orthogonal to generic (...)
        binding. The configuration applies to the type instance associated with a Property, not to
        the ValueType definition block itself. Both may coexist: Box(Integer){minItems: 1}.
        Multiplicity brackets apply after configuration: Text{maxLength: 50}[0..n].
        
        Syntax examples:
        • Text{maxLength: 100} — named configuration property (JSON-like key: value)
        • Decimal{precision: 10, scale: 2} — named precision and scale
        • Decimal{10, 2} — positional by ValueType property declaration order (precision, scale)
        • Constant(SizeXY){value: {x: 1, y: 2}} — nested object literals for StructType config values
        
        Rules:
        • Named and positional configuration must not be mixed in one {...} block →
          BML_MIXED_TYPE_CONFIGURATION
        • Positional mapping follows declaration order — same principle as FunctionMapping
        • Do not use key=value inside {...} (that is expression call syntax, not type configuration)
        • Unknown config property → BML_UNKNOWN_TYPE_CONFIGURATION; value mismatch →
          BML_TYPE_CONFIGURATION_VIOLATION
        • Generic application uses round parentheses: Box(Integer), TypeReference(core:Block)
        • Constant(T){value: …} — generic (...) binds T; {value: …} supplies the singleton literal
        
        Prefer multiplicity on the type reference for collection bounds (Text[1..10]) over List{…}
        wrappers. Prefer inline configuration for type-intrinsic thresholds (Text{maxLength: 100});
        keep Constraints for contextual/composable/business rules (Pattern, cross-property, …).
        
        ValueType definitions declare configuration Properties on the type block (e.g. precision and
        scale on Decimal). Values live on Properties of Block instances — never on the ValueType
        definition. There is no new Integer(5) in BlockML; the Property owns value 5 with type Integer.
        
        Decimal is the default for non-integer numerics (3.5, 12.75). Float is special-purpose only
        when binary floating-point semantics are explicitly required.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.GenericsModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Orthogonal (...) generic binding.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.TypeExpressions" relation="SeeAlso">
          Multiplicity brackets and type-expression composition.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.ConstantValueType" relation="ReadNext">
          Next — Constant(T){value: …} singleton domains.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.FunctionMappingModel" relation="SeeAlso">
          Shared declaration-order rule for positional mapping.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:mapping.FunctionMapping" relation="SeeAlso">
          Function call argument mapping.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.Decimal" relation="SeeAlso">
          Primitive with precision and scale configuration Properties.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:type.Constant" relation="SeeAlso">
          Constant(T){value: …} — configured singleton for union alternatives.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.ValueType" relation="Extends">
          ValueType branch — types describe nature; Properties own values.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.WOTFoundation-->
    <block>
      <name>
        WOTFoundation
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:documentation.WOTFoundation
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Documentation
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A guide to the WOT foundation
      </is>
      <documentation>
        The Web of Things (WOT) provides the philosophical foundation for BlockML. Optional reading
        for context — not required to use the compiler or author BML.
        
        Idea (Plato):
        The only true constant. Exists independently of Universes, Worlds, Technologies, and
        Manifestations. An Idea does not change. In BlockML: Block, ValueType, Language, FQN —
        describable without any render target. BML holds the Idea stable. The Block definition
        itself is the Idea; the authoring field &lt;is> (BOM block.is, reflection bml:Self.is)
        names that Idea in an ultra-short noun phrase for humans and LLMs — it is not the Idea.
        
        Manifestation:
        The entire process from Idea to Thing — not a single step. Manifestor (compiler) produces
        preconditions; Runtime completes Manifestation in a World the Manifestor cannot fully know.
        In BlockML: renderer working model, target output, runtime behaviour. The renderer decides
        interpretation — SCAD mb_block(), React components, KiCad symbols are different Manifestations
        of the same Ideas.
        
        Grammar (BlockML / BOM):
        How Things are structured. Blocks are the grammar of a World. Types, Languages, Services,
        and Capabilities are part of that grammar — not implementation detail. stud:// is how Things
        communicate; BOM Core is how Things are structured.
        
        World:
        Delivers interpretation. An Idea carries no properties of its own — no substance, material,
        or weight. The World assigns meaning. MachineBlocks is one World (physical geometry → SCAD);
        other Worlds (ScreenMachine → React, EBOM → electronics) share type FQNs with different
        render mappings.
        
        WOT alignment at language layer: ontological unity in BML; engineering freedom in compiler
        internals and renderer output. Without adoption, there is no WOT — and without internal
        freedom, there is no maintainable compiler.
      </documentation>
      <references>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.EverythingIsABlock" relation="SeeAlso">
          WOT alignment and Idea vs Manifestation.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:core.Block" relation="SeeAlso">
          Universal semantic unit — grammar element of a World.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.RendererFreedom" relation="SeeAlso">
          Renderer freedom — Manifestation is target-defined, not prescribed by BlockML.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.EverythingIsABlock" relation="SeeAlso">
          Everything is a Block — grammar includes types, languages, and documentation.
        </see>
        <see type="blockml:documentation.CoreUseCases" relation="SeeAlso">
          Applied Idea vs Manifestation — documentation, domain modeling, and AI workspaces.
        </see>
      </references>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Group — block group branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.group.BlockGroup-->
    <block>
      <name>
        BlockGroup
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:group.BlockGroup
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Group
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A Block grouping category
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract Group branch for block groups — named categories that organize Blocks
            for editors and tooling. Intentionally empty — no properties, aggregations, or Sub-Blocks.
            Concrete group kinds extend this branch. BlockDefinition.groups overrides
            StructuralDefinition.groups to TypeReferenceEntity(group:BlockGroup)[].
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Group — member group branch

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.group.MemberGroup-->
    <block>
      <name>
        MemberGroup
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:group.MemberGroup
      </type>
      <baseType>
        blockml:core.Group
      </baseType>
      <is>
        A member grouping category
      </is>
      <documentation>
        Abstract Group branch for member groups — named categories that organize Block
            members (properties, aggregations, associations, capabilities) for editors and tooling.
            A member may reference several groups via MemberDefinition.groups (e.g. UI category and usage
            facet). Intentionally empty — no properties, aggregations, or Sub-Blocks. Concrete group kinds
            extend this branch. MemberDefinition.groups overrides StructuralDefinition.groups to
            TypeReferenceEntity(group:MemberGroup)[].
      </documentation>
      <abstract>
        true
      </abstract>
    </block>
    <!--BlockML Core — BML implementation language

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.language.BML-->
    <block>
      <name>
        BML
      </name>
      <type>
        blockml:language.BML
      </type>
      <baseType>
